OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026", "title": "Quantitative Near-Kerr Evolution and Generic C2 Future Inextendibility", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "264", "family_title": "Strong cosmic censorship near two-ended Kerr data", "abstract_html": "For every fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge, we prove that smooth vacuum data admitting a <i>C</i><sup>2</sup> future extension of their full maximal globally hyperbolic development form a meagre set in a neighborhood defined by one finite-order seminorm. The topology allows arbitrary symbol-bounded asymptotically flat tails and imposes no symmetry.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Local \\(C^{2}\\) strong cosmic censorship — original Theorem 1.2.** Fix \\(M>0\\) and \\(0<a<M\\).", "statement": "<p>**Local \\(C^{2}\\) strong cosmic censorship — original Theorem 1.2.**</p><p>Fix \\(M&gt;0\\) and \\(0&lt;a&lt;M\\). Let\n\\[\n(\\Sigma,h_*,K_*),\\qquad \\Sigma\\simeq\\mathbb R\\times S^2,\n\\]\nbe the complete two-ended Kerr bridge datum through the bifurcation sphere, with a specified future orientation. Let \\(\\nabla_*\\) and \\(|\\cdot|_*\\) be the connection and tensor norm of \\(h_*\\), and let \\(\\rho\\geq1\\) be smooth and equal to the Kerr radial coordinate outside a compact set on each end.</p><p>For smooth symmetric covariant two-tensors \\(u,v\\), define\n\\[\np_m(u,v)=\\sum_{j=0}^{m}\n\\left(\n\\sup_\\Sigma \\rho^{1+j}|\\nabla_*^j u|_*\n+\n\\sup_\\Sigma \\rho^{2+j}|\\nabla_*^j v|_*\n\\right).\n\\]\nLet \\(E\\) consist of the pairs for which every \\(p_m\\) is finite. Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\\subset (h_*,K_*)+E\n\\]\nconsist of the positive definite complete smooth vacuum data satisfying\n\\[\nR(h)+(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)^2-|K|_h^2=0,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{div}_hK-d(\\operatorname{tr}_hK)=0,\n\\]\nwith the relative topology of all the seminorms \\(p_m\\). No leading coefficient in either asymptotic tail is fixed.</p><p>For \\(d\\in\\mathcal D\\), let \\((M_d,g_d)\\) be its full maximal globally hyperbolic vacuum development, with the specified Cauchy embedding of \\(\\Sigma\\).</p><p>A \\(C^2\\) future extension means a connected time-oriented smooth four-manifold \\(\\widetilde M\\), a nondegenerate \\(C^2\\) Lorentzian metric \\(\\widetilde g\\), and a smooth time-orientation-preserving isometric embedding\n\\[\n\\iota:M_d\\longrightarrow\\widetilde M\n\\]\nonto a proper open subset, such that some future-directed timelike \\(C^1\\) curve \\(c:[0,1]\\to\\widetilde M\\) satisfies\n\\[\nc([0,1))\\subset\\iota(M_d),\n\\qquad\nc(1)\\in\\partial\\iota(M_d).\n\\]\nThe extension is not required to be vacuum or globally hyperbolic outside \\(\\iota(M_d)\\).</p><p>There exists \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) such that the data in\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\n=\n\\left\\{\nd\\in\\mathcal D:\np_{10}\\bigl(d-(h_*,K_*)\\bigr)&lt;\\epsilon\n\\right\\}\n\\]\nwhose full maximal globally hyperbolic vacuum developments admit a \\(C^2\\) future extension form a meagre subset of \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\).</p><p>Equivalently, the future-inextendible data contain a countable intersection of relatively open dense subsets of \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\). Constants and \\(\\epsilon\\) may depend on the fixed Kerr center. No uniformity as \\(a\\to0\\) or \\(a\\to M\\), and no smallness of seminorms beyond \\(p_{10}\\), is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>It suffices to prove the theorem for \\(M=1\\). Under \\(g\\mapsto M^{-2}g\\), induced covariant data become\n\\[\n(h,K)\\mapsto(M^{-2}h,M^{-1}K).\n\\]\nDimensionless Kerr coordinates identify the constraint spaces homeomorphically; weighted seminorms change by fixed-order comparable factors. Completeness, vacuum maximality, and the extension property are preserved. An original \\(p_{10}\\)-ball can be chosen inside the inverse rescaled neighborhood.</p><p>Fix the single base neighborhood supplied by Propositions 7–9. Its choices precede the seed family, curvature bound, approximation order, and late experiment.</p><p>Take \\(O\\in\\mathscr O\\), \\(N\\geq1\\), \\(d\\in B(O,N)\\), \\(m\\geq10\\), and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). Proposition 10 supplies a seed \\(w\\in O\\) with noncorner first runout from \\(P_d\\). Since \\(d\\in A(O,N)\\), that seed has bounded parallel curvature even if it no longer has a regular exit in the chosen extension.</p><p>By Lemma 1.3, in a neighborhood of \\((d,w)\\) there is a common finite \\(C_1\\) such that\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Riem}(U',X',U',X')|\n\\leq C_1Q_{d'}(w',\\tau)\n\\]\nfor future unit geodesic tangents and orthogonal unit spacelike vectors. Choose \\(J&gt;C_1N\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition 16 with tolerance smaller than\n\\[\n\\min\\left\\{\n\\eta,\\\n\\epsilon-p_{10}\\bigl(d-(h_*,K_*)\\bigr)\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large finite \\(T\\),\n\\[\nw_T\\in O,\\qquad d_T\\in\\mathcal U_\\epsilon,\\qquad\np_m(d_T-d)&lt;\\eta,\n\\]\nand at the finite observation point in its full maximal development,\n\\[\nQ_{d_T}(w_T,\\tau_T)\n\\geq C_1^{-1}\n|\\operatorname{Riem}(g_{d_T})(U_T,X_T,U_T,X_T)|\n&gt;N.\n\\]\nThis is precisely the finite-observation criterion of Proposition 1. Every relative closure \\(\\overline{B(O,N)}\\) is nowhere dense, and their countable union covers all future-extendible data. Hence the extendible set is meagre, and the intersection of their relatively open dense complements consists of future-inextendible data.</p><p>The same \\(\\epsilon\\) serves every test: physical profile range and depth, computing depths, horizon and ray margins, gauges, probes, compact/far absorption scales, overlap regions, and base continuation thresholds were fixed first. All their initial requirements follow from one sufficiently small \\(p_{10}\\) bound. Higher constants and later-time thresholds may depend on the smooth datum and fixed derivative order but impose no higher-order smallness. The seed family, \\(N,m\\), branch location, frequency, formal accuracy, and late time enter only the finite experiment.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Closed curvature tests and the finite-observation criterion — original Proposition 2.6.** Use the data space and developments of Theorem 1.", "statement": "<p>**Closed curvature tests and the finite-observation criterion — original Proposition 2.6.**</p><p>Use the data space and developments of Theorem 1. For a seed \\(w\\in T\\Sigma\\), including its base point, define\n\\[\nU_d(w)=\\sqrt{1+h(w,w)}\\,n+w,\n\\]\nwhere \\(n\\) is the future unit normal to \\(\\Sigma\\). Let\n\\[\n\\gamma_{d,w}:[0,T_d(w))\\to M_d\n\\]\nbe the maximal future unit timelike geodesic with that initial velocity.</p><p>Parallel transport the positive inner product \\(1\\oplus h\\) on\n\\(\\mathbb Rn\\oplus T\\Sigma\\) along \\(\\gamma_{d,w}\\), obtaining \\(k_{d,w}\\), and set\n\\[\nQ_d(w,\\tau)=|\\operatorname{Riem}(g_d)|_{k_{d,w}}(\\tau).\n\\]\nChoose a countable relatively compact open basis \\(\\mathscr O\\) of \\(T\\Sigma\\). For \\(O\\in\\mathscr O\\) and \\(N\\in\\mathbb N\\), \\(N\\geq1\\), set\n\\[\nA(O,N)=\n\\left\\{\nd\\in\\mathcal D:\n\\begin{array}{l}\nT_d(w)\\leq N\\quad\\text{for every }w\\in O,\\\\\nQ_d(w,\\tau)\\leq N\\quad\n\\text{for every }w\\in O,\\ 0\\leq\\tau&lt;T_d(w)\n\\end{array}\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal B_\\epsilon\\) be the extendible data in \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\). Let \\(B(O,N)\\) consist of the data in\n\\(\\mathcal B_\\epsilon\\cap A(O,N)\\) for which at least one seed in \\(O\\) has a regular exit in some extension; “regular” means that its position and unit tangent continue through the endpoint on an ambient geodesic arc.</p><p>Suppose one \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) has the following property: for every \\(O,N\\), every \\(d\\in B(O,N)\\), every integer \\(m\\geq10\\), and every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), there are\n\\[\nd'\\in\\mathcal U_\\epsilon,\\qquad\nw'\\in O,\\qquad\n0\\leq\\tau&lt;T_{d'}(w')\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\np_m(d'-d)&lt;\\eta,\n\\qquad\nQ_{d'}(w',\\tau)&gt;N.\n\\]\nThen the conclusion of Theorem 1 holds.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\overline{B(O,N)}^{\\,\\mathcal U_\\epsilon}\\) had nonempty relative interior, that interior would meet \\(B(O,N)\\) and, by Lemma 1.5, would be contained in \\(A(O,N)\\). At an intersection point choose a finite-seminorm ball inside the interior, increasing its order to at least ten by Lemma 1.1. The assumed finite observation lies in this ball but fails \\(A(O,N)\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Thus every closure is nowhere dense. The countable intersection of their relatively open dense complements contains no extendible datum by Lemma 1.5.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The data topology — original Lemma 2.1.** The space \\(\\mathcal D\\) is completely metrizable, \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\) is Baire, and every neighborhood of a datum contains a relative ball \\( p_m(d'-d)<\\eta \\) for some …", "statement": "<p>**The data topology — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><p>The space \\(\\mathcal D\\) is completely metrizable, \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\) is Baire, and every neighborhood of a datum contains a relative ball\n\\[\np_m(d'-d)&lt;\\eta\n\\]\nfor some finite \\(m\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A sequence Cauchy in every \\(p_m\\) has compatible smooth limits on compact coordinate sets. Passing to the pointwise limit in its weighted uniform Cauchy bounds proves convergence in every \\(p_m\\). Thus \\(E\\) is Fréchet; a complete metric is\n\\[\n\\sum_{m\\geq0}2^{-m-1}\\min\\{1,p_m(d-d')\\}.\n\\]</p><p>Every positive metric in the affine space is uniformly comparable to \\(h_*\\), by decay on the ends and compactness of their complement, and hence is complete. Positivity is open in \\(p_0\\). The constraints are closed under local smooth convergence within this positive open set. An open subset of a completely metrizable space, and a relatively closed subset thereof, are completely metrizable. Consequently \\(\\mathcal D\\), and its open subset \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\), are completely metrizable and Baire.</p><p>The seminorms increase with \\(m\\), so a sufficiently small ball in the largest of finitely many orders lies in any basic neighborhood.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Closed tests — original Lemma 2.2.** Every \\(A(O,N)\\) is closed in \\(\\mathcal D\\).", "statement": "<p>**Closed tests — original Lemma 2.2.**</p><p>Every \\(A(O,N)\\) is closed in \\(\\mathcal D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every strict failure has a compact witness. If \\(T_d(w)&gt;N\\), use a geodesic segment extending beyond \\(N\\). If the curvature bound fails, use a segment through a point \\(\\tau&lt;T_d(w)\\) where \\(Q_d(w,\\tau)&gt;N\\).</p><p>Smooth local Cauchy stability and geometric uniqueness place a neighborhood of the fixed segment in a common gauge for sufficiently close data. Its causal shadow on \\(\\Sigma\\) is compact; otherwise a limit of past causal curves to escaping initial points would contradict Cauchyness. Finite local Cauchy constructions therefore suffice. Geodesic and parallel-transport ODE stability preserve the strict witness. This proves openness of the complement without asserting continuity of maximal duration.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tidal contractions — original Lemma 2.3.** Along \\(\\gamma_{d,w}\\), let \\(U=\\dot\\gamma_{d,w}\\), and let \\(X\\) satisfy \\( g_d(X,X)=1,\\qquad g_d(U,X)=0. \\) Then …", "statement": "<p>**Tidal contractions — original Lemma 2.3.**</p><p>Along \\(\\gamma_{d,w}\\), let \\(U=\\dot\\gamma_{d,w}\\), and let \\(X\\) satisfy\n\\[\ng_d(X,X)=1,\\qquad g_d(U,X)=0.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Riem}(U,X,U,X)|\n\\leq\n\\bigl(1+2h(w,w)\\bigr)^2Q_d(w,\\tau).\n\\]\nThe factor is uniformly bounded for data and seeds near a fixed pair.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the transported Lorentz orthonormal frame write\n\\[\nU=\\alpha e_0+v,\\qquad X=\\beta e_0+z.\n\\]\nThe unit and orthogonality conditions give\n\\[\n\\alpha^2=1+|v|^2,\\qquad\n|z|^2=1+\\beta^2,\\qquad\n\\alpha\\beta=v\\cdot z,\n\\]\nhence \\(\\beta^2\\leq|v|^2\\). Therefore\n\\[\n|X|_k^2\\leq|U|_k^2=1+2h(w,w),\n\\]\nthe last identity being constant under parallel transport. Tensor Cauchy–Schwarz proves the estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Fixed-endpoint homotopy — original Lemma 2.4.** Let \\(P\\) be a globally hyperbolic open subspacetime of a Hausdorff Lorentzian manifold.", "statement": "<p>**Fixed-endpoint homotopy — original Lemma 2.4.**</p><p>Let \\(P\\) be a globally hyperbolic open subspacetime of a Hausdorff Lorentzian manifold. In a continuous family of future causal paths with fixed endpoints \\(p,q\\in P\\), if the first path lies in \\(P\\), then every path lies in \\(P\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Parametrize the family by a connected compact interval. The parameters for which the whole path lies in \\(P\\) form an open set. Every such path lies in the intrinsic compact diamond\n\\[\nJ_P^+(p)\\cap J_P^-(q),\n\\]\nwhich is closed in the ambient Hausdorff manifold. Continuity makes the parameter set closed as well. It is therefore the whole interval.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Regular-exit covering — original Proposition 2.5.** If \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\) admits a \\(C^2\\) future extension, then there are \\(O\\in\\mathscr O\\) and \\(N\\geq1\\) such that \\(d\\in A(O,N)\\) and every seed in \\(O\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Regular-exit covering — original Proposition 2.5.**</p><p>If \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\) admits a \\(C^2\\) future extension, then there are \\(O\\in\\mathscr O\\) and \\(N\\geq1\\) such that \\(d\\in A(O,N)\\) and every seed in \\(O\\) reaches its finite maximal-development endpoint on a regular ambient geodesic arc.</p><p>Consequently,\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_\\epsilon=\\bigcup_{O\\in\\mathscr O,\\ N\\geq1}B(O,N),\n\\qquad\n\\overline{B(O,N)}^{\\,\\mathcal U_\\epsilon}\n\\subset A(O,N)\\cap\\mathcal U_\\epsilon.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Identify the development with its image in an extension and write \\(z=c(1)\\). The curve has no interior future endpoint, by the Hausdorff property. Extend it maximally to the past inside the development. Cauchyness puts a sufficiently late point \\(p=c(s_0)\\) to the intrinsic future of \\(\\Sigma\\).</p><p>In a small temporal chart at \\(z\\), the local timelike relation from \\(p\\) to \\(z\\) gives a short timelike geodesic continuing past \\(z\\). No continuing tangent of \\(c\\) is needed. Since the Christoffel symbols of a \\(C^2\\) metric are \\(C^1\\), geodesic flow has local uniqueness and continuous dependence.</p><p>Consider close geodesic arcs on one temporal slice with close unit directions, parametrized by chart time on a common interval \\([t_0,t_1]\\), with the reference arc passing through \\(z\\) at \\(t_z\\). Every sufficiently close arc exits the image. Otherwise, for a bump \\(\\beta\\) supported in \\((t_0,t_1)\\), \\(\\beta(t_z)=1\\), the paths\n\\[\n\\bigl(t,x_\\gamma(t)+\\lambda\\beta(t)(x(z)-x_\\gamma(t_z))\\bigr),\n\\qquad 0\\leq\\lambda\\leq1,\n\\]\ngive a fixed-endpoint timelike homotopy. After shrinking the family, the perturbation is small in \\(C^1\\) and remains uniformly timelike. Lemma 1.4 would keep the homotopy inside although its last member passes through \\(z\\). The first exit is the maximal intrinsic geodesic endpoint by uniqueness, regardless of later reentry.</p><p>The reference geodesic continued backwards meets \\(\\Sigma\\). A maximal timelike geodesic cannot end at an interior position limit: in a precompact temporal chart, causal slopes \\(V=(1,dx/dt)\\) are bounded and\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\log\\frac{dt}{d\\tau}\n=-\\Gamma^0_{\\alpha\\beta}V^\\alpha V^\\beta.\n\\]\nOver a finite chart-time tail this bounds the unit tangent and gives its limit, permitting continuation. Cauchyness supplies the intersection with \\(\\Sigma\\).</p><p>Flow dependence through the earlier compact segment yields an open seed family. Shrink it to be relatively compact. The earlier tube has uniformly bounded duration, parallel transport, and curvature. In the extension chart, tangents remain in a compact phase-space set, parallel transport has bounded coefficients on a bounded time interval, and curvature is continuous and bounded. Choose a basis element \\(O\\) and an integer \\(N\\) exceeding both duration and curvature bounds. The covering follows; its closure inclusion follows from Lemma 1.2.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Kerr trapping and escape — original Proposition 3.6.** Fix \\(M>0\\), \\(0<a<M\\), and write \\( \\Sigma_K=r^2+a^2\\cos^2\\theta,\\qquad \\Delta=(r-r_+)(r-r_-),\\qquad r_\\pm=M\\pm\\sqrt{M^2-a^2}. \\) Choose a horizon-crossing …", "statement": "<p>**Kerr trapping and escape — original Proposition 3.6.**</p><p>Fix \\(M&gt;0\\), \\(0&lt;a&lt;M\\), and write\n\\[\n\\Sigma_K=r^2+a^2\\cos^2\\theta,\\qquad\n\\Delta=(r-r_+)(r-r_-),\\qquad\nr_\\pm=M\\pm\\sqrt{M^2-a^2}.\n\\]\nChoose a horizon-crossing stationary spacelike slicing as below.</p><p>The energy-normalized photon trap is symplectically transverse of codimension two, with smooth stable and unstable surfaces and normal expansion and contraction rates bounded away from zero. Every fixed-order tangent derivative grows subexponentially, so the normal rates dominate tangent dynamics at every fixed order. These properties persist on a sufficiently small interval of nearby levels of the separated fixed-frequency symbol.</p><p>Departing radial tails escape the trapping neighborhood without homoclinic return. Together with the outer-horizon radial source and strict inner outflow, the radial alternatives exhaust non-escaping null behavior in the computing cylinder.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.4 gives invariant stable and unstable surfaces with nonzero bracket and positive normal rates. Lemma 2.5 supplies polynomial tangent bounds, proving domination at every fixed order.</p><p>For nearby levels, with \\(X=H_{q_E}/2\\),\n\\[\nz_1^2-z_2^2=q_E/E^2+U(r_c)-D/E^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nXz_2=(\\partial_rz_2)E\\sqrt\\Delta\\,z_1,\\qquad\nXz_1=(\\partial_rz_2)E\\sqrt\\Delta\\,z_2.\n\\]\nThe regular-time speed has \\(E/Xt&gt;0\\) near trapping, so the same invariant surfaces and positive rates persist.</p><p>Lemma 2.2 excludes homoclinic returns and other radial recurrence. Between the horizons \\(dr\\) is timelike. Lemma 2.3 identifies the sole nontransverse horizon behavior, and Lemma 2.1 supplies the strict inner exit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Bent spacelike time — original Lemma 3.1.** For any fixed interval \\([r_b,R_1]\\), where \\(r_-<r_b<r_+\\) and \\(R_1>r_+\\), one can choose \\( t=T+r_*-G(r),\\qquad r_*'=\\frac{r^2+a^2}{\\Delta}, \\) such that \\(G'=1\\) on the …", "statement": "<p>**Bent spacelike time — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>For any fixed interval \\([r_b,R_1]\\), where \\(r_-&lt;r_b&lt;r_+\\) and \\(R_1&gt;r_+\\), one can choose\n\\[\nt=T+r_*-G(r),\\qquad r_*'=\\frac{r^2+a^2}{\\Delta},\n\\]\nsuch that \\(G'=1\\) on the interval,\n\\[\n1\\leq G'\\leq r_*'\n\\]\nin a subsequent exterior interpolation, and \\(G=r_*\\) near infinity.</p><p>The time \\(t\\) is regular at the future event horizon, its level sets are spacelike, and \\(r=r_b\\) is strict future outflow. The delay \\(G-r_*\\) at every fixed depth above \\(r_-\\) can be made arbitrarily large by moving the exterior bend outward.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the ingoing azimuth\n\\[\nd\\widetilde\\phi=d\\phi+\\frac{a}{\\Delta}\\,dr\n\\]\nnear the horizon and return smoothly to the Boyer–Lindquist azimuth toward infinity. Direct substitution gives\n\\[\n\\Sigma_K b^{-1}(dt,dt)\n=-2(r^2+a^2)G'+\\Delta(G')^2+a^2\\sin^2\\theta.\n\\]\nFor \\(G'=1\\), this is \\(-\\Sigma_K-2Mr&lt;0\\). In the exterior it is convex in \\(G'\\), and at \\(G'=r_*'\\) it equals\n\\[\n-\\frac{(r^2+a^2)^2}{\\Delta}+a^2\\sin^2\\theta&lt;0.\n\\]\nEvery indicated interpolation is therefore spacelike. Between the horizons,\n\\[\nb^{-1}(dr,dr)=\\Delta/\\Sigma_K&lt;0,\n\\]\nand future causal curves have decreasing \\(r\\), proving outflow.</p><p>Normalize \\(G=r_*\\) after the bend. Extending the \\(G'=1\\) interval increases the fixed-depth delay by the integral of\n\\[\nr_*'-1=\\frac{2Mr}{\\Delta}=\\frac{2M}{r}+O(r^{-2}),\n\\]\nwhich diverges positively. Near infinity \\(t=T\\), and\n\\[\nb-\\eta=O(r^{-1}),\\quad b^{ti}=O(r^{-2}),\\quad\n\\Gamma(b)=O(r^{-2}),\\quad\nr_*=r+2M\\log r+O(1),\n\\]\nwith differentiated symbol bounds. The order-\\(r^{-1}\\) terms are rotation invariant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Radial alternatives — original Lemma 3.2.** For a future nonzero null covector \\(k\\), set \\( E=-k(\\partial_t),\\qquad L_z=k(\\partial_\\phi), \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Radial alternatives — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><p>For a future nonzero null covector \\(k\\), set\n\\[\nE=-k(\\partial_t),\\qquad L_z=k(\\partial_\\phi),\n\\]\n\\[\nD=k_\\theta^2+\n\\left(\\frac{L_z}{\\sin\\theta}-aE\\sin\\theta\\right)^2,\n\\qquad\nP=(r^2+a^2)E-aL_z.\n\\]\nIn the exterior \\(P&gt;0\\). If \\(E\\leq0\\), the allowed radial set has at most one simple turning point and no photon trapping. If \\(E&gt;0\\), trapped rays are spherical, and their radii satisfy\n\\[\nF=\\frac{2r\\Delta}{r-M},\\qquad\n\\frac D{E^2}=\\frac{4r^2\\Delta}{(r-M)^2},\n\\qquad\nF=\\frac PE=r^2+a^2-a\\frac{L_z}{E}.\n\\]\nThe normalized trapped set is compact, separated from the horizon and infinity. The relevant radial critical point is a unique nondegenerate minimum. A simple turn reflects toward the same end of its allowed interval, and no homoclinic orbit returns to a double radial root.</p>", "proof": "<p>The vector\n\\[\nX=(r^2+a^2)\\partial_T+a\\partial_\\phi\n\\]\nis future timelike, since \\(b(X,X)=-\\Sigma_K\\Delta\\). Thus \\(k(X)=-P&lt;0\\).</p><p>Separation gives\n\\[\nq_E=\\Sigma_Kb^{-1}(k,k)\n=\\Delta k_r^2+D-\\frac{P^2}{\\Delta},\n\\]\n\\[\n(\\Sigma_K\\dot r)^2=P^2-\\Delta D,\\qquad\n\\Sigma_K\\dot T=\\frac{(r^2+a^2)P}{\\Delta}\n+a(L_z-aE\\sin^2\\theta).\n\\]\nThe quantities \\(E,L_z,D\\) are conserved.</p><p>If \\(E\\leq0\\), then \\(P'=2rE\\leq0\\), whereas \\(\\sqrt{\\Delta D}\\) is strictly increasing when \\(D&gt;0\\). The case \\(D=0\\) would imply \\(P=E\\Sigma_K\\leq0\\), impossible. There is therefore at most one simple boundary point of the allowed positive-\\(P\\) branch.</p><p>For \\(E&gt;0\\), put\n\\[\nN=2r\\Delta-(r-M)F.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n(F/\\sqrt\\Delta)'=N/\\Delta^{3/2},\n\\quad\nN'=2\\Delta+2r(r-M)-F,\n\\quad\nN''=6(r-M)&gt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(F(r_+)&gt;0\\), \\(N(r_+)&lt;0\\), and strict convexity gives one zero with positive derivative: \\(F/\\sqrt\\Delta\\) has one nondegenerate minimum. If \\(F(r_+)\\leq0\\), at the first zero \\(r_0\\) of \\(F\\) both \\(N(r_0)\\geq0\\) and \\(N'(r_0)&gt;0\\), so no radial critical point occurs.</p><p>At a double root, \\(N=0\\) and \\(F^2=\\Delta D/E^2\\), giving the displayed identities. The inequality\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{L_z}{E}\\right|-a\\leq\\frac{\\sqrt D}{E}\n\\]\nexcludes approach to \\(r_+\\); the double-root identities exclude approach to infinity because \\(|L_z/E|\\) would grow quadratically while \\(\\sqrt D/E\\) grows only linearly. Angular smoothness at the poles gives compactness in the intrinsic cotangent bundle.</p><p>The one-minimum description classifies the allowed radial intervals. Away from a double root \\(F^2/\\Delta\\) increases strictly in either radial direction, excluding return.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Outer-horizon radial source — original Lemma 3.3.** Let \\(H\\) be the future horizon Killing generator, normalized by Killing parameter, so that \\( \\nabla_HH=\\kappa_+H,\\qquad Ht>0. \\) There is a positive homogeneous …", "statement": "<p>**Outer-horizon radial source — original Lemma 3.3.**</p><p>Let \\(H\\) be the future horizon Killing generator, normalized by Killing parameter, so that\n\\[\n\\nabla_HH=\\kappa_+H,\\qquad Ht&gt;0.\n\\]\nThere is a positive homogeneous degree-one scale \\(b_1\\), equal to \\(|\\varrho|\\) on \\(k=\\varrho H^\\flat\\), such that the increasing-\\(t\\) ray field \\(V\\) satisfies\n\\[\nV\\log b_1=-\\frac{\\kappa_+}{Ht}\n\\]\non the generators. Near them,\n\\[\nf_H=\\frac{E^2+D}{b_1^2}\n\\]\nvanishes exactly on the generators and satisfies \\(Vf_H\\geq c_Hf_H\\) for some \\(c_H&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Along an affine generator, \\(k^\\sharp=\\varrho H\\) and the affine geodesic equation gives\n\\[\nH\\log|\\varrho|=-\\kappa_+.\n\\]\nDivide by \\(Ht\\), extend the scale smoothly and homogeneously, and use conservation of \\(E,D\\):\n\\[\nVf_H=-2(V\\log b_1)f_H.\n\\]\nThe coefficient remains strictly positive nearby. On the null set, \\(E=D=0\\) forces \\(L_z=k_\\theta=0\\); near the horizon the remaining direction is its generator covector.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Transverse defining functions — original Lemma 3.4.** Near the energy-normalized trapped set on either temporal sheet, \\(r_c=r_c(L_z/E)\\) is smooth.", "statement": "<p>**Transverse defining functions — original Lemma 3.4.**</p><p>Near the energy-normalized trapped set on either temporal sheet, \\(r_c=r_c(L_z/E)\\) is smooth. Define\n\\[\nz_1=\\frac{\\Sigma_K\\dot r}{E\\sqrt\\Delta},\n\\qquad\nz_2=\\operatorname{sgn}(r-r_c)\n\\sqrt{\\frac{F(r)^2}{\\Delta(r)}-\\frac{F(r_c)^2}{\\Delta(r_c)}}.\n\\]\nThese functions are smooth and homogeneous of degree zero, vanish jointly exactly on trapping, and satisfy\n\\[\nVz_2=\\lambda z_1,\\qquad Vz_1=\\lambda z_2,\\qquad\\lambda&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\phi_\\pm=z_1\\pm z_2\\) satisfy \\(V\\phi_\\pm=\\pm\\lambda\\phi_\\pm\\). Their reduced spatial symplectic bracket is nonzero on each trapped halfwave, and \\(\\lambda\\) has a uniform positive lower bound on the normalized trap.</p>", "proof": "<p>The strict minimum and the implicit function theorem give \\(r_c\\). With \\(U=F^2/\\Delta\\),\n\\[\nU(r)-U(r_c)=(r-r_c)^2a_0,\\qquad a_0&gt;0,\n\\]\nso \\(z_2=(r-r_c)\\sqrt{a_0}\\) is smooth and \\(\\partial_rz_2&gt;0\\). Null separation gives\n\\[\nz_1^2-z_2^2=U(r_c)-D/E^2.\n\\]\nConservation and differentiation yield the two equations, where\n\\[\n\\lambda=(\\partial_rz_2)\\frac{E\\sqrt\\Delta}{\\Sigma_K\\dot t}.\n\\]\nThe ratio \\(E/\\dot t\\) is positive on both sheets with increasing-time orientation.</p><p>At fixed \\(E\\), \\(z_1=\\sqrt\\Delta\\,k_r/E\\), and the radial bracket is, up to convention,\n\\[\n\\{z_1,z_2\\}=\\frac{\\sqrt\\Delta}{E}\\partial_rz_2\\neq0.\n\\]\nStationary time changes add an exact spatial one-form times \\(E\\), preserving the reduced bracket.</p><p>For the characteristic temporal root \\(E=e(y,\\eta)\\),\n\\[\n\\{f|_e,g|_e\\}\n=\n\\{f,g\\}\n+\n\\frac{g_E\\{q_E,f\\}-f_E\\{q_E,g\\}}{\\partial_Eq_E},\n\\qquad\n\\partial_Eq_E=-2\\Sigma_Kk^\\sharp(dt)\\neq0.\n\\]\nThe numerator vanishes on trapping for \\(f=z_1,g=z_2\\). Compactness gives uniform positive margins.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Polynomial tangent growth — original Lemma 3.5.** At fixed \\(E\\neq0\\), on a neighborhood of the null trapped set in \\( r=r_c(L_z/E),\\qquad k_r=0, \\) every fixed-order tangent derivative of the separated …", "statement": "<p>**Polynomial tangent growth — original Lemma 3.5.**</p><p>At fixed \\(E\\neq0\\), on a neighborhood of the null trapped set in\n\\[\nr=r_c(L_z/E),\\qquad k_r=0,\n\\]\nevery fixed-order tangent derivative of the separated fixed-frequency flow grows polynomially. The bounds are uniform on compact invariant sets, include a small interval of nearby symbol levels, and persist under smooth positive time changes bounded above and away from zero. They therefore hold in regular stationary time.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the null trap,\n\\[\nD+2aEL_z&gt;(r^2+2a^2)E^2,\n\\qquad\nc_1=D+2aEL_z-2a^2E^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis margin persists nearby, and the angular covector is nonzero.</p><p>With \\(x_1=\\cos\\theta\\), \\(A=a^2E^2\\),\n\\[\nQ=D-(L_z-aE)^2,\n\\]\nthe angular motion satisfies\n\\[\n(x_1')^2+c_1x_1^2+Ax_1^4=Q,\n\\qquad\nx_1''=-c_1x_1-2Ax_1^3.\n\\]\nIts squared amplitude\n\\[\nd_1^2=\\frac{2Q}{c_1+\\sqrt{c_1^2+4AQ}}\n\\]\nand period\n\\[\nT_{\\rm ang}=\n\\int_0^{2\\pi}\n\\frac{du}{\\sqrt{c_1+Ad_1^2(1+\\sin^2u)}}\n\\]\ndepend smoothly on the invariants, including equatorial and polar limits.</p><p>The axial circle action is free on the angular phase region with nonzero covectors. After one angular period, the flow equals a unique smooth circle-valued rotation \\(R_{\\alpha(z)}\\). Both period and return rotation are invariant along the flow. Hence\n\\[\n\\Phi_sz=R_{\\alpha(z)}^n\\Phi_{s-nT_{\\rm ang}(z)}z.\n\\]\nChoose \\(n\\) at the evaluation point so the residual time is bounded, keeping \\(n\\) fixed while differentiating. Since \\(|n|=O(1+|s|)\\), each fixed derivative costs only a polynomial.</p><p>For a positive time change\n\\[\nt=\\int_0^s\\tau(\\Phi_uz)\\,du,\\qquad 0&lt;c\\leq\\tau\\leq C,\n\\]\nthe integral has polynomial derivatives and its \\(s\\)-derivative is bounded below. Repeated implicit differentiation gives polynomial derivatives of \\(s(t,z)\\). Nearby levels change only the allowed \\(D\\), and all formulas persist where \\(c_1&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Spatial ellipticity at the projected trap and zero-energy propagation — original Proposition 3.7 and Lemma 3.8.** For the fixed Kerr metric of Proposition 2, choose \\(G'=1\\) through the photon region.", "statement": "<p>**Spatial ellipticity at the projected trap and zero-energy propagation — original Proposition 3.7 and Lemma 3.8.**</p><p>For the fixed Kerr metric of Proposition 2, choose \\(G'=1\\) through the photon region. There is a spatial conic neighborhood \\(\\mathcal T\\) of the projections of both trapped null sheets, compactly based away from the horizon and the ends, such that\n\\[\nb^{ij}(y)\\eta_i\\eta_j\\geq c|\\eta|^2.\n\\]\nBoth temporal roots are nonzero and comparable in absolute value to \\(|\\eta|\\). Nested conic neighborhoods and the same strict margins persist for nearby exact Kerr parameters and stationary charts.</p><p>The zero-temporal-energy characteristic set has no photon trapping away from the horizon radial set. Strictly outside the horizon its spatial Hamilton flow is real principal and nonradial, including at covectors dual to the stationary Killing field on the stationary limit surface. Thus spatial fiber infinity at bounded temporal frequency introduces no additional stationary trapped set.</p>", "proof": "<p>The normalized trap is compact, and its spatial projection is nonzero because a nonzero multiple of the timelike covector \\(dt\\) cannot be null. Lemma 3.1 therefore yields a uniform positive lower bound on that projection and, by continuity, on nested conic neighborhoods.</p><p>The temporal equation\n\\[\nb^{tt}E^2-2b^{ti}\\eta_iE+b^{ij}\\eta_i\\eta_j=0\n\\]\nhas \\(b^{tt}&lt;0\\) and positive constant term on \\(\\mathcal T\\), so the roots have opposite signs and are uniformly comparable to \\(|\\eta|\\). Smooth dependence preserves the margins.</p><p>Lemma 3.2 and Proposition 2 exhaust zero-energy escape and horizon behavior. Standard horizon-source propagation and the supported reverse estimate are followed by ordinary real-principal propagation. No positive Killing-current norm throughout the ergoregion is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Projected-trap identity — geometric calculation in original Proposition 3.7.** At a trapped covector \\(k=-E\\,dt+\\eta\\), let \\(\\eta^\\uparrow=k+E\\,dt\\).", "statement": "<p>**Projected-trap identity — geometric calculation in original Proposition 3.7.**</p><p>At a trapped covector \\(k=-E\\,dt+\\eta\\), let \\(\\eta^\\uparrow=k+E\\,dt\\). Then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Sigma_Kk^\\sharp(dt)}E\n=\n\\Sigma_K+\\frac{4Mr^2}{r-M},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Sigma_K}{E^2}b^{-1}(\\eta^\\uparrow,\\eta^\\uparrow)\n=\n\\Sigma_K+\\frac{8Mr^2}{r-M}-2Mr&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>At trapping \\(\\dot r=0\\), so \\(\\dot t=\\dot T\\). Substitute the double-root identities from Proposition 2 into the separated time equation. Then use nullness and\n\\[\n\\Sigma_Kb^{-1}(dt,dt)=-\\Sigma_K-2Mr.\n\\]\nThe last expression is positive for \\(r&gt;r_+&gt;M\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Zero-energy directions — original Lemma 3.8.** For a future exterior null covector with \\(E=0\\), one has \\(L_z\\neq0\\), \\(D>0\\), and \\( R(r)=a^2L_z^2-\\Delta D,\\qquad R'(r)=-2(r-M)D<0. \\) The corresponding spatial …", "statement": "<p>**Zero-energy directions — original Lemma 3.8.**</p><p>For a future exterior null covector with \\(E=0\\), one has \\(L_z\\neq0\\), \\(D&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\nR(r)=a^2L_z^2-\\Delta D,\\qquad\nR'(r)=-2(r-M)D&lt;0.\n\\]\nThe corresponding spatial characteristic flow has at most one simple radial turn and is nonzero and nonradial.</p>", "proof": "<p>Positivity of \\(P=-aL_z\\) gives \\(L_z\\neq0\\), hence \\(D&gt;0\\), proving the radial assertions.</p><p>If the spatial base component of the Hamilton field vanishes, its spacetime dual is proportional to \\(\\partial_T\\); nullness puts the point on the stationary limit surface. The radial root is still simple, so the spatial Hamilton field is nonzero. It cannot be pure nonzero fiber dilation because the nonzero \\(L_z\\) is conserved. Between the horizons the radial component is nonzero; all nongenerator horizon crossings are transverse.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Stationary gauges, causal modulation, and retarded tests — original Proposition 4.13.** Fix one computing cylinder from Propositions 2–3, with exact Kerr metric \\(b\\), strict inner outflow, and outgoing function …", "statement": "<p>**Stationary gauges, causal modulation, and retarded tests — original Proposition 4.13.**</p><p>Fix one computing cylinder from Propositions 2–3, with exact Kerr metric \\(b\\), strict inner outflow, and outgoing function \\(\\varphi=r_*\\) near infinity and zero on a fixed inner collar. Use the Laplace convention \\(\\partial_t=\\zeta\\), \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\zeta\\geq0\\), and\n\\[\nH_b^{s,\\ell}=r^{-\\ell-3/2}H_b^s(dr\\,d\\omega/r).\n\\]\nInterior primal spaces are extendible at inner outflow; spatial-transpose spaces are supported there.</p><p>There are real compactly supported spatial pseudodifferential gauge and partner additions, exact stationary Kerr reference families, causal parameter feedback, and a reduced vacuum evolution with the properties established below.</p><p>In particular, for\n\\[\nM_R(\\zeta)=L(\\zeta)+\\chi_RQ(\\zeta)F(\\zeta)\\ell,\n\\]\nfix a compact test support and finitely many angular derivatives. There are finite \\(q_*,N_*\\) such that, after choosing the filter order \\(P\\) large, its parameter \\(\\sigma_0&gt;0\\) small, and then \\(\\Lambda,R\\) large in the stated order, the spatial-transpose equation\n\\[\nM_R(\\zeta)^tv_*=f\n\\]\nhas solutions on \\(0&lt;\\operatorname{Re}\\zeta\\leq D_*\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\|v_*\\|_{\\dot H^{-4}(K)}\n\\leq CR^{3\\iota}\\langle\\zeta\\rangle^{N_*}\\|f\\|_{H^{q_*}},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|(v_*,r(\\partial_r+\\zeta)v_*)\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\leq Cr^{-1+\\iota}R^{3\\iota}\n\\langle\\zeta\\rangle^{N_*}\\|f\\|_{H^{q_*}}\n\\]\nfor large \\(r\\), with the prescribed angular derivatives. The bounds are uniform down to the imaginary axis on a strip reaching a forward energy line. The slack \\(\\iota&gt;0\\) may be arbitrarily small without making \\(N_*\\) diverge. The associated tested operators are retarded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Near zero, write\n\\[\n\\delta_f=F(\\zeta)^tQ(\\zeta)^t\\chi_Rv_*,\n\\qquad\nL(\\zeta)^tv_*=f-\\ell^t\\delta_f.\n\\]\nSupplement Lemma 4.4 by a compact measurement separating \\(K_0^*\\). Pairing a normalized limiting equation with \\(H,J\\) forces \\(\\delta_f=0\\); the measurement removes the remaining nullstate. For \\(v_0\\in K_0^*\\) matching that measurement,\n\\[\n\\|e^{\\zeta\\varphi}v_*-v_0\\|_A+|\\delta_f|\n\\leq C(\\|f\\|_{H^{q_*}}+|\\zeta||v_0|).\n\\]\nColumn pairings cost at most\n\\[\nC_\\iota\\min(R,|\\zeta|^{-1})^{2\\iota}\n(\\|f\\|+|\\zeta||v_0|).\n\\]\nLemma 4.12 and invertibility of \\(F(0)\\) absorb the resulting\n\\(O(|\\zeta|^{1-2\\iota})|v_0|\\) term after \\(R\\) is large and the zero neighborhood small. Index zero then gives inversion.</p><p>On closed nonzero bands, first retain the full columns. Integration by parts reduces the finite-dimensional condition to\n\\[\nI+[I_H,I_J+\\zeta\\ell H^1]\n\\operatorname{diag}(F_m/\\zeta,F_v/\\zeta^2).\n\\]\nFor small \\(\\sigma_0\\), it is near identity when \\(|\\zeta|/\\sigma_0\\) is large. With \\(w=\\zeta/\\sigma_0\\) bounded, multiplying the Kerr columns by \\(w\\) and translation columns by \\(w^2\\) gives limiting blocks \\(1+w\\) and \\((1+w)^2\\), nonzero for \\(\\operatorname{Re}w\\geq0\\). A large \\(R\\) absorbs the removed tail.</p><p>At high frequency, choose \\(P\\) to dominate the fixed polynomial losses of Lemma 4.3 and the columns. For compact forcing, rotation scattering estimates and\n\\[\n(\\partial_{r_*}-\\zeta)(\\partial_{r_*}+\\zeta)(rv_*)\n=\nO(r^{-2})\\langle\\zeta\\rangle^2\n(\\partial_\\omega^{\\leq2}(rv_*),\n\\partial_\\omega^{\\leq1}\\partial_{r_*}(rv_*))\n\\]\ngive the radiation estimate by two radial integrations. Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz supplies \\(r^{-1+\\iota}\\); decreasing \\(\\iota\\) changes constants, not the derivative count.</p><p>Fix testing orders first, then \\(P,\\sigma_0\\), a uniform forward line, sufficiently large \\(\\Lambda\\), and finally \\(R\\). Analytic physical inversion on the open strip agrees on the forward line with wave–Volterra uniqueness. Contour displacement proves retardation down to the boundary. Only a finite base Sobolev list is made small.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Local spectral input — original Proposition 4.1.** There are arbitrarily small stationary smooth compactly based local modifications of harmonic gauge and its symmetric-gradient partner such that:", "statement": "<p>**Local spectral input — original Proposition 4.1.**</p><p>There are arbitrarily small stationary smooth compactly based local modifications of harmonic gauge and its symmetric-gradient partner such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The stationary gauge-potential operator is invertible at decay strictly between powers \\(1\\) and \\(r^{-1}\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The reduced linearized vacuum operator has outgoing inversion at nonzero frequency in the closed physical half-plane; bounded-frequency estimates with weaker compact error are uniform down to zero for\n\\[\n-\\tfrac32&lt;\\ell&lt;-\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nIts zero-frequency index is zero and its kernel has dimension seven: four gauge-corrected Kerr variations and three translations.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. An outgoing-time polynomial mode with coefficients \\(O(r^{-1+})\\), if of degree one, has translation leading coefficient; none has degree at least two.</p><p>One may take tensor order \\(s&gt;5/2\\), with margin. The near-zero equation norm is \\(H_b^{s-1,\\ell+2}\\). At nonzero frequency, outgoing conditions are \\(\\ell&lt;-1/2\\) and \\(s+\\ell&gt;-1/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>These are the local gauge-potential, bounded-frequency, index, kernel, and polynomial-mode results cited in the paper from Häfner–Hintz–Vasy [28, Theorems 5.1, 5.2, 6.6, Proposition 6.8, Lemma 6.9].</p><p>The geometric conventions agree: time is horizon regular and outgoing time is \\(t-\\varphi\\), with\n\\[\nb^{ti}=O(r^{-2}),\\qquad\n\\partial_y(\\varphi-r)=O(r^{-1}),\\qquad\nb^{-1}(d(t-\\varphi),d(t-\\varphi))=O(r^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe logarithmic coefficient \\(2M\\) is spin independent. Bounded smooth offsets and compact stationary chart changes preserve the outgoing graphs. Extending the inner edge between strict interior depths uses ordinary hyperbolic propagation in radial time, with polynomial tangential-frequency cost on bounded real Laplace strips. Choose the local damping sufficiently small for subsequent fixed thresholds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Conic gauge calibration — original Lemma 4.2.** For a nearby exact stationary Kerr metric \\(\\bar g\\) and nearby stationary target \\(B_0\\), additional gauge and partner factors can be chosen as real operators …", "statement": "<p>**Conic gauge calibration — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><p>For a nearby exact stationary Kerr metric \\(\\bar g\\) and nearby stationary target \\(B_0\\), additional gauge and partner factors can be chosen as real operators\n\\[\nA_0+A_1\\partial_t,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{ord}_yA_0=0,\\quad\n\\operatorname{ord}_yA_1=-1,\n\\]\nwith compact kernels in both variables away from boundaries and high-frequency microsupport in \\(\\mathcal T\\).</p><p>At high frequency their transport near trapping is compatible with a smooth positive homogeneous bundle metric. At the base metric, unsaturated transport and cutoff transitions have arbitrarily small imaginary subprincipal defect relative to the minimum normal rate. Additional temporal-acceleration coefficients have spatial order at most \\(-1\\) and are small on any fixed finite Sobolev list for sufficiently large cutoff \\(\\Lambda\\). The prescription is smooth in the metric and target.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a null covector \\(k\\), define\n\\[\nQ_kH=H(k^\\sharp,\\cdot)-\\tfrac12(\\operatorname{tr}_{\\bar g}H)k,\n\\qquad\nd_ke=k\\odot e.\n\\]\nThe map \\(d_k\\) is injective, \\(Q_k\\) is surjective, and\n\\(\\operatorname{im}d_k\\subset\\ker Q_k\\). Parallel transport preserves this flag; its quotient is the tracefree symmetric screen tensors.</p><p>Normalize \\(n=k^\\sharp/E\\), which is parallel. For stationary unit timelike \\(T\\), put\n\\[\n\\alpha=-\\bar g(n,T),\\qquad\nl=T/\\alpha-n/(2\\alpha^2).\n\\]\nIn a screen orthonormal frame,\n\\[\n\\nabla n=0,\\qquad \\nabla l=w,\\qquad\n\\nabla e_A=\\sum_B\\omega_{AB}e_B+\\bar g(w,e_A)n,\n\\quad \\omega_{AB}=-\\omega_{BA}.\n\\]\nGive \\(n,l\\) positive lengths \\(\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon^{-1}\\). Null-rotation terms then have norm \\(O(\\varepsilon)\\), while screen rotation is skew.</p><p>Choose metric transport preserving an orthogonal flag splitting and the same quotient transport. Its difference \\(A\\) from the original transport maps \\(\\ker Q_k\\) to \\(\\operatorname{im}d_k\\). For a right inverse \\(R_k\\), \\(P_k=1-R_kQ_k\\), realize it by\n\\[\nE_g(k)=d_k^{-1}AP_k,\\qquad C(k)=AR_k.\n\\]\nCompensate the target’s algebraic gauge term and the local additions as well.</p><p>On the roots \\(k=(-P_\\pm,\\eta)\\), interpolate the prescribed values by\n\\[\nA_1=\\frac{E_+^{\\rm add}-E_-^{\\rm add}}{-i(P_+-P_-)},\n\\qquad\nA_0=E_+^{\\rm add}+iP_+A_1.\n\\]\nThe root gap is comparable to \\(|\\eta|\\). Reversal symmetry makes the operator real.</p><p>Use even conic and high-frequency cutoffs and compact kernel cutoffs. The common scalar cutoff interpolates first-order transport; derivatives in compositions are lower order. Separated-support leakage is \\(O(\\Lambda^{-N})\\) smoothing in every fixed seminorm. Extra coefficients of \\(\\partial_t^2\\) gain a spatial derivative and are small on the prescribed finite list.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Stationary estimates after modification — original Proposition 4.3.** For sufficiently large \\(\\Lambda\\):", "statement": "<p>**Stationary estimates after modification — original Proposition 4.3.**</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(\\Lambda\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The bounded-frequency compact-error estimates, index zero, and kernel/cokernel upper semicontinuity persist. Closed nonzero bounded bands retain physical estimates without compact error.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. On each fixed strip \\(0\\leq\\operatorname{Re}\\zeta\\leq D_*\\), fixed admissible orders have polynomial high-frequency estimates without kernel, uniformly for large \\(\\Lambda\\). These include compact \\(H^s\\) control from \\(H^{s-1}\\), the supported dual orders \\(1-s,-s\\), and physical-transpose scattering control\n\\[\n\\langle r\\rangle^{-1/2-\\iota}(v,\\partial_yv)\\in L^2\n\\]\nfor compact smooth sources.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Zero-frequency estimates and fixed finite-dimensional supplements persist for nearby exact Kerr principal metrics in the prescribed asymptotically affine charts.</p>", "proof": "<p>On compact elliptic microsupport, nested cutoffs give\n\\[\n\\|(L-L_h)(\\zeta)u\\|_{H^{s-1}}\n\\leq\nC\\Lambda^{-1}\\|\\operatorname{Op}(\\beta)u\\|_{H^{s+1}}\n+O(\\Lambda^{-N})\\|u\\|_{H^s(K')}.\n\\]\nAn elliptic parametrix absorbs the stronger norm. The same argument applies to supported dual and gauge-potential orders.</p><p>Homogeneous physical limiting-absorption states on a nonzero band belong to the outgoing class. Rotation commutators at infinity are short range because the mass \\(r^{-1}\\) term is rotation invariant. After removing \\(e^{-\\zeta r_*}\\), the radial equation gives symbol derivatives by induction:\n\\[\n(\\partial_{r_*}-2\\zeta)\\partial_{r_*}w\n=O(r^{-2})(\\partial_\\omega^{\\leq2}w,\n\\partial_\\omega^{\\leq1}\\partial_{r_*}w).\n\\]\nThe raw decay excludes the incoming homogeneous solution, including on the imaginary axis. The local spectral input excludes the resulting homogeneous outgoing state.</p><p>At high frequency apply the cited normally hyperbolic local outgoing estimates [22,29]. Propositions 2–3 and Lemma 4.2 verify symplectic transversality, smooth tails, escape, and a strict subprincipal margin. Symmetric normalization by \\(\\sqrt{\\Sigma_K}\\) scales both transport and Hamilton flow without an additional first bracket term. Fix this normalization before choosing the arbitrarily small defect, using the maximum time-change factor and minimum normalized normal rate.</p><p>The added characteristic microsupport has \\(|\\eta|\\asymp|\\zeta|\\). Its rescaled symbols are uniformly lower order. At small relative spatial frequency the perturbation is\n\\[\n\\zeta^2K_{-1}+\\zeta K_0+K_1,\n\\]\nwith relative cost \\(O(\\Lambda^{-1})+O(|\\zeta|^{-1})\\). At large relative spatial frequency its support is elliptic. The modifications miss the horizon radial set and all zero-energy nonelliptic directions.</p><p>Nearby zero-frequency models retain strict horizon margins and the same indicial gaps after a constant linear spatial change. Supplemented primal and dual compact-error estimates and equation convergence exclude normalized contradictory nullstates. Compact high regularity may be switched to lower far derivative orders across full elliptic annuli.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Outgoing spatial transpose — original Lemma 4.4.** Let \\(\\ell_{\\rm wt}=-1/2-\\iota\\), with small \\(\\iota>0\\), and let \\(j\\) be a sufficiently large fixed exterior order.", "statement": "<p>**Outgoing spatial transpose — original Lemma 4.4.**</p><p>Let \\(\\ell_{\\rm wt}=-1/2-\\iota\\), with small \\(\\iota&gt;0\\), and let \\(j\\) be a sufficiently large fixed exterior order. Let \\(A\\) have supported interior order \\(-4\\) and exterior space \\(H_b^{j,\\ell_{\\rm wt}}\\); let \\(S\\) have supported interior order \\(-5\\) and exterior space \\(H_b^{j-1,\\ell_{\\rm wt}+2}\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nP^\\sharp(\\zeta)=e^{\\zeta\\varphi}L(\\zeta)^te^{-\\zeta\\varphi}\n\\]\nthere is a uniform near-zero estimate from \\(S\\) to \\(A\\) with weaker compact error. At fixed small nonzero \\(\\zeta\\), \\(\\operatorname{Re}\\zeta&gt;0\\), the graph with exterior equation space \\(H_b^{j,\\ell_{\\rm wt}+1}\\) has index zero, also after compact smooth finite-rank augmentation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Combine the supported interior dual estimate with a primal outgoing estimate cut to infinity. The comparison difference is\n\\[\nO(r^{-2})\\partial_y+O(r^{-3})\n+(\\zeta+\\zeta^2)O(r^{-2}),\n\\]\nsmall at large radius and near zero.</p><p>The unconjugated physical primal and supported transpose have index zero in the open half-plane: the flat normal operator is invertible, zero-energy spatial propagation and redshift give compact-error estimates, and a large forward line has wave-energy inversion after the small acceleration solve. Continue the index through the half-plane.</p><p>Exchange the end of a comparison outgoing primal problem with the physical supported-transpose end in their direct sum, using a signed exchange on an elliptic annulus. Collar errors are compact. The conjugated fixed-frequency graph gains\n\\[\nH_b^{j+1,\\ell_{\\rm wt}-1}\n\\]\nby exterior ellipticity applied to\n\\[\nr^2P=B_2+rB_1+B_0.\n\\]\nThe difference \\(O(r^{-2})\\partial_y+O(r^{-2})\\) then gains one tail weight relative to the equation space; its norm beyond radius \\(Y\\) is \\(O(Y^{-1})\\), and the compact part is Rellich compact. The index is therefore zero.</p><p>This is an index, not automatically an inverse. Trivial augmented outgoing-transpose kernel supplies its inverse. Physical exponentially decaying tests then kill the physical primal kernel by supported–extendible integration by parts. The same-sign conjugation is not identified with the transpose of the outgoing-conjugated primal.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Profiles and mode preservation — original Lemma 4.5.** There are stationary profiles \\(H_i\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq4\\), and \\(J_i,H_i^1\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq3\\), with stationary vector fields \\(V_i,W_i\\), such that …", "statement": "<p>**Profiles and mode preservation — original Lemma 4.5.**</p><p>There are stationary profiles \\(H_i\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq4\\), and \\(J_i,H_i^1\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq3\\), with stationary vector fields \\(V_i,W_i\\), such that\n\\[\nJ_i=\\mathcal L_{V_i}b,\\qquad\nH_i^1=\\mathcal L_{W_i}b+2dt\\odot V_i^\\flat,\n\\]\n\\[\nV_i=e_i+O(r^{-1}),\\qquad W_i=y_i\\partial_t+O(r^{0+}),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathsf BH_i=\\mathsf BJ_i=\\mathsf B(tJ_i+H_i^1)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\nL(0)H_i=L(0)J_i=0,\\qquad\nL(0)H_i^1=-[L,t]J_i,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathsf B\\) denotes the linearized gauge operator. Moreover,\n\\[\nH_i,H_i^1=r^{-1}(\\text{smooth angular term})+O(r^{-2+}),\n\\]\n\\[\nJ_i=r^{-2}(\\text{smooth angular term})+O(r^{-3+}).\n\\]\nFinite-rank smoothing corrections \\(O(\\Lambda^{-N})\\) preserve these identities exactly. The decaying stationary kernel is exactly the seven-dimensional span of \\(H,J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Start with the local Kerr, translation, and boost profiles cited from [28]. Correct cutoff generators using the stationary gauge-potential inverse. Slight-growth weights are surjective for the nondecaying corrections.</p><p>The leading translation error is a constant combination of second Cartesian derivatives of degree-\\(-1\\) coefficients, with zero spherical mean by integration over shells and scaling. Its correction has an \\(r^{-1}\\) angular leading term without a leading logarithm. The flat identity\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{te_i+y_i\\partial_t}\\eta=0\n\\]\ngives the chain. Indicial expansion gives the profile orders.</p><p>The seven profiles are independent on a far exterior region: the stationary Killing-time norm detects mass, the leading curl of \\(b_{ti}dy^i/b_{tt}\\) detects angular momentum, and a nonzero translation acts nontrivially on the leading radial mass term.</p><p>For a basis \\(q_\\alpha\\) of compactly cut-off profile restrictions and compact dual tests \\(\\lambda_\\alpha\\), subtract\n\\[\nS_0f=\\sum_\\alpha(A_{0,\\Lambda}q_\\alpha)\\lambda_\\alpha(\\chi_{\\rm in}f),\n\\]\nand similarly for the \\(A_1\\) action on \\(J\\). Each \\(q_\\alpha\\) is fixed and smooth, so the subtractions are \\(O(\\Lambda^{-N})\\) in every fixed smoothing norm. They preserve the identities; stationary compactness excludes extra decaying modes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exact stationary families and supplements — original Proposition 4.6.** There is a smooth exact diffeomorphic stationary Kerr family \\(G_p\\), \\(p=(m,v)\\in\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^3\\), tangent at zero to …", "statement": "<p>**Exact stationary families and supplements — original Proposition 4.6.**</p><p>There is a smooth exact diffeomorphic stationary Kerr family \\(G_p\\), \\(p=(m,v)\\in\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^3\\), tangent at zero to \\(H,\\mathcal L_Wb\\), with\n\\[\nG_p-\\eta_v=O(r^{-1}),\\qquad\n\\eta_v=-(1-|v|^2)dt^2-2v_i\\,dy^i\\,dt+|dy|^2.\n\\]\nIts stationary reduced operator has seven independent decaying modes and ten further homogeneous metric modes with independent constant tensor asymptotes, with no further dimensions at growth strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).</p><p>There are compact smooth measurements \\(\\ell,\\ell_p^C\\) and ten modes \\(H^C(p)\\), supported for measurement away from a physical trapping neighborhood, satisfying\n\\[\n\\ell(H,J)=I,\\qquad\n\\ell H^C(p)=0,\\qquad\n\\ell_p^CH^C(p)=I,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\ell_p^C\\) annihilating decaying modes. A second exact stationary Kerr family satisfies\n\\[\nG_{p,0}=G_p,\\qquad \\partial_cG_{p,0}=H^C(p),\n\\quad c\\in\\mathbb R^{10}.\n\\]\nThe calibration is smooth on these families, and the frozen gauge of \\(G_{p,c}\\) relative to \\(G_p\\) is \\(O(c^2)\\) on fixed compact sets.</p>", "proof": "<p>Integrate the stationary correction flows. For velocity use\n\\[\nt_K=\\sqrt{1-|v|^2}\\,t+\\frac{v\\cdot y}{\\sqrt{1-|v|^2}},\n\\qquad\ny_K=\\left(I+\\frac{vv^t}{1-|v|^2}\\right)^{1/2}y,\n\\]\nwhose flat pullback is \\(\\eta_v\\). Express nearby Kerr metrics in smooth ingoing coordinates, bend on a larger domain, and return to Boyer–Lindquist conventions at infinity. Nonzero central spin permits smooth axis alignment.</p><p>Seven decaying modes follow from supplemented stationary and gauge-potential estimates. Ten constant tensor asymptotes arise from affine pure gauge, including stationary-generator scaling, corrected by \\(O(r^{0+})\\) stationary vectors. The flat indicial gap gives the dimension count below growth one.</p><p>Fixed far tests separate the modes. Uniform supplemented estimates and difference quotients give smooth dependence and prevent nearby normalized modes from losing separation. Normalize the additional modes by \\(\\ell H^C=0\\), integrate their physical and diffeomorphism directions, and use stationary gauge tangency to obtain the quadratic residual.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Adjoint tails — original Lemma 4.7.** The supported bilinear-spatial-transpose kernel \\(K_p^*\\) of the decaying stationary problem is seven-dimensional, locally \\(\\dot H^{-4}\\), and conormal \\(O(r^{-2+})\\) at …", "statement": "<p>**Adjoint tails — original Lemma 4.7.**</p><p>The supported bilinear-spatial-transpose kernel \\(K_p^*\\) of the decaying stationary problem is seven-dimensional, locally \\(\\dot H^{-4}\\), and conormal \\(O(r^{-2+})\\) at infinity, with locally uniform weak bases and far symbol bounds.</p><p>If \\(H_0\\) is smooth extendible stationary with symbol growth \\(O(r^{0+})\\), then\n\\[\n\\langle v,L_p(0)H_0\\rangle=0,\\qquad v\\in K_p^*.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Index zero and the kernel dimension give the adjoint dimension. Indicial regularity initially permits \\(r^{-1}\\) fundamental tails. Green pairing with the ten constant-asymptote homogeneous modes kills their leading coefficients. The supported convention has no inner boundary contribution, leaving \\(O(r^{-2+})\\).</p><p>For \\(v=O(r^{-2+\\varepsilon})\\), \\(H_0=O(r^{\\varepsilon'})\\), the outer Green terms are \\(O(r^{-1+\\varepsilon+\\varepsilon'})\\). Choose \\(\\varepsilon+\\varepsilon'&lt;1\\) and let \\(r\\to\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Causal averaging and filter regularity — original Lemma 4.8 and definitions (4.17)–(4.23).** Write \\(p'=a\\), \\(\\lambda'=v\\), with zero-past histories.", "statement": "<p>**Causal averaging and filter regularity — original Lemma 4.8 and definitions (4.17)–(4.23).**</p><p>Write \\(p'=a\\), \\(\\lambda'=v\\), with zero-past histories. Choose \\(d(x)\\geq0\\), zero on a ball containing every compact kernel and probe and equal to \\(|x|\\) near infinity. Choose a signed smooth kernel \\(k_0\\), compactly supported in \\((0,1/10)\\), with\n\\[\n\\int k_0=1,\\qquad \\int\\sigma k_0(\\sigma)\\,d\\sigma=0,\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\nf_s(t,x)=\\int k_0(\\sigma)f(t-\\sigma d(x))\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]\nThe equation \\(x=z+\\lambda_s(t,x)\\) is a contraction for small speed. Define the target by\n\\[\nB=G_{p_s(t,x)}(x)\n\\quad\\text{in the formal coframe }(dt,dz+v_sdt).\n\\]</p><p>With \\(h=g-B\\), \\(u=\\partial_th_{\\rm co}\\),\n\\[\nD=\\partial_{t,\\rm lab}-v_s\\cdot\\partial_z,\\qquad\n\\psi=Dh_{\\rm lab},\n\\]\nuse\n\\[\nc=\\ell_p^Ch_{\\rm co},\\qquad\na=F(\\partial_t)(\\chi_{\\rm st}\\ell h_{\\rm co}),\n\\]\n\\[\nF_m(\\zeta)=\\frac{\\sigma_0}{(1+\\zeta)^P},\n\\qquad\nF_v(\\zeta)=\\frac{2\\sigma_0\\zeta+\\sigma_0^2}{(1+\\zeta)^P},\n\\qquad P\\geq5.\n\\]\nFor every \\(j\\geq0\\), \\(a^{(j)}\\) is controlled by input derivatives through\n\\[\n\\max(0,j-P+1)\n\\]\nin polynomially weighted \\(L_t^2\\) and \\(L_t^\\infty\\), including finite intervals. For \\(j\\geq1\\), one may differentiate the input first and use \\(\\ell u\\), charging startup terms to initial data.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(G_0=\\delta_0\\) and\n\\[\nG_j(t)=1_{t\\geq0}e^{-t}\\frac{t^{j-1}}{(j-1)!}.\n\\]\nThe filter kernels are\n\\[\n\\sigma_0G_P,\\qquad\n2\\sigma_0G_{P-1}+(\\sigma_0^2-2\\sigma_0)G_P.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\partial_tG_j=G_{j-1}-G_j\\), derivatives through \\(P-1\\) are finite measures with all polynomial moments finite. Move the remaining derivatives to the smooth zero-past input and apply weighted Young inequality. Causality gives the same bounds on finite intervals.</p><p>The zero first moment makes \\(B\\) exactly the frozen stationary metric in co-moving coordinates whenever \\(p\\) is constant and \\(\\lambda\\) affine throughout the sampled interval.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.9", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Joint linearization and columns — original Lemma 4.9.** Use \\( \\Upsilon=\\Upsilon_{\\rm wave}(g;B)+E_g(p,c)h+\\Theta \\) in \\( \\operatorname{Ric}(g)+(D_g+C)\\Upsilon=0. \\) The compensator is the gauge image of …", "statement": "<p>**Joint linearization and columns — original Lemma 4.9.**</p><p>Use\n\\[\n\\Upsilon=\\Upsilon_{\\rm wave}(g;B)+E_g(p,c)h+\\Theta\n\\]\nin\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ric}(g)+(D_g+C)\\Upsilon=0.\n\\]\nThe compensator is the gauge image of\n\\[\nY=Hm_s+J\\lambda_s+H^1v_s+\n2(V-e)^\\flat\\odot d_x\\lambda_s,\n\\]\ncomputed in the unsmoothed region and extended by the prescribed formal-coframe construction.</p><p>The joint linearization at \\(g=b\\), zero parameters, is\n\\[\nLh+Q(\\partial_t)a.\n\\]\nWriting\n\\[\nK_d(y,\\zeta)=\\int k_0(\\sigma)e^{-\\zeta\\sigma d(y)}\\,d\\sigma,\n\\]\nthe columns are\n\\[\nY_m=HK_d,\\qquad\nY_v=(J+\\zeta H^1)K_d+\n2(V-e)^\\flat\\odot d_yK_d,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ(\\zeta)=\\left(\\frac{L(\\zeta)Y_m}{\\zeta},\n\\frac{L(\\zeta)Y_v}{\\zeta^2}\\right).\n\\]\nThe poles are removable. On fixed compact sets the columns have bounded-length causal memories and polynomial fixed-derivative frequency costs. For every fixed \\(N\\),\n\\[\nQ(\\zeta)=O_N\\!\\left(r^{-2}(1+|\\zeta|r)^{-N}\\right),\n\\qquad \\operatorname{Re}\\zeta\\geq0,\n\\]\nwith far symbol derivatives.</p>", "proof": "<p>The target variation in fixed co-moving coordinates, plus the Lie derivative generated by\n\\[\ne\\lambda+(V-e)\\lambda_s,\n\\]\nis \\(Y\\). Vacuum invariance removes this Lie derivative from the linearized Ricci tensor, and the compensator produces the stated columns.</p><p>The stationary and chain identities cancel the constant and linear Taylor coefficients required by the denominators. The kernel moments give \\(K_d=1+O(|\\zeta|^2r^2)\\) at small scaled frequency. At large scaled frequency integrate by parts in \\(\\sigma\\); positive compact delays give uniformity in the closed half-plane. The profile orders yield the \\(r^{-2}\\) bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.10", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Nonlinear target defect — original Lemma 4.10.** For small \\(\\int|a|\\) and small speed, define, at comparable large radii, \\( \\widetilde a(t,r) = Cr^{-1}\\int_{t-Cr}^{t}|a(\\tau)|\\,d\\tau. \\) In laboratory components …", "statement": "<p>**Nonlinear target defect — original Lemma 4.10.**</p><p>For small \\(\\int|a|\\) and small speed, define, at comparable large radii,\n\\[\n\\widetilde a(t,r)\n=\nCr^{-1}\\int_{t-Cr}^{t}|a(\\tau)|\\,d\\tau.\n\\]\nIn laboratory components,\n\\[\n\\Theta=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a),\\qquad\nf_B:=\\operatorname{Ric}(B)+(D_B+C)\\Theta\n=O(r^{-2}\\widetilde a),\n\\]\n\\[\nDf_B=O(r^{-3}\\widetilde a),\\qquad\nDB=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a),\n\\]\nwith every fixed required number of scaled laboratory derivatives. Fixed inner and transition defects use bounded-length parameter jets and vanish at exact freezing with affine position.</p>", "proof": "<p>Positive-delay integration by parts and the zero first moment give\n\\[\n|\\partial_t^j\\partial_x^\\alpha p_s|\n\\leq C_{j,\\alpha}r^{1-j-|\\alpha|}\\widetilde a\n\\quad(j+|\\alpha|\\geq1),\n\\]\nand the same bound for \\(\\partial_x\\lambda_s\\), including order zero. With\n\\[\nS_x=(I-\\partial_x\\lambda_s)^{-1},\n\\]\nimplicit differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\partial_{z_i}=(S_x)^j{}_i\\partial_{x_j},\n\\qquad D=\\partial_t|_x,\n\\qquad\n[D,\\partial_{z_i}]=(\\partial_{z_i}v_s^j)\\partial_{z_j}.\n\\]</p><p>The affine flat asymptote cancels exactly in the formal laboratory coframe:\n\\[\nB=\\eta+h(p_s,x),\\qquad\n\\partial_x^\\alpha\\partial_p^\\beta h=O(r^{-1-|\\alpha|}).\n\\]\nFreeze parameters and affine worldline at a point. First- and second-jet differences from that exact Kerr metric are \\(O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a)\\) and \\(O(r^{-2}\\widetilde a)\\). Vacuum freezing gives the Ricci bound. Applying \\(D\\), with its displayed commutators, gains an inverse radius.</p><p>The profile identities reduce \\(\\mathsf BY\\) to commutators with \\(m_s,v_s,\\lambda_s\\); no undifferentiated position or velocity remains. Profile decay and the averaged bounds give\n\\[\n\\Theta=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a),\\quad\n\\partial\\Theta,D\\Theta=O(r^{-2}\\widetilde a),\\quad\nD\\partial\\Theta=O(r^{-3}\\widetilde a).\n\\]\nFixed transition regions use smooth finite jet expressions and the filter bounds, not inverse-\\(d\\) estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.11", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Local evolution and vacuum initialization — original Proposition 4.11.** For smooth wave data small in \\( \\mathcal E_{10}(t) = \\|h_{\\rm co}(t)\\|_{H_{\\rm uloc}^{10}} + \\|\\partial_th_{\\rm co}(t)\\|_{H_{\\rm uloc}^{9}}, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Local evolution and vacuum initialization — original Proposition 4.11.**</p><p>For smooth wave data small in\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{10}(t)\n=\n\\|h_{\\rm co}(t)\\|_{H_{\\rm uloc}^{10}}\n+\n\\|\\partial_th_{\\rm co}(t)\\|_{H_{\\rm uloc}^{9}},\n\\]\nthe reduced equation with retarded feedback has smooth local evolution. It continues on finite intervals while this norm is bounded, parameters remain in their small range, acceleration and implicit-coordinate inverses remain controlled, and spacelike and outflow margins stay positive. Higher smooth orders propagate without additional smallness.</p><p>For vacuum constraint data, initial lapse and shift derivatives can be chosen so that \\(\\Upsilon\\) and its first normal derivative vanish. The resulting evolution is vacuum.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize and solve the extra temporal-acceleration coefficient, which is identity plus a compact order-\\(-1\\) operator small on a finite base list. Its inverse extends to every smooth order by derivative gain. Applied to second spatial and mixed derivatives, its correction is at most first order on wave jets.</p><p>Use high wave-pair energy and contraction one order lower. Kernels are bounded at high order and Lipschitz one order lower. Filters are zero-past Volterra variables; fixed histories are local outside the compact interaction region. Strict inner outflow supplies exiting flux. The construction does not assert metric finite propagation inside the nonlocal interaction region.</p><p>At order \\(s\\), target and compensator jets through \\(a^{(s+3)}\\) require at most\n\\[\n\\max(0,s+4-P)\\leq s-1\n\\]\nprojection derivatives. Normal solves and tame wave induction propagate smoothness. Annular rescaling propagates symbols on bounded slabs.</p><p>Imposing \\(\\Upsilon=0\\) solves for initial lapse and shift derivatives with the same small coefficient inverse. The normal Einstein constraints then give \\(\\partial_t\\Upsilon=0\\). Contracted Bianchi yields a homogeneous wave system with lower-order compact terms; uniqueness gives \\(\\Upsilon=0\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Ric}(g)=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.12", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Zero-frequency coupling — original Lemma 4.12.** For sufficiently large \\(\\Lambda\\), \\( Q(0)^t:K_0^*\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C^7 \\) has full rank.", "statement": "<p>**Zero-frequency coupling — original Lemma 4.12.**</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(\\Lambda\\),\n\\[\nQ(0)^t:K_0^*\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C^7\n\\]\nhas full rank.</p>", "proof": "<p>Expand in outgoing time \\(t_*=t-\\varphi\\). In adjoint pairings discard terms \\(L(0)H_0\\), \\(H_0=O(r^{0+})\\), using Lemma 4.7. The residuals are exactly the stationary equation errors of\n\\[\nt_*H,\\qquad\n\\tfrac12t_*^2J+t_*(H^1+\\varphi J).\n\\]\nThey are \\(O(r^{-3+})\\): the outgoing linear Taylor term is a multiple of \\(\\partial_r+1/r\\), modulo lower symbols, and annihilates the leading \\(r^{-1}\\) term.</p><p>At the local-gauge limit, singular coupling would permit solving away a nonzero combination of these residuals by a decaying stationary correction. The resulting outgoing polynomial has all coefficients \\(O(r^{-1+})\\). A nonzero quadratic coefficient violates the absence of degree-two modes; otherwise its nontranslation Kerr linear coefficient violates the degree-one classification. The discarded slight-growth terms are not retained in this polynomial. Uniform stationary compactness and adjoint tails preserve the rank for large \\(\\Lambda\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Compact finite-slab estimates — original Proposition 5.5.** Work on an arbitrary smooth finite bootstrap slab \\([0,T]\\) for the reduced vacuum evolution of Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>**Compact finite-slab estimates — original Proposition 5.5.**</p><p>Work on an arbitrary smooth finite bootstrap slab \\([0,T]\\) for the reduced vacuum evolution of Proposition 4. Write \\(u=\\partial_th_{\\rm co}\\), \\(\\psi=Dh_{\\rm lab}\\), \\(p'=a\\), \\(c=\\ell_p^Ch_{\\rm co}\\), and \\(\\bar g=G_{p,c}\\).</p><p>Use the levels\n\\[\n(U,s,A_i)=(h_{\\rm co},10,0),\\quad\n(h_{\\rm co},9,A_H),\\quad\n(u,8,A),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nA_H=1-\\eta_*,\\qquad A=1.36,\\qquad A_S=1.06,\n\\qquad 2A_H&gt;A.\n\\]\nThe compact budget \\(K_R\\) includes, on fixed nested reaches,\n\\[\n\\partial_t^bU\\in\nL^\\infty_{A_i}H^{s-b}\\cap L^2_{A_i}H^{s-b-1/2},\n\\qquad 0\\leq b\\leq s,\n\\]\nfull bulk order outside a physical trapping neighborhood, and\n\\[\nB_{\\rm nt}\\partial_t^bU\\in L^2_{A_i}H^{s-b},\n\\qquad b=0,1,\n\\]\nwhere \\(B_{\\rm nt}=1-\\Psi_{\\rm tr}\\) is scalar and \\(\\Psi_{\\rm tr}\\) is microsupported in the stationary-elliptic cone. It also includes the differential-end energy, bulk, and Hardy controls of\n\\[\n\\widetilde r\\,\\partial_y^\\mu U,\\qquad |\\mu|\\leq s-1,\n\\]\nthrough a cutoff at radii \\(3R\\)–\\(4R\\). Here \\(L^\\nu_\\alpha X\\) means\n\\(\\|\\langle t\\rangle^{\\alpha/2}\\cdot\\|_{L^\\nu([0,T];X)}\\).</p><p>Let \\(F\\) be the far budget specified in Proposition 6, let\n\\[\nS_a=\\|a\\|_{L_A^2},\n\\]\nand let \\(\\mathcal N\\) include these budgets and the sliced static \\(L^2_{A_S}\\) mixed-order-eight norms. Let \\(e_{\\rm in}\\) be the base input.</p><p>For some \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nK_R\n\\leq\nCR^{-\\kappa}(F+K_R)\n+\nC_{\\rm fix}\n\\left(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^{3/2}\n+(e_{\\rm in}\\mathcal N)^{1/2}\\right).\n\\]\nConstants are independent of \\(T\\). Choose stationary energy parameters first, then \\(R\\), then the time plateau \\(T_0\\), then bootstrap and data smallness. Acceleration and static bounds enter only as coefficient smallness and nonlinear errors, not additional linear forcing.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 5.4 to the three frozen energies of Lemma 5.2. The unfavorable outer-cutoff flux is bounded by the good shell derivatives\n\\[\n\\sum_{|\\mu|\\leq s-1}\n\\|\\tau^{A_i/2}(1,\\Omega,rX)\\partial_y^\\mu U\\|^2_{\\rm shell}.\n\\]\nIts square root is\n\\[\nCR^{-\\kappa_b}F+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2),\n\\quad\n0&lt;\\kappa_b&lt;\\min_i\\{(p_i-A_i)/2,0.98\\}.\n\\]\nExpand spatial derivatives using\n\\[\n\\partial_{z_d}=-n_dT_l+r^{-1}(n_drX+\\Omega_d).\n\\]\nTerms with an emitter derivative gain an inverse radius; pure time terms use \\(p_i&gt;A_i\\). Frame differences carry strong memories.</p><p>Every compact lower error is below the half-loss bulk order. Interpolate it by\n\\[\nR^{-\\iota_0}K_R+C_RR^{K\\iota_0}l,\n\\qquad K\\iota_0&lt;\\kappa_0/2,\n\\]\nand use Lemma 5.1. The linear-source pairing costs\n\\(CR^{\\delta/2}lK_R\\), with \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;\\kappa_0\\).</p><p>Time-weight derivatives use the unweighted top bulk for the extra half derivative; for example,\n\\[\n\\tau^{A_i-1}\\|U\\|_{H^s}^2\n\\lesssim\n\\tau^{A_i/2-1}\n\\bigl(\\tau^{A_i/2}\\|U\\|_{H^{s-1/2}}\\bigr)\n\\|U\\|_{H^{s+1/2}}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(A_H,A&lt;2\\), a large \\(T_0\\) makes these small.</p><p>Recover mixed jets successively from the normalized equation. At the \\(j\\)-th recovery the bulk target order is\n\\[\nr'=s-j-2-\\theta,\\qquad\n\\theta=\\tfrac12\\text{ globally},\\quad\n\\theta=0\\text{ off physical trapping}.\n\\]\nCoefficient/product margins are\n\\[\nr'\\geq-\\tfrac12,\\qquad\ns-k\\geq r'+2,\\qquad\n(s-k)+(r'+k)-r'=s.\n\\]\nNested physical cutoffs and one-order kernel commutators recover full mixed bulk off trapping. No higher-time directional conic estimate is needed. Taking square roots yields the stated estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Low-frequency transfer — original Proposition 5.1.** Let \\(l\\) be the sum on the fixed compact reaches of the \\(L^2_{A_H}H^{-N_-}\\) norms of \\(h_{\\rm co},\\partial_th_{\\rm co}\\) and the \\(L^2_AH^{-N_-}\\) norms of …", "statement": "<p>**Low-frequency transfer — original Proposition 5.1.**</p><p>Let \\(l\\) be the sum on the fixed compact reaches of the\n\\(L^2_{A_H}H^{-N_-}\\) norms of \\(h_{\\rm co},\\partial_th_{\\rm co}\\) and the \\(L^2_AH^{-N_-}\\) norms of \\(u,\\partial_tu\\), for sufficiently large fixed \\(N_-\\). Then, after \\(R\\) and \\(T_0\\) are large,\n\\[\nl\\leq CR^{-\\kappa_0}(K_R+F)\n+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2)\n\\]\nfor some \\(\\kappa_0&gt;0\\) fixed before enlarging prior compact reaches.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the augmented retarded operator to \\(\\chi_RU\\), differentiating first for \\(U=u\\). Removing \\(Lh+Qa\\) leaves compact forcing bounded by \\(C_{\\rm fix}\\mathcal N^2\\) in \\(L^2_{A_i}H^4\\), plus shell and startup terms. Changed coefficients carry field, parameter, or bounded-memory factors; exact freezing removes free position. After differentiation there is a strong factor or a product of two weak factors, using \\(2A_H&gt;A\\).</p><p>Commute time weights through memories and filters, costing \\(C_RT_0^{-1}\\) budgets, and insert\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{c_*}{c_*+\\partial_t}\\right)^{M_*}\n\\]\nabove the fixed testing loss. Shell integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\int\\chi_R'b^{rr}\n(v_*\\partial_rU_*-U_*\\partial_rv_*)r^2\\,dr\\,d\\omega.\n\\]\nReplacing both radial derivatives by \\(\\partial_r+\\zeta\\) leaves the Wronskian unchanged. Proposition 4 bounds it by a polynomial in \\(c_*\\), times \\(R^{4\\iota}\\), times the shell norm of\n\\[\n(1,\\Omega_{\\rm co},r(\\partial_r+\\partial_t))U.\n\\]\nThe latter is\n\\[\nCR^{-(p_i-A_i)/2}F\n+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2).\n\\]\nCo-moving/emitter differences contain small parameter coefficients, and bad derivatives occur only in products on the fixed shell.</p><p>Causality permits stopping forcing at \\(T\\) without terminal data. Duality gives the negative spatial norm. Removing the auxiliary filter costs \\(Cc_*^{-1}\\) adjacent time-jet budgets. Choose \\(c_*=R^{\\kappa'}\\) with\n\\[\n\\kappa'N'+4\\iota&lt;\\tfrac12\\min_i(p_i-A_i),\n\\]\nthen \\(T_0\\) large.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Frozen time-local energy — original Proposition 5.2.** For a nearby stationary pair \\((p,c)\\) and integer \\(s\\geq8\\), \\(L_{p,c}\\) has a positive time-local first-jet energy with endpoint orders \\(H^s\\oplus H^{s-1}\\).", "statement": "<p>**Frozen time-local energy — original Proposition 5.2.**</p><p>For a nearby stationary pair \\((p,c)\\) and integer \\(s\\geq8\\), \\(L_{p,c}\\) has a positive time-local first-jet energy with endpoint orders \\(H^s\\oplus H^{s-1}\\). On each fixed compact reach its favorable derivative controls\n\\[\n\\|Y\\|_{H^{s-3/2}}^2+\n\\|B_{\\rm nt}Y\\|_{H^{s-1}}^2\n-C_s\\|Y\\|_{H^{s-2}(K')}^2,\n\\quad\nY=(\\langle D_y\\rangle U,D_tU).\n\\]\nIt gives full mixed order outside physical trapping. Near the boundaries it is a literal differential wave current with strict bulk sign and exiting inner flux. Remaining compact derivative errors have total field order at most \\(s-1\\), and compact additions have strictly interior kernels.</p>", "proof": "<p>Solve the order-\\(-1\\) acceleration correction and diagonalize the two scalar real principal root blocks, dividing only by their gap. The positive principal energy on polarizations is a Hermitian form times wave action.</p><p>Near trapping use the compatible metric multiplied by\n\\[\n|k(\\partial_t)|^{2s-1}\nb_C,\\qquad\nb_C=e^{-C(\\phi_+^2-\\phi_-^2)}.\n\\]\nIts principal derivative has sign\n\\(-2C\\lambda(\\phi_+^2+\\phi_-^2)\\). At the critical next order, take the next symbol to be \\(b_C\\) times a \\(C\\)-independent symbol. Frame changes add terms linear in \\(db_C\\); on trapping,\n\\[\ndb_C=0,\\qquad H_{p_{\\rm hw}}(db_C)=0.\n\\]\nThus critical derivative errors there are \\(C\\)-independent.</p><p>For self-adjoint\n\\[\nG_\\pm=\\operatorname{Op}^w(g\\phi_\\pm),\n\\]\nuse\n\\[\n\\|G_+z\\|^2+\\|G_-z\\|^2\n\\geq\\pm\\langle i[G_+,G_-]z,z\\rangle.\n\\]\nThe nonzero bracket supplies positive order \\(2s-3\\), multiplied by \\(C\\), dominating the critical errors. Retain some squares, fix \\(C\\), shrink the neighborhood, and use Gårding modulo order \\(2s-4\\).</p><p>At the horizon use wave action times \\(b_1^{2s-1}\\) and a scaled null-flag metric. Rank-two amplitude growth is at most \\(2\\kappa_++\\varepsilon'\\), whereas the squared frequency damping has margin\n\\[\n(2s-1)\\kappa_+&gt;4\\kappa_+.\n\\]\nThe additions miss the horizon. Extend the strict signs along finitely many escape flow tubes, allowing endpoints in already controlled collars.</p><p>Far out use component wave currents for\n\\[\n\\chi_\\mu=\\widetilde r\\,\\partial_y^\\mu U,\\qquad\nN=\\partial_{\\widetilde t}\n+(f_\\infty-\\widetilde r^{-\\delta})\\partial_{\\widetilde r},\n\\quad 0.9&lt;f_\\infty&lt;1.\n\\]\nTheir deformation controls\n\\[\nf'|\\partial_{\\widetilde t,\\widetilde r}\\chi_\\mu|^2\n+(2f/\\widetilde r-f')\\widetilde r^{-2}\n|\\partial_\\omega\\chi_\\mu|^2,\n\\]\nwith Hardy controlling lower potentials. Match compact forms to these literal currents by invariant-impact cutoffs, favorable incoming/outgoing scaling, and monotone interpolation. Near inner outflow use an increasing radial exponential weight.</p><p>Strict principal sign away from both projected traps permits subtraction of a small \\(B_{\\rm nt}\\) square at full order. Scalar cutoff commutators cost one order. Parameter differentiation contributes at most\n\\[\nC_R(|a|+|\\dot c|)\n\\]\ntimes endpoint energy. No nonlocal boundary trace sign is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Sliced stationary estimates — original Proposition 5.3.** On fixed enlarged compact reaches, \\( S_a\\leq C(R_0)K_R+CF +C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2), \\) where \\(C\\) is independent of \\(R_0\\), and …", "statement": "<p>**Sliced stationary estimates — original Proposition 5.3.**</p><p>On fixed enlarged compact reaches,\n\\[\nS_a\\leq C(R_0)K_R+CF\n+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2),\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) is independent of \\(R_0\\), and\n\\[\n\\|g_{\\rm co}-G_{p,c}\\|_{L^2_{A_S}H^8_{\\rm loc}}\n\\leq C_{\\rm loc}(K_R+F+S_a)\n+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2).\n\\]\nThe latter includes mixed jets of total order eight and one additional time derivative at the same mixed order on fixed physically nontrapped sets.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\chi_t\\) supported in \\(r\\leq2c_1t\\), equal to one for \\(r\\leq c_1t\\), so its support is timelike. The sliced equation is\n\\[\nL_p(0)(\\chi_th_{\\rm co})\n=\n\\chi_t(R_h-f_B)+[L_p(0),\\chi_t]h_{\\rm co}.\n\\]\nOn fixed reaches the source has order six globally and seven after \\(B_{\\rm nt}\\), and\n\\[\n\\|R_h\\|_{L_A^2H^4}\n+\\|f_B-Q(0)a\\|_{L_A^2H^4}\n\\leq C_{\\rm loc}K_R+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2).\n\\]\nThe limiting source factors are \\(\\partial_y^2h,\\partial_yu,\\partial_tu\\): globally \\(H^{6.5}\\), directionally \\(H^7\\), multiplied by undifferentiated endpoint-\\(H^9\\) coefficients. The scalar cutoff estimate\n\\[\n\\|[B_{\\rm nt},f]v\\|_{H^7}\n\\leq C\\|f\\|_{H^9}\\|v\\|_{H^{6.5}}\n\\]\nplaces the cutoff on the limiting factor. Its Fourier multiplier bound is\n\\[\n\\langle\\eta+\\ell\\rangle^7\\langle\\eta\\rangle^{-6.5}\n\\min(1,|\\ell|/\\langle\\eta\\rangle)\n\\leq C\\langle\\ell\\rangle^7.\n\\]</p><p>At infinity, with\n\\[\n\\Delta_a\\leq\nC\\int_{t-Cr}^t|a|\n\\lesssim S_at^{-A/2}r^{1/2},\n\\]\nthe sliced defect consists of leading constant combinations of\n\\(T_l\\psi,\\partial_z\\psi\\), and\n\\[\n(r^{-1}+\\Delta_a)|\\partial\\psi|+r^{-2}|\\psi|\n+\\Delta_a(|\\partial^2h|+r^{-1}|\\partial h|+r^{-2}|h|)\n\\]\n\\[\n+|h||\\partial^2h|+|\\partial h|^2\n+r^{-1}|h||\\partial h|+r^{-2}|h|^2.\n\\]\nPair with the adjoint kernel. The \\(Q(0)a\\) coupling is full rank. Split its target-defect comparison at a radius \\(Y\\) fixed before \\(R_0\\); the tail costs \\(CY^{-1+\\varepsilon}S_a\\) and is absorbed. A single spatial integration by parts on leading far terms reduces to\n\\[\n\\|r^{\\zeta_1}t^{A/2}(rX\\psi,\\psi)\\|_{L^2(dt\\,dr\\,d\\omega/r)}\n\\leq CF,\n\\quad\n0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;\\zeta_1&lt;0.13.\n\\]\nThe gap \\(p_P-A=0.26\\) makes \\(C\\) independent of \\(R_0\\).</p><p>For the static field use growth power \\(\\gamma=0.96\\), the seven decaying and ten constant-mode measurements. On compact sets an order-\\(-2\\) parametrix \\(Q_p\\) on the trapping cone gives\n\\[\nL_p(0)Q_p\\Psi_{\\rm tr}=\\Psi_{\\rm tr}+R_p.\n\\]\nSubtract \\(Q_p\\Psi_{\\rm tr}f\\in H^8\\); the remaining source is\n\\(B_{\\rm nt}f-R_pf\\in H^7\\), so the one-order stationary estimate gives \\(H^8\\). Switch to weighted order-zero far sources across a fixed full elliptic annulus.</p><p>Replacing \\(G_p+H^C(p)c\\) by \\(G_{p,c}\\) costs quadratic \\(c\\)-terms; replacing \\(B\\) by \\(G_p\\) costs acceleration memories. Mixed time jets come directly from \\(u\\), not differentiated parametrices. The ninth parameter-projection jet is used only in bulk at physically nontrapped probes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Actual-principal comparison — original Proposition 5.4.** At each compact level …", "statement": "<p>**Actual-principal comparison — original Proposition 5.4.**</p><p>At each compact level,\n\\[\nL_{p(t),c(t)}U+d_M^{\\alpha\\beta}\\partial_{\\alpha\\beta}U\n=S_i^{\\rm lin}+S_i^{\\rm err},\n\\qquad\nS_i^{\\rm lin}=\\pm Q(\\partial_t)b_i,\n\\quad b_i=a,a,a',\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nS_i^{\\rm err}\\in\nL^1_{A_i}H^{s-1}+L^2_{A_i}H^{s-1/2},\n\\qquad\n\\|S_i^{\\rm err}\\|\n\\leq C_{\\rm fix}(\\mathcal N^2+e_{\\rm in}).\n\\]\nOn larger differential annuli \\(L^2_{A_i}H^{s-1}\\) suffices.</p><p>The frozen energy has an actual-principal correction whose extra integrated derivative errors are \\(C_{\\rm fix}\\mathcal N^3\\), up to input terms, in squared estimates. Frozen half-loss dissipation and directional full-order control remain on the left.</p>", "proof": "<p>The sliced estimate, \\(A_S&gt;1\\), makes low mixed coefficient differences integrable in time. Acceleration, \\(\\dot c\\), and true-minus-frozen reference derivatives are likewise integrable. No integrable high norm is asserted.</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\xi=G_{p,c}-G_p,\\qquad\nk_h=h_{\\rm co}-\\xi,\\qquad X_0=B+\\xi.\n\\]\nExpand the reduced expression at the common point \\(X_0\\):\n\\[\nD_{\\rm red}'(X_0)h_{\\rm co}\n+\\operatorname{Rem}_{X_0}(k_h)\n-\\operatorname{Rem}_{X_0}(-\\xi).\n\\]\nThis avoids a high first jet multiplied only by one slowly decaying \\(\\xi\\). The gauge residual at freezing is \\(O(c^2)\\); away from freezing it also contains integrable acceleration factors.</p><p>Retain the differential second-order change in \\(d_M\\partial^2U\\). Remaining first-jet products use integrable low \\(k_h\\), endpoints, and half-loss bulk; smooth \\(\\xi^2\\)-terms use \\(2A_H&gt;A\\). After differentiation, \\(u\\partial^2h\\) uses unweighted \\(H^{7.5}\\) bulk for \\(\\partial^2h\\) and a strong low endpoint. Nonlocal remaining second-order terms have a spatial gain; for example\n\\[\nK_{-1}(d_1\\partial^2u)\n\\]\nmaps an \\(L_A^1H^6\\) placement to \\(L_A^1H^7\\).</p><p>The separate linear source is controlled by filtered low measurements. Through the differential end,\n\\[\n\\left(\\int^{O(R)}\n\\widetilde r^{1+\\delta}\n\\|\\widetilde r\\,\\partial^{\\leq s-1}Qb_i\\|_{L_{A_i}^2L_\\omega^2}^2\n\\,d\\widetilde r\\right)^{1/2}\n\\lesssim CR^{\\delta/2}l+C_{\\rm fix}e_{\\rm in}.\n\\]</p><p>Use actual differential currents. On compact buffered supports, after first-jet normalization and paraproduct reduction, the actual principal symbol is\n\\[\np_d=S_d\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_+I,\\lambda_-I)S_d^{-1}.\n\\]\nFor stationary diagonal energy forms \\(e_\\pm\\), replace their principal addition by\n\\[\nA_d=S_d^{-*}\\operatorname{diag}(e_+,e_-)S_d^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe exact algebraic identity\n\\[\nA_dp_d=p_d^*A_d\n\\]\ncancels the order-one energy error. One-Lipschitz-derivative composition and adjoint calculus leave an order-zero quadratic error bounded by\n\\[\nC_s\\bigl(\n\\|d_M\\|_{W^{1,\\infty}}+\n\\|\\partial_td_M\\|_\\infty+|a|+|\\dot c|\n\\bigr)\\|z\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nIts coefficient is integrable by the sliced estimate, giving the cubic integrated cost. Smallness preserves positivity; all kernels stay away from outflow traces.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Far-region estimate and radius-uniform coupling — original Proposition 6.3 and Lemma B.2.** On a smooth finite bootstrap slab, use laboratory emitter coordinates …", "statement": "<p>**Far-region estimate and radius-uniform coupling — original Proposition 6.3 and Lemma B.2.**</p><p>On a smooth finite bootstrap slab, use laboratory emitter coordinates\n\\[\nt=q+r,\\qquad z=C_e(q)+rn,\\qquad\nC_e=-\\lambda,\\quad s_e=C_e',\\quad k=1-s_e\\cdot n,\n\\]\nwith small speed. Set\n\\[\nT_l=\\partial_{t,\\rm lab},\\quad\nX=T_l+n\\cdot\\partial_z,\\quad\nZ=(rX,\\Omega),\\quad\nD=T_l+s_s\\cdot\\partial_z,\\quad s_s=-v_s.\n\\]\nAll tensor components are laboratory components. Let \\(h=h_{\\rm lab}\\), \\(\\psi=Dh\\), and \\(H^{ij}=g^{ij}-\\eta^{ij}\\).</p><p>The levels are\n\\[\n(U_i,N_i,A_i,p_i)\n=\n(h,9,0,0.64),\\\n(h,8,A_H,1-\\eta_*/6),\\\n(\\psi,7,A,1.62),\n\\]\nwhere \\(A_H=1-\\eta_*\\), \\(A=1.36\\), \\(0&lt;\\eta_*\\leq10^{-8}\\). Put\n\\[\n\\delta_f=\\eta_*/100,\\quad \\nu=10\\delta_f,\\quad\ne=0.1\\delta_f,\\quad d_*=200\\eta_*,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mu=\\langle q/r^{\\delta_f}\\rangle,\\quad\nl_f=\\langle q/r^\\nu\\rangle,\\quad\nm_f=(r^{-1}+l_f^{-1})^{-1},\n\\]\n\\[\nW_i=r^{p_i-A_i}(t+10r)^{A_i},\n\\]\n\\[\nw_H=r^{-e}\\mu^{A_H},\\quad\nw_{P,j}=w_Hl_f^{A-A_H}m_f^{-(7-j)d_*},\n\\]\n\\[\nw_{0,j}=r^{-0.02}m_-^{jd_*}m_+^{-(9-j)d_*},\n\\]\nwhere \\(m_-\\) and \\(m_+\\) are the paper’s smooth negative- and positive-\\(q/r^\\nu\\) weights, the latter capped at \\(r\\).</p><p>For \\(\\chi=Z^I(rT_l^jU_i)\\), \\(|I|+j\\leq N_i\\), the far budget \\(F\\) consists of the sums of\n\\[\n\\|W_i^{1/2}(r^{-1}Z\\chi,\\chi/r)\\|_{L^2(dt\\,dr\\,d\\omega/r)},\n\\quad\n\\|w_{i,j}^{1/2}B_1\\chi\\|_{L^2(dt\\,dr\\,d\\omega/r)},\n\\]\ntheir time-slice endpoint norms in \\(L^2(dr\\,d\\omega)\\), and the corrected outgoing bulk and cap norms specified below. Here \\(B_1\\chi\\) denotes the full first derivative.</p><p>After fixed finite hierarchy weighting,\n\\[\nF\\leq o_{R_0}(1)(F+S_a)+C(R_0)K_R+\nC_{\\rm fix}\\bigl(\\text{input errors}+\\mathcal N^{1+c_1}\\bigr)\n\\]\nfor some \\(c_1&gt;0\\), independently of the upper matching radius \\(R\\).</p><p>The coefficient of \\(F\\) in the acceleration estimate of Proposition 5 is independent of \\(R_0\\). The target-defect pairing can be split at a radius \\(Y\\) fixed before \\(R_0\\), with compact cost \\(C(Y)K_R\\) and tail cost \\(CY^{-1+\\varepsilon}S_a\\). Conversely the pure far defect, including the starting annulus, has an \\(o_{R_0}(1)\\) coefficient on \\(S_a\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 6.1 verifies the laboratory equation and differentiable weak-null structure, and Lemma 6.2 verifies initial and overlap inputs. Lemmas 6.3–6.7 supply all coefficient point bounds as consequences of the finite-slab bootstrap, not of an already global solution.</p><p>Apply Lemma 6.20 to the two equations and then the energy reduction of Lemma 6.13. Fix hierarchy weights before increasing \\(R_0\\). This proves the far estimate. Lemma 6.21 proves the radius-uniform acceleration coupling and compatible defect split.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Far symbol and weak-null structure — original Lemma 6.1.** In the setting of Proposition 6, \\( B-\\eta=O(r^{-1}),\\quad DB=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a), \\quad \\partial^ds_s=O(r^{1-d}\\widetilde a),\\ d\\geq1, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Far symbol and weak-null structure — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 6,\n\\[\nB-\\eta=O(r^{-1}),\\quad DB=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a),\n\\quad \\partial^ds_s=O(r^{1-d}\\widetilde a),\\ d\\geq1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Theta=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a),\\quad\nf_B=O(r^{-2}\\widetilde a),\\quad\nDf_B=O(r^{-3}\\widetilde a),\\quad\nr\\widetilde a\\lesssim\\mathcal N(1+t/r)^{-A/2}.\n\\]\nThe laboratory equation and gauge are\n\\[\ng^{ij}\\partial_{ij}h\n=\nf_B+O(r^{-2}\\partial h+r^{-3}h)\n+Q_\\eta(\\partial h,\\partial h)\n+O((r^{-1}+|h|)|\\partial h|^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{flat linear gauge}(h)\n=\nO((r^{-1}+|h|)\\partial h)+O(r^{-2}h)+O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell_n=(1,-n)\\), if\n\\[\n\\ell_n^\\alpha p_{\\alpha\\beta}\n-\\tfrac12\\ell_{n,\\beta}\\operatorname{tr}_\\eta p=0,\n\\]\nthen the constant flat quadratic form on \\(\\ell_n\\otimes p\\) is a multiple of\n\\[\n\\ell_n\\otimes\\ell_n\\,|\\Pi(n)p|^2.\n\\]\nThe polarized and differentiated identities hold with the same good-derivative, vanishing-coefficient, symbol, and acceleration error placements.</p>", "proof": "<p>The target is \\(\\eta+h(p_s,x)\\) after exact affine cancellation, with\n\\(\\partial_p^b\\partial_x^\\alpha h=O(r^{-1-|\\alpha|})\\).\nImplicit differentiation gives \\(Dx=0\\), and Proposition 4 gives the displayed symbols and differentiated defects.</p><p>Constraint propagation yields\n\\[\n\\Gamma_\\mu(g)=g_{\\mu\\sigma}g^{ab}\\Gamma(B)^\\sigma_{ab}+\\Theta_\\mu.\n\\]\nExpand this identity and vacuum Ricci. The nonlocal additions vanish here.</p><p>For the flat polarization, use a null frame. The gauge eliminates \\(p_{LL},p_{LA}\\), and screen trace. Equivalently, with \\(n=(1,0,0)\\), the constrained tensor has the form\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\na&amp;b&amp;c&amp;d\\\\\nb&amp;-a-2b&amp;-c&amp;-d\\\\\nc&amp;-c&amp;e&amp;f\\\\\nd&amp;-d&amp;f&amp;-e\n\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\tfrac18(\\operatorname{tr}_\\eta p)^2\n-\\tfrac14\\operatorname{tr}((\\eta^{-1}p)^2)\n=-\\tfrac14|\\Pi p|^2.\n\\]\nThe gauge contraction map is surjective on \\(\\ker\\Pi\\), so a smooth right inverse projects onto constrained tensors without changing \\(\\Pi p\\). Differentiate in laboratory coordinates before projecting; each error has a good derivative, a vanishing coefficient, an inverse-radius field, or an acceleration average.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Initial and overlap inputs — original Lemma 6.2.** The initial, current-correction, and field overlap costs on the fixed-factor starting annulus are bounded by \\( C(R_0)K_R+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2). \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Initial and overlap inputs — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><p>The initial, current-correction, and field overlap costs on the fixed-factor starting annulus are bounded by\n\\[\nC(R_0)K_R+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^2).\n\\]\nOnly metric \\(H^{10}\\), second-form \\(H^9\\), and their order-\\(-1,-2\\) initial symbol bounds are required. No asymptotic coefficients or higher-order smallness are imposed. Pure acceleration forcing is kept separate and estimated directly by averaging.</p>", "proof": "<p>Startup parameters and far kernel terms vanish. The wave equation supplies mixed initial jets through order ten with an inverse radius per derivative. Initial weighted integrals converge because \\(p_0,p_H&lt;1\\), while \\(\\psi\\) has an extra inverse power.</p><p>The starting overlap is physically nontrapped and has full mixed compact control through \\(N_i+1\\). The co/laboratory transformation gives \\(\\psi=u\\) plus terms of the forms\n\\[\n(v(t)-v_s)\\partial h_{\\rm co},\\qquad v'h_{\\rm co}.\n\\]\nTheir coefficients have strong weighted bounded-memory controls, so they are products, not growing linear coefficients on \\(S_a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Spacelike anchors — original Lemma A.1.** For \\(r\\asymp R_f\\), \\(q\\leq-R_f^{0.45}/10\\), the emitter center is zero.", "statement": "<p>**Spacelike anchors — original Lemma A.1.**</p><p>For \\(r\\asymp R_f\\), \\(q\\leq-R_f^{0.45}/10\\), the emitter center is zero. On \\(-q\\asymp Q\\),\n\\[\n\\|Z^I(rT_l^jh)\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\lesssim(e_{\\rm in}+S_a)(r/Q)^{1/2},\n\\qquad |I|+j\\leq9.\n\\]\nThe ordinary unweighted spatial energy of Minkowski commutations through nine on such a band is\n\\[\n\\lesssim(e_{\\rm in}+S_a)Q^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nThe constant on \\(S_a\\) is uniform far out.</p>", "proof": "<p>Work up to time \\(O(R_f)\\) outside\n\\[\n|z|\\geq t+cQ+\nC_1\\int_0^t((u+Q)Q)^{-1/2}\\,du.\n\\]\nThe extra displacement is \\(o(Q)\\), because \\(Q\\gtrsim R_f^{0.45}\\). Bootstrap unweighted energies after rotations, boosts, translations, and dilation through order nine. Initial first jets have squared tail integral \\(O(Q^{-1})\\). Radial integration gives commuted field and sphere-supremum bounds.</p><p>The moving boundary outruns outgoing speeds. Commuted principal errors are \\(Z^bH\\,\\partial^2Z^dh\\), \\(b+d\\leq9,d&lt;9\\). High factors use sphere \\(L^2\\) and two spare rotations on low factors; one derivative costs \\(Q^{-1}\\) through Minkowski commutations. Time-integrated losses are \\(o(1)\\), including \\(O(R_f^{1/2}Q^{-3/2})\\). Pure defects cost \\(CS_a|z|^{-3}\\) and \\(CS_aQ^{-1/2}\\) in \\(L_t^1L_z^2\\). Close the domain energy bootstrap. The emitter commutations expand with bounded coefficients in the Minkowski list.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Radial point estimates — original Lemma A.2.** With \\(\\tau_f=1+t/r\\), \\( \\|\\chi(h)\\|_{L_\\omega^2} \\lesssim\\mathcal N r^{2\\eta_*}\\tau_f^{-A_H/2} \\quad\\text{through count }8, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Radial point estimates — original Lemma A.2.**</p><p>With \\(\\tau_f=1+t/r\\),\n\\[\n\\|\\chi(h)\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\lesssim\\mathcal N r^{2\\eta_*}\\tau_f^{-A_H/2}\n\\quad\\text{through count }8,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|\\chi(h)\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\\lesssim\\mathcal N r^{0.52}\n\\quad\\text{through count }9,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|B_1\\chi(h)\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n+\\|\\chi(\\psi)\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\lesssim\\mathcal N r^{e/2}\\mu^{-A_H/2}\n\\quad\\text{through count }7.\n\\]\nFirst-derivative versions for \\(\\psi\\) hold one order lower; two spare rotations give sphere supremum versions.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(t\\lesssim r\\), integrate radially to a comparable spacelike anchor and use Lemma 6.3. For \\(t\\gg r\\), integrate inward to a weighted compact endpoint anchor. Use the endpoint bad weights and a radial unit trace at the next order.</p><p>In that trace,\n\\[\n\\partial_q(rT_l^jh)\n=krT_l^{j+1}h+s_e^db_dZT_l^jh,\n\\]\nso a second \\(q\\)-derivative uses first derivatives of next and lower fields, inverse-radius fields, and bounded acceleration factors. Finally\n\\[\nD=(1-s_s\\cdot n)T_l+r^{-1}s_s^db_dZ\n\\]\ngives the \\(\\psi\\) bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Differentiated null projection — original Lemma A.3.** Contract \\(T_l^jH\\) with \\(\\ell_n\\ell_n\\) before applying \\(Z\\).", "statement": "<p>**Differentiated null projection — original Lemma A.3.**</p><p>Contract \\(T_l^jH\\) with \\(\\ell_n\\ell_n\\) before applying \\(Z\\). Then\n\\[\n\\|Z^IH_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\lesssim r^{-1}+\\mathcal N r^{-1+2\\eta_*}\\tau_f^{-A_H/2},\n\\quad |I|\\leq8,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\|Z^I(T_l^jH)_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\lesssim r^{-2}+\\mathcal N r^{-2+8\\eta_*}\\tau_f^{-A_H/2}\n\\]\nfor \\(j\\geq1\\), \\(|I|+j\\leq8\\). The same second bound holds after \\(\\partial_q\\) through total count seven.</p>", "proof": "<p>The first estimate is smooth inversion of \\(B+h\\) and Lemma 6.4. For the others, solve the differentiated flat gauge for the bad derivative of the \\(LL\\), \\(L\\)-screen, and screen-trace components. Good derivatives cost \\(r^{-2+2\\eta_*}\\tau_f^{-A_H/2}\\). Products \\(H\\partial h\\), quadratic inverse errors, and \\(\\Theta=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a)\\) have the required placement. Laboratory differentiations precede projection.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tight cone projection and top order — original Lemma A.4.** Let \\(b_q=1+\\min(|q|,r)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Tight cone projection and top order — original Lemma A.4.**</p><p>Let \\(b_q=1+\\min(|q|,r)\\). Then\n\\[\nr\\|Z^IH_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\\lesssim1,\n\\quad |I|\\leq8,\\quad |q|\\leq10r^{0.45},\n\\]\n\\[\nr\\|Z^I(T_l^jH)_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\lesssim r^{-0.46},\n\\quad j\\geq1,\\quad |I|+j\\leq9,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nr\\|Z^9H_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\n\\lesssim\n\\log r+b_q^{1/2}r^{-p_0/2}+b_qr^{-1+8\\eta_*}.\n\\]\nThe relevant linear constants are independent of \\(R_0\\), after nonlinear smallness is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>The differentiated estimate follows from the preceding gauge argument with the rough ninth-order field bound. For undifferentiated projections integrate the constrained equation for \\(Z^d(rh_{LL})\\) from negative \\(q\\) at fixed \\(t,n\\). Its sources are good derivatives, field-over-radius terms, coefficient-suppressed full jets, and \\(O(\\widetilde a)\\).</p><p>On \\(-q\\asymp Q\\gtrsim r^{0.45}\\), Lemma 6.3 makes good terms summable through eight; at nine the first-jet energy costs \\(O(\\mathcal N)\\) per band, giving a logarithm. Near the cone, slice Cauchy–Schwarz gives \\(r^{(0.45-p_i)/2}\\); ninth-order undifferentiated terms use a good derivative of an eighth-order field. Extending to length \\(b_q\\) gives the two additional displayed terms. For \\(t\\gg r\\), begin with a comparable-radius local average and integrate over length \\(O(r)\\). Quadratic inversion errors are smaller.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform news — original Lemma A.5.** The lowest radiative laboratory jets satisfy \\( |r\\Pi T_lh|+|r\\Pi T_l^2h|+|r\\Pi T_l\\psi| \\lesssim\\mathcal N. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Uniform news — original Lemma A.5.**</p><p>The lowest radiative laboratory jets satisfy\n\\[\n|r\\Pi T_lh|+|r\\Pi T_l^2h|+|r\\Pi T_l\\psi|\n\\lesssim\\mathcal N.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\chi_j=rT_l^jh\\), \\(j=0,1\\), and \\(Y_j=\\Pi k^{-1}\\partial_q\\chi_j\\). Projection of the two leading terms is\n\\[\n(-2k\\partial_r+H_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\partial_q)Y_j.\n\\]\nEvery remaining term is \\(O(\\mathcal N r^{-2+0.04})\\). Angular terms gain \\(r^{-2}\\); mixed principal terms gain \\(r^{-1}\\); acceleration terms use \\(r|a(q)H_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}|\\lesssim1\\). For \\(j=1\\), the two-bad coefficient \\(T_lH_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\) has the improved inverse-radius bound.</p><p>Project the flat quadratic after replacing each polarization by its constrained projection. The constrained–constrained term is annihilated by \\(\\Pi\\). Every remainder contains a gauge or good-derivative error \\(O(\\mathcal N r^{-2+2\\varepsilon})\\), paired with a full jet. Thus\n\\[\n|(-2k\\partial_r+H_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\partial_q)Y_j|\n\\lesssim\\mathcal N r^{-2+0.04}.\n\\]\nIts backward integral curve reaches \\(t=0\\) or the fixed starting region, and the radial source is integrable. Convert back to \\(T_lh,T_l^2h\\), then differentiate the expression for \\(Dh\\) once in laboratory time.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Corrected outgoing divergence — original Claim 1 in the proof of Proposition A.6.** Let \\(X_g,\\bar X_g\\) be the true outgoing and incoming future null normals to emitter spheres, normalized by \\(dt=1\\), and …", "statement": "<p>**Corrected outgoing divergence — original Claim 1 in the proof of Proposition A.6.**</p><p>Let \\(X_g,\\bar X_g\\) be the true outgoing and incoming future null normals to emitter spheres, normalized by \\(dt=1\\), and \\(G=-g(X_g,\\bar X_g)\\). Write\n\\[\n\\partial_q=\\alpha\\bar X_g+\\text{good directions},\n\\qquad\n\\vartheta=-g(\\bar X_g,\\cdot)/G,\n\\qquad\n\\xi=d\\chi+\\frac{G\\alpha}{2k}P\\,\\vartheta.\n\\]\nFor a source term \\(\\partial_qP\\), this correction cancels its incoming derivative in the outgoing multiplier identity. With\n\\[\nM_f=r^{0.72}\\mu^{-A_H}j_0(q/r),\n\\]\nthe outgoing current controls\n\\[\n\\frac{W_i}{r}\n\\left((1+M_f)|\\xi_{X_g}|^2+|\\xi_{\\rm scr}|^2\\right)\n\\]\nand the corrected outgoing cap flux. Its correction costs are\n\\[\n\\|W_i^{1/2}(P,ZP,rd_g\\partial_qP)\\|_{\\rm bulk}\n+\\sup_t\\|W_i^{1/2}P\\|_{L^2(dr\\,d\\omega)},\n\\]\nwhere \\(d_g=|H_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}|+|H|^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use density \\(r^{-2}d\\operatorname{vol}_g\\). For \\(F_c=(G\\alpha/2k)P\\), the derivative-\\(F_c\\) contributions from divergence and curl sum to\n\\[\n-2G^{-1}\\xi_{X_g}\\bar X_gF_c\n+\\nabla_{\\rm scr}F_c\\cdot\\xi_{\\rm scr}.\n\\]\nThe potentially bad \\(\\xi_{\\bar X_g}\\) terms cancel exactly. Derivatives of \\(\\vartheta\\) also contain no remaining incoming component.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\nK_f=1+r^{-1}\\int_q^\\infty M_f(q',r)\\,dq',\n\\]\nso \\(K_f=1+o(1)\\), \\(r|X_gK_f|=o(1)\\), and\n\\(-r\\bar X_gK_f\\gtrsim M_f-o(1)\\). The multiplier \\(W_iK_fX_g\\) has positive deformation because \\(p_i&gt;A_i\\) and \\(p_i+0.1A_i&lt;2\\). Improved derivatives of \\(g_{LL}\\) make bad–good errors absorbable using \\(W_i/w_{i,j}\\ll r^{1.7}\\).</p><p>Spacelike caps are\n\\[\nu=q+\\int_{R_0/2}^r\n\\left[\\rho^{\\delta_f-1}\n+\\rho^{20\\eta_*-1}j(|q|/\\rho^{0.45})\\right]d\\rho.\n\\]\nThey satisfy \\(X_gu&gt;0,\\bar X_gu&gt;c&gt;0\\). Coarea gives cap control of \\(W_i|\\xi_{X_g}|^2\\,dr\\,d\\omega\\), without requiring a uniform lower bound on \\(X_gu\\).</p><p>A scalar Hardy current for \\(W_iX_g|\\chi|^2/r^2\\) controls \\(\\chi/r\\). Finally\n\\[\n|r^{-1}Z\\chi|\n\\leq C(|\\xi_{X_g}|+|\\xi_{\\rm scr}|+|P|)\n+Cd_g|B_1\\chi|,\n\\]\nand \\(d_g^2W_i/w_{i,j}\\) has a negative radial power.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.9", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Laboratory-time multiplier — original Claim 2 in the proof of Proposition A.6.** The uncorrected multiplier \\(w_{i,j}T_l\\) controls full bad-derivative bulk and time-endpoint norms.", "statement": "<p>**Laboratory-time multiplier — original Claim 2 in the proof of Proposition A.6.**</p><p>The uncorrected multiplier \\(w_{i,j}T_l\\) controls full bad-derivative bulk and time-endpoint norms. Its only exceptional weak-weight region,\n\\[\n0\\leq q\\leq r^{0.3},\n\\]\nhas a summable radius-small cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>The two-bad coefficient from \\(\\mathcal L_{T_l}g\\) is improved by Lemma 6.5. Weight differentiation gives\n\\[\nrX_gw_{i,j}/w_{i,j}&lt;-e/2.\n\\]\nAdverse incoming derivatives of \\(w_{i,j}\\) are absorbed by outgoing good bulk except in the stated weak region.</p><p>On a shell \\(r\\asymp R_f\\), from \\(q=-R_f^{0.45}\\) to \\(q=R_f^{0.3}\\), the spacelike anchor and top endpoint give\n\\[\n\\|Z\\chi\\|_{L_\\omega^2}^2\n\\lesssim\n(e_{\\rm in}+S_a)^2R_f^{0.55}\n+F^2R_f^{0.471}.\n\\]\nThe exceptional shell power is\n\\[\n-1+0.3+\\max(0.55,0.471)=-0.15.\n\\]\nTrue-minus-flat good derivatives are absorbed with the extra \\(d_g^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.10", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Flat triangular commutators — original Claim 3 in the proof of Proposition A.6.** After commuting laboratory derivatives first and emitter derivatives afterwards, the flat recurrence contributes a bounded nonnegative …", "statement": "<p>**Flat triangular commutators — original Claim 3 in the proof of Proposition A.6.**</p><p>After commuting laboratory derivatives first and emitter derivatives afterwards, the flat recurrence contributes a bounded nonnegative multiple of \\(X\\chi/r\\); all other size-one flat terms are triangular in lower total order, then more laboratory times, then fewer dilations.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(S=r\\partial_r|_{q,n}\\),\n\\[\nP_\\eta S=(S+1)P_\\eta+r^{-2}A_s.\n\\]\nThe \\(S^2\\) coefficient contributes \\(+(1+s_e\\cdot n)X\\chi/r\\) for each dilation. Rotation commutators and differentiated \\(s_e\\)-coefficients are \\(O(s_e)\\). Remaining terms are good derivatives or field-over-radius of a prior order; a same-order \\(r^{-2}\\chi\\) term uses \\(r^{-1}\\chi=X\\chi_{\\rm prior}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.11", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Boundary costs — original Claim 4 in the proof of Proposition A.6.** Starting radial fluxes may be averaged over \\([R_0/2,R_0]\\) and estimated using the initial and physically nontrapped compact overlap controls.", "statement": "<p>**Boundary costs — original Claim 4 in the proof of Proposition A.6.**</p><p>Starting radial fluxes may be averaged over \\([R_0/2,R_0]\\) and estimated using the initial and physically nontrapped compact overlap controls. No outgoing cap supremum on that overlap is required. Pure acceleration forcing retains its direct radius-small estimate.</p>", "proof": "<p>The common principal source uses at most \\(N_i+1\\) physical derivatives of the field. Additional strong-equation terms use at most nine derivatives of \\(h\\), accompanied by acceleration or a strong field factor. Full mixed compact controls therefore estimate all field source and correction products on the averaging annulus.</p><p>Choose radial cutoffs by averaging the full annular time bounds. At infinity first use radial truncations on bounded time ranges; finite-time symbol decay and the strict initial tail powers remove boundary terms. Keep pure defects separate from compact field costs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.12", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite hierarchical absorption — original Claim 5 in the proof of Proposition A.6.** Suppose finitely many energy blocks \\(E_k\\), ordered by the prior relation above, satisfy …", "statement": "<p>**Finite hierarchical absorption — original Claim 5 in the proof of Proposition A.6.**</p><p>Suppose finitely many energy blocks \\(E_k\\), ordered by the prior relation above, satisfy\n\\[\nE_k^2\\leq\\varepsilon(R_0)\\sum_{\\ell=1}^JE_\\ell^2\n+C\\sum_{\\ell&lt;k}E_\\ell^2+B^2,\n\\]\nwith \\(C,J\\) independent of matching radii and \\(\\varepsilon(R_0)\\to0\\). Then fixed hierarchy weights, chosen before \\(R_0\\), absorb all prior-order terms and all sufficiently small uniform errors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose positive weights \\(a_k\\) decreasing so rapidly that\n\\[\nC\\sum_{k&gt;\\ell}a_k\\leq a_\\ell/4.\n\\]\nSum the inequalities with weights \\(a_k\\). Then enlarge \\(R_0\\) so\n\\[\n\\varepsilon(R_0)\\sum_ka_k\n\\leq\\tfrac14\\min_ka_k.\n\\]\nThe weighted norm is equivalent to the finite sum defining \\(F\\), with radius-independent equivalence constants.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.13", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Far energy reduction — original Proposition A.6.** Suppose each scalar commuted equation has \\( P_g\\chi=\\lambda_1X\\chi/r+f_{\\rm flat} +\\partial_qP+f_r+f_{\\rm mix}, \\qquad \\lambda_1\\geq0, \\) and the correction, raw …", "statement": "<p>**Far energy reduction — original Proposition A.6.**</p><p>Suppose each scalar commuted equation has\n\\[\nP_g\\chi=\\lambda_1X\\chi/r+f_{\\rm flat}\n+\\partial_qP+f_r+f_{\\rm mix},\n\\qquad \\lambda_1\\geq0,\n\\]\nand the correction, raw, remainder, and mixed budgets have bounds\n\\[\no_{R_0}(1)(F+S_a)+C\\sum_{\\rm prior}F_{\\rm prior}\n+C(R_0)K_R+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^{1+c}).\n\\]\nThe correction budgets are those in Lemma 6.8; the remainder and raw budgets are\n\\[\n\\|(W_i/(1+M_f))^{1/2}rf_r\\|_{\\rm bulk},\n\\qquad\n\\|w_{i,j}^{1/2}rf_{\\rm raw}\\|_{\\rm bulk}.\n\\]\nA mixed shell bound\n\\[\n\\|rf_{\\rm mix}\\|_{L_\\omega^2}\\leq l_{R_f}(u)T(t,r)\n\\]\nis charged by\n\\[\n\\sum_{R_f}R_f^{(p_i-1)/2}\n\\|l_{R_f}\\|_{L^2(du)}\n\\sup_t\\|T(t,\\cdot)\\|_{L_r^2}.\n\\]\nAssume corresponding initial and averaged starting-annulus correction bounds. Then the far estimate in Proposition 6 follows.</p>", "proof": "<p>Add the corrected outgoing estimate of Lemma 6.8 and the laboratory-time estimate of Lemma 6.9 with fixed coercive relative factors. Apply Hardy and true-to-flat conversion. Mixed sources are paired first on outgoing caps, then integrated in \\(u\\); Cauchy–Schwarz gives the displayed shell charge. Lemmas 6.10–6.11 identify the prior and boundary costs. Apply Lemma 6.12 and take separate endpoint suprema.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.14", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Common principal sources — original Claim 1 in the proof of Proposition B.1.** Every common principal commutator is estimated by the source budgets of Lemma 6.13.", "statement": "<p>**Common principal sources — original Claim 1 in the proof of Proposition B.1.**</p><p>Every common principal commutator is estimated by the source budgets of Lemma 6.13. Its two-bad term is\n\\[\nC_h\\partial_q(c_0\\partial_q\\chi_\\ell),\n\\qquad\nC_h=Z^d(T_l^mH)_{\\ell_n\\ell_n},\n\\]\nwith lower input count. For coefficient count at most five, or at every count at level \\(P\\), use correction\n\\[\nP=C_hc_0\\partial_q\\chi_\\ell.\n\\]\nHigher coefficient counts at the \\(h\\)-levels use direct or mixed-cap estimates.</p>", "proof": "<p>For an upper coefficient \\(J\\),\n\\[\nP[J]\\chi\n=\nJ_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}\\partial_q(k^{-1}\\partial_q\\chi)\n+\nO\\!\\left(\n\\frac Jr(1,Z)\\partial_q\\chi+\n\\frac J{r^2}(1,Z,Z^2)\\chi+\n\\frac{a(q)}rJ_{\\ell_n\\ell_n}(1,Z)\\chi\n\\right).\n\\]\nLaboratory commutation precedes this expansion, and \\(Z\\) differentiates the full projected contraction.</p><p>Use the lists\n\\[\nV^U_{bj}=|Z^{\\leq b}T_l^jU|,\\quad\nG^U_{bj}=V^U_{b+1,j},\\quad\nB^U_{bj}=G^U_{bj}+rV^U_{b,j+1}.\n\\]\nBad commutations satisfy\n\\[\n|\\partial_q(c_0\\partial_q\\chi)|\n\\lesssim B_{b,j+1}+r^{-1}B_{b+1,j}+|a(q)|B_{bj},\n\\]\nand \\(B^h_{b,j+1}\\) converts to \\(B^\\psi_{bj}\\) plus inverse-radius lower \\(h\\)-terms using \\(Dh=\\psi\\).</p><p>Put high coefficients in sphere \\(L^2\\) and transfer two rotations to low companions when their count is at least six. Lemmas 6.5–6.6 bound \\(rC_h\\), tightly near the cone and with extra decay when \\(m\\geq1\\). The raw budget follows from time-count weight transfer and the prior strong level.</p><p>For corrections, \\(\\partial_qC_h\\) gains an inverse radius. \\(ZP\\) uses at most the target count, and \\(rd_g\\partial_qP\\) is controlled by \\(rd_g\\lesssim r^{2\\eta_*}\\). At high coefficient counts and \\(m=0\\), timelike regions use strong bad bulk; near the cone use mixed caps. Counts six through eight have squared kernel integral \\(O(R_f^{0.013\\eta_*})\\), while\n\\[\n0.013\\eta_*&lt;1-p_H.\n\\]\nAt count nine only level \\(0\\) occurs. The additional\n\\((1+\\min(|q|,r))r^{-p_0}\\) kernel contribution has powers less than \\(-1/4\\) after its mixed shell factor. Logarithms and \\(r^{8\\eta_*}\\) leave summable margins.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.15", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Additional strong-equation sources — original Claim 2 in the proof of Proposition B.1.** The extra terms in …", "statement": "<p>**Additional strong-equation sources — original Claim 2 in the proof of Proposition B.1.**</p><p>The extra terms in\n\\[\ng^{ij}\\partial_{ij}\\psi\n=\nD f_h-(Dg^{ij})\\partial_{ij}h\n+2g^{ij}(\\partial_is_s^d)\\partial_{jd}h\n+g^{ij}(\\partial_{ij}s_s^d)\\partial_dh\n\\]\nsatisfy the source and correction budgets through seven target commutations, using \\(h\\)-bad indices through eight.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(Dg\\), ordered coefficient derivatives satisfy\n\\[\n|J|\\lesssim\\mathcal N r^{-1+8\\eta_*}\\tau_f^{-A_H/2},\n\\qquad\n|C|\\lesssim\\mathcal N r^{-2+8\\eta_*}\\tau_f^{-A_H/2}.\n\\]\nThe projected improvement follows from differentiated gauge applied to \\(h\\), without a stationary offset, and \\(DB=O(r^{-1}\\widetilde a)\\). If coefficient count is \\(c\\), the remaining input bad count is at most \\(8-c\\); high coefficients leave room for transferred rotations. The restrictive squared factor is\n\\[\n\\mathcal N^2r^{p_P-2+16\\eta_*+e}\\tau_f^{A-2A_H},\n\\]\nwith both exponents negative.</p><p>The \\(H\\,\\partial s_s\\) and \\(\\partial^2s_s\\) terms are directly suppressed. For the flat smooth-acceleration coefficient \\(Y=2\\eta^{ij}\\partial_is_s^d\\), use the same two-bad correction; \\(\\partial_qC\\) gains \\(r^{-1}\\). An explicit frame term\n\\[\nC(\\partial_qc_2)D_Z\\chi_\\ell/r\n\\]\nuses correction \\(Cc_2D_Z\\chi_\\ell/r\\). Its remainders and derivatives carry \\(\\widetilde a\\), and the weight comparisons absorb them.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.16", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Pure defects, linear terms, and suppressed products — original Claim 3 in the proof of Proposition B.1.** These sources satisfy the budgets of Lemma 6.13, with a radius-small coefficient on \\(S_a\\) for pure defects …", "statement": "<p>**Pure defects, linear terms, and suppressed products — original Claim 3 in the proof of Proposition B.1.**</p><p>These sources satisfy the budgets of Lemma 6.13, with a radius-small coefficient on \\(S_a\\) for pure defects, including the starting annulus.</p>", "proof": "<p>On \\(r\\asymp R_f\\), set\n\\[\nA_{R_f}(t)=\\frac{C_1}{R_f}\n\\int_{t-C_2R_f}^t|a(u)|\\,du.\n\\]\nSince \\(A&gt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\|A_{R_f}\\|_{L_t^2}\\leq CR_f^{-1/2}S_a,\n\\qquad\n\\|\\langle t\\rangle^{A/2}A_{R_f}\\|_{L^2(t\\geq2C_2R_f)}\n\\leq CS_a.\n\\]\nAfter \\(r^2\\) source scaling, defects are \\(\\widetilde a\\) for \\(h\\) and \\(r^{-1}\\widetilde a\\) for \\(\\psi\\). The squared early/late shell powers are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n0&amp;-0.36&amp;-0.72\\\\\nH&amp;-\\eta_*/6&amp;-0.36-\\eta_*/6\\\\\nP&amp;-1.38&amp;-1.74.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nTheir sums tend to zero with \\(R_0\\).</p><p>Linear \\(r^{-2}\\partial h+r^{-3}h\\) terms become \\(r^{-1}B^h\\), with strict weight gain. Applying \\(D\\) gives corresponding \\(\\psi\\)-terms or additional \\(\\widetilde a,\\psi\\) factors.</p><p>Suppressed quadratic terms have placements\n\\[\n|Z^bT_l^mH|\\,B^h_{b_1i_1}B^h_{b_2i_2},\n\\qquad b+m+b_1+i_1+b_2+i_2\\leq N_i.\n\\]\nUse \\(r^{-1+2\\eta_*}\\) on coefficients through eight and \\(r^{-0.48}\\) at nine, put a low bad factor in point control, and the high factor in bulk. Strong products use \\(A&lt;2A_H\\). The restrictive radial comparisons include\n\\[\nr^{p_P-2+6\\eta_*+2e}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nr^{p_0+0.021-0.96+e},\n\\]\nboth strictly decaying.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.17", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Leading weak-null source and strong correction — original Claim 4 in the proof of Proposition B.1.** For \\(F_i=r\\Pi T_l^{i+1}h\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Leading weak-null source and strong correction — original Claim 4 in the proof of Proposition B.1.**</p><p>For \\(F_i=r\\Pi T_l^{i+1}h\\),\n\\[\nT_l^jQ_\\eta\n=\nr^{-2}\\ell_n\\otimes\\ell_n\n\\sum_{i+l=j}C_{il}(F_i,F_l)\n+\\operatorname{Err}_j.\n\\]\nThe errors satisfy suppressed-product or good–bad estimates. At \\(h\\)-levels the leading term uses direct timelike or mixed-cap bounds. At level \\(P\\), its differentiated leading term uses the signed correction\n\\[\nP_*=\nZ^{c_3}\\!\\left[\n(1-s_s\\cdot n)r^{-1}\n\\ell_n\\otimes\\ell_n C_{il}(F_i,F_l)\n\\right].\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the constrained projection sequentially to each polarization, leaving the partner as a full jet. Gauge errors yield coefficient-suppressed products, \\(\\widetilde a B^h\\), or\n\\[\nG^h_{b_1i_1}B^h_{b_2i_2}.\n\\]\nIf the good factor is low, use its point decay; if high, use its outgoing bulk and a low bad point factor. The comparisons\n\\[\nw_H^{-1}\\lesssim1+M_f,\\qquad\nw_{i,j}w_H^{-1}\\lesssim W_i\n\\]\nsuffice. After \\(D\\), a hit on a good factor gains \\(r^{-1}\\) and one bad count; total count is at most eight.</p><p>For the leading \\(h\\)-term on \\(t\\lesssim r\\), mixed squared shell exponents are\n\\[\np_H-1+2e+\\delta_f&lt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\np_0-1+0.021+e+\\delta_fA_H+1-A_H&lt;0.\n\\]\nFor the strong equation,\n\\[\nkD=(1-s_s\\cdot n)\\partial_q+r^{-1}d'Z.\n\\]\nThe correction cancels the leading \\(q\\)-derivative. Its remainders have \\(\\widetilde a\\) or \\(r^{-1}\\), and all correction derivatives use total \\(B^h\\)-count at most eight. Time caps use high–low placement and the already controlled field terms.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.18", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Raw leading products on finite slabs — original Claim 5 in the proof of Proposition B.1.** The raw laboratory-time budgets of the leading products at all three levels are controlled on the entire finite slab …", "statement": "<p>**Raw leading products on finite slabs — original Claim 5 in the proof of Proposition B.1.**</p><p>The raw laboratory-time budgets of the leading products at all three levels are controlled on the entire finite slab, including a terminal time cutting through near-cone tiles.</p>", "proof": "<p>Away from \\(|q|\\leq r^\\nu\\), ordinary high–low placement works because the low point factor offsets time-count weight ratios:\n\\[\ne-(\\nu-\\delta_f)A_H&lt;0,\\quad\n9d_*-A_H&lt;0,\\quad A-2A_H&lt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Near the cone replace \\(F_i\\) by \\(T_l^iF_0\\), and the strong counterpart by \\(T_l^l\\widehat F_0\\). Replacement errors gain \\(r^{-1}\\). Tile \\((q,\\varrho,n)\\), \\(\\varrho=\\log r\\), in fixed small boxes. Since\n\\[\ndt\\,dr/r=dq\\,d\\varrho,\n\\]\nthe measure is uniform; operators and weights are uniformly comparable on enlarged boxes. Through the target count, ordinary derivatives of \\(F_0,\\widehat F_0\\) are controlled by the corresponding bad lists.</p><p>The uniform news bound supplies the undifferentiated supremum. For \\(a+b=m&gt;0\\), local Gagliardo–Nirenberg and Hölder give\n\\[\n\\|\\partial^af\\,\\partial^bg\\|_2\n\\leq C\\mathcal N(\\|f\\|_{H^m}+\\|g\\|_{H^m}).\n\\]\nSquare and sum with bounded overlap. If a terminal slice intersects a tile, extend across its uniformly smooth graph by a finite reflection combination matching derivatives; the extension has uniform Sobolev and supremum bounds and comparable weights. Near-starting boxes use compact overlap controls.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.19", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Correction boundaries — original Claim 6 in the proof of Proposition B.1.** The signed sum of all corrections has initial and starting-annulus costs allowed by Lemma 6.13.", "statement": "<p>**Correction boundaries — original Claim 6 in the proof of Proposition B.1.**</p><p>The signed sum of all corrections has initial and starting-annulus costs allowed by Lemma 6.13. Strong/weak correction products use \\(2A_H&gt;A\\); pure defects retain their direct averaging estimate.</p>", "proof": "<p>Two-bad corrections contain first derivatives of lower commuted fields with bounded coefficients. Smooth-acceleration corrections carry \\(\\widetilde a\\). Strong screen corrections are quadratic weak-field products. Full mixed physically nontrapped overlap bounds and sphere Sobolev placement estimate them. Time-zero costs follow from cap estimates or initial symbols. Sum corrections before forming \\(\\xi\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.20", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Complete far-source estimate — original Proposition B.1.** For every commuted equation, the correction, remainder, raw, and mixed budgets satisfy …", "statement": "<p>**Complete far-source estimate — original Proposition B.1.**</p><p>For every commuted equation, the correction, remainder, raw, and mixed budgets satisfy\n\\[\no_{R_0}(1)(F+S_a)+C\\sum_{\\rm prior}F_{\\rm prior}\n+C(R_0)K_R+C_{\\rm fix}(e_{\\rm in}+\\mathcal N^{1+c})\n\\]\nfor some fixed \\(c&gt;0\\). Prior linear constants are independent of both matching radii.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 6.14–6.19 account respectively for common principal commutators, the additional strong equation, pure and suppressed sources, the leading weak-null structure, raw leading products, and all correction boundaries. Their source ledger covers the laboratory equation and its \\(D\\)-derivative. All radius-gain terms are summable; remaining size-one terms have exactly the finite prior order required by Lemma 6.12.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.21", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Radius-uniform coupling — original Lemma B.2.** The final coupling assertions in Proposition 6 hold.", "statement": "<p>**Radius-uniform coupling — original Lemma B.2.**</p><p>The final coupling assertions in Proposition 6 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a fixed \\(t\\)-slice,\n\\[\ny=C_e(t-r)-C_e(t)+rn,\n\\quad\nd^3y=kr^2\\,dr\\,d\\omega,\n\\quad\n\\partial_{y_i}r=n_i/k,\n\\quad\n\\operatorname{div}_yn=2/r.\n\\]\nThese first derivatives contain no acceleration.</p><p>An adjoint zero mode obeys\n\\[\n|v_p|\\leq Cr^{-2+\\varepsilon},\\qquad\n|\\partial_yv_p|\\leq Cr^{-3+\\varepsilon}.\n\\]\nIn the leading source pairing replace \\(T_l\\psi\\) by \\(X\\psi-n\\cdot\\partial_y\\psi\\), then integrate once in \\(d^3y\\). Derivatives of cutoffs, \\(v_p\\), and \\(n\\) gain an inverse radius:\n\\[\n|I(t)|\n\\leq C\\int_{r\\geq cR_0}\nr^\\varepsilon(|rX\\psi|+|\\psi|)\\,\\frac{dr\\,d\\omega}{r}.\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n\\varepsilon&lt;\\zeta&lt;(p_P-A)/2=0.13.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz contributes a uniformly bounded factor\n\\[\n(cR_0)^{-(\\zeta-\\varepsilon)}/\\sqrt{2(\\zeta-\\varepsilon)},\n\\]\nand gives \\(\\|\\langle t\\rangle^{A/2}I\\|_2\\leq CF\\).</p><p>Choose \\(Y\\) beyond fixed transitions before choosing \\(R_0\\). On the timelike cut, direct averaging bounds \\(\\widetilde a\\) in the strong time norm uniformly in radius. Pairing\n\\[\n|f_B|+|Q(0)a|\n\\leq Cr^{-2}(\\widetilde a+|a(t)|)\n\\]\nwith the adjoint tail costs\n\\[\nCS_a\\int_Y^\\infty \\rho^{-2+\\varepsilon}\\,d\\rho\n\\leq C_\\varepsilon Y^{-1+\\varepsilon}S_a.\n\\]\nThe pure-defect shell powers of Lemma 6.16 also apply on the starting annulus.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Simultaneous exterior closure and higher smooth propagation — original Proposition 6.4, Corollary B.3, and Proposition 5.6.** For each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr center and the fixed computing cylinders …", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous exterior closure and higher smooth propagation — original Proposition 6.4, Corollary B.3, and Proposition 5.6.**</p><p>For each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr center and the fixed computing cylinders, sufficiently small \\(p_{10}\\) bridge input gives global smooth vacuum evolution on both cylinders. The compact, far, static, modulation, and low-frequency bounds of Propositions 4–6 hold on every finite slab, with arbitrarily small total base bounds after shrinking the same neighborhood.</p><p>Every higher initial seminorm need only be finite. For every fixed smooth order, every \\(C_0&gt;0\\), and every \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\), all metric, coefficient, and parameter norms at that order on\n\\[\n0\\leq t\\leq T',\\qquad r_y\\leq C_0(1+T')\n\\]\nare bounded by \\(C_\\sigma e^{\\sigma T'}\\), with datum- and order-dependent constants. This is denoted \\(e^{o(T')}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Propositions 5–6 give exactly the three inequalities in Lemma 7.1. Their finite-slab norms are continuous in the endpoint; spacelike cap transversality handles cap suprema, and finite-time symbols control tails. The strict improvement preserves parameters, wave coefficients, spacelike and outflow margins, and implicit inverses. Proposition 4 supplies local continuation and vacuum constraint propagation.</p><p>All base input orders are supplied by \\(p_{10}\\): the far counts \\(9,8,7\\) carry one energy derivative, matching compact orders \\(10,9,8\\). Higher smoothness on finite intervals is tame and requires only finiteness. Apply Lemma 7.2 only after the base closure.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Acyclic base absorption — original closure system (6.28)–(6.31) and (B.22).** Suppose \\( K_R\\leq CR^{-\\kappa}(F+K_R)+E, \\) \\( F\\leq\\varepsilon_0(R_0)(F+S_a)+C(R_0)K_R+E, \\) \\( S_a\\leq C(R_0)K_R+C_*F+E, \\) where …", "statement": "<p>**Acyclic base absorption — original closure system (6.28)–(6.31) and (B.22).**</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\nK_R\\leq CR^{-\\kappa}(F+K_R)+E,\n\\]\n\\[\nF\\leq\\varepsilon_0(R_0)(F+S_a)+C(R_0)K_R+E,\n\\]\n\\[\nS_a\\leq C(R_0)K_R+C_*F+E,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_*\\) is independent of \\(R_0\\), \\(\\varepsilon_0(R_0)\\to0\\), and \\(E\\) consists of fixed input and superlinear bootstrap errors. Then choose \\(R_0\\), then \\(R\\), then \\(T_0\\), and finally bootstrap and data smallness to obtain a strict improvement uniform in lifespan.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(R_0\\) so \\(\\varepsilon_0(R_0)(1+C_*)\\) is sufficiently small. Substitute the acceleration estimate in the far estimate and absorb \\(F\\), obtaining \\(F+S_a\\leq C(R_0)K_R\\) plus errors. Enlarge \\(R\\) to absorb the resulting compact feedback, then \\(T_0\\) for plateau-small terms.</p><p>The sliced and low norms cost only fixed linear combinations of these absorbed quantities plus errors. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathcal N\\leq C_{\\rm fix}\\bigl(\\mathcal R(e_{\\rm in},\\mathcal N)\n+\\mathcal N^{1+c_2}\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal R\\to0\\) with input, uniformly on bounded small bootstrap ranges. Choose the cap first, then the input, to improve it.</p><p>All strict geometry, gauges, finite test orders, compact energy gluing, and the tail radius \\(Y\\) were fixed before \\(R_0\\). Enlarging compact reaches to include the starting overlap changes constants, not radius-gain exponents. No radius cycle occurs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Subexponential higher bounds — original Proposition 5.6.** Assume the global base bounds obtained by Lemma 7.1.", "statement": "<p>**Subexponential higher bounds — original Proposition 5.6.**</p><p>Assume the global base bounds obtained by Lemma 7.1. Then the higher-order conclusion of Proposition 7 holds without further data smallness.</p>", "proof": "<p>The base bounds imply uniform low decay\n\\[\nh=o(1),\\qquad g_{\\rm co}-G_{p,c}=o(1),\\qquad\nc,\\dot c,a=o(1)\n\\]\nthrough the required low derivatives. Compactly use weighted endpoints; far out use the point estimates with spare rotations. Also \\(B-G_p=o(1)\\). Since \\(a\\in L^1\\), \\(p\\) converges.</p><p>Fix \\(T'\\) and use shrinking cylinders with outer radius\n\\[\nC_0(1+T')+C_1(T'-t),\n\\]\nwith strict exit. Start induction through order seven using bulk order eight and polynomial measure/weight conversions.</p><p>At each \\(s\\geq8\\), the frozen conic energy is available; compatible trapping transport is independent of \\(s\\), and horizon damping improves. Start sufficiently late, depending on \\(s\\), so actual-principal positivity holds. Continue the differential current to the exiting outer boundary. Its top energy equivalence is radius independent because\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde r\\,G\\|_{L^2(d\\widetilde r\\,d\\omega)}^2\n=\n|\\det A_{\\rm aff}|\\|G\\|_{L^2(d^3y)}^2.\n\\]</p><p>Use vanishing low coefficients instead of integrability at top order. Lower-order norms and polynomial losses are \\(e^{o(T')}\\). The fixed filter gives\n\\[\na^{(s+3)}\n\\quad\\text{from at most}\\quad\n\\max(0,s+4-P)\\leq s-1\n\\]\nprojection derivatives. Negative-order acceleration corrections use the preceding induction order.</p><p>For \\(X_s=(1+\\mathcal E_s)^{1/2}\\),\n\\[\n\\dot X_s\\leq\\alpha_s(t)X_s+B_{s-1}(T'),\n\\qquad\n\\alpha_s(t)\\to0,\\quad B_{s-1}=e^{o(T')},\n\\]\nwith the vanishing coefficient independent of terminal radius. Gronwall gives any prescribed small exponential rate. Recover higher time jets by successive normal solves; coefficient derivatives are of lower recovered order. Sobolev embedding yields all fixed smooth bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Full two-ended double-null comparison wedge — original Proposition 7.7 and Lemma 7.8.** Fix the physical Kerr parameter range near the center.", "statement": "<p>**Full two-ended double-null comparison wedge — original Proposition 7.7 and Lemma 7.8.**</p><p>Fix the physical Kerr parameter range near the center. Choose a common \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\), a sufficiently small profile depth \\(q_0&gt;0\\), and a sufficiently small base-data neighborhood as below.</p><p>The vacuum data induced on the complete deep spacelike cylinder \\(S\\simeq\\mathbb R\\times S^2\\) have a smooth development on\n\\[\n\\{s\\geq0\\}\\times\\mathbb R_x\\times S^2,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\ns=(y^0+y^1)/2,\\quad x=(y^1-y^0)/2,\\quad\nq=q_0e^{-2\\kappa s},\n\\]\n\\[\ng=-2a\\,dy^0dy^1+\n\\gamma_{AB}(d\\theta^A-b^A dy^0)(d\\theta^B-b^Bdy^0),\n\\qquad a=qA.\n\\]\nThe uniformly summed low bounds, pure-jet estimates, and higher estimates below hold. With a small past collar, \\(S\\) is Cauchy for this development.</p><p>The labels can be exchanged on the same product spacetime and the same cylinder, using the other one-shift angular gauge. All conclusions remain valid, with the same \\(\\kappa\\) and a frozen physical profile depending only on the new \\(y^0\\). No identification with the whole maximal development is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(q_0\\) using only exact-profile constants and the return-ray margin, then choose the interior bootstrap size and finally the entry size. Lemmas 8.1–8.3 give the required cylinder data. Lemmas 8.4–8.5 give uniform low and pure bounds, while the fixed-region part of Lemma 8.6 permits the finite restarts of Lemma 8.7. Exhaustion then justifies the global tails needed for its expanding part. Lemma 8.8 gives the exchanged labeling on the same product spacetime.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exact common-rate profiles — original Lemma 7.1.** Let \\(B_0=b,B_1=0\\), \\( L_i=\\partial_{y^i}+B_i,\\qquad \\mathcal L_i=\\partial_{y^i}+\\mathcal L_{B_i}, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Exact common-rate profiles — original Lemma 7.1.**</p><p>Let \\(B_0=b,B_1=0\\),\n\\[\nL_i=\\partial_{y^i}+B_i,\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_i=\\partial_{y^i}+\\mathcal L_{B_i},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\chi_i=\\tfrac12\\mathcal L_i\\gamma,\\quad\n\\tau_i=\\operatorname{tr}_\\gamma\\chi_i,\\quad\n\\ell_i=L_i\\log a,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\xi_0+\\xi_1=d\\log a,\\qquad\n\\xi_0-\\xi_1=-a^{-1}\\gamma\\partial_{y^1}b,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\beta_i=\\operatorname{div}\\chi_i-d\\tau_i,\\quad\nQ_i=(K,\\operatorname{curl}\\xi_j),\\quad\n\\mu_i=K-\\operatorname{div}\\xi_j,\\quad j=1-i.\n\\]\nThere are exact axisymmetric Kerr profiles, smoothly dependent on \\((q,\\theta)\\) and physical parameters, for which \\(A,\\gamma\\) and inverses extend smoothly to \\(q=0\\),\n\\[\nb=O(q),\\qquad\n\\chi_i,\\ell_i+\\kappa,\\beta_i=O(q),\n\\qquad\n\\xi_i,K,\\operatorname{curl}\\xi_j,\\mu_i=O(1),\n\\]\nafter every fixed angular or parameter derivative. Either shift can be eliminated. Constants are fixed before choosing \\(q_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>In Boyer–Lindquist variables solve\n\\[\n2(r^2+a^2)F_r+\\Delta F_r^2+\n|d_\\omega F|_{\\rm rd}^2+a^2\\sin^2\\vartheta=0,\n\\qquad F(r_-)=0.\n\\]\nIts finite root is noncharacteristic since the coefficient of \\(F_r\\) at \\(r_-\\) is \\(2(r_-^2+a^2)&gt;0\\). The functions \\(r_*+F\\pm T_K\\) are optical.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nQ=e^{-2\\kappa_-(r_*+F)},\\quad\ns=-\\frac{\\log(Q/q_0)}{2\\kappa},\\quad\nx=\\frac{\\kappa_-}{\\kappa}T_K.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\nr_*=-\\frac1{2\\kappa_-}\\log(r-r_-)+\\text{smooth},\n\\]\n\\(Q\\) is \\(r-r_-\\) times a smooth positive factor. The common scale factor in both optical directions makes \\(\\kappa\\) independent of parameters.</p><p>Corotate by the inner angular velocity. Cross coefficients with \\(ds,dx\\) are \\(O(Q)\\), the angular metric extends positively, and volume divided by \\(Q\\) is smooth positive. Eliminate one \\(O(Q)\\) shift by a sphere transport map whose equation after division by \\(Q\\) is regular. Then \\(L_iq=-\\kappa q\\), giving the stated orders.</p><p>Choose \\(q_0\\) small for later absorptions and also so\n\\[\n\\bigl(|\\partial_\\vartheta F|^2+a^2\\sin^2\\vartheta\\bigr)_{\\rm entry}&lt;4a^2.\n\\]\nThis is possible because \\(\\partial_\\vartheta F=O(q_0)\\) and the fixed spin stays positive. It supplies the return-ray radial-potential margin before choosing exterior constants.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Moving preparation without growing phases — original Lemma 7.2.** On fixed entry supports write the stationary reference variables as \\( T_K=\\varsigma(\\pi)t+P_K(\\pi,y). \\) For time-dependent \\(\\pi(t)\\), use …", "statement": "<p>**Moving preparation without growing phases — original Lemma 7.2.**</p><p>On fixed entry supports write the stationary reference variables as\n\\[\nT_K=\\varsigma(\\pi)t+P_K(\\pi,y).\n\\]\nFor time-dependent \\(\\pi(t)\\), use instantaneous \\(Q\\) and angular map, and define\n\\[\nx=I(t)+\\lambda_-P_K,\\qquad\n\\alpha_{\\rm rot}=I_{\\rm rot}(t)+\\Omega_-P_K,\n\\]\n\\[\nI'=\\lambda_-\\varsigma,\\qquad\nI_{\\rm rot}'=\\Omega_-\\varsigma,\\qquad\n\\lambda_-=\\kappa_-/\\kappa.\n\\]\nThe actual and frozen Jacobians differ only by positive parameter derivatives with bounded coefficients. No \\(t\\pi'\\) term occurs. Fixed \\((s,x)\\)-spheres near \\(S\\) are uniformly controlled graphs, with times a bounded distance from \\(I^{-1}(x)\\) and nonsingular angular Jacobians.</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(t_0\\), freeze \\(\\pi(t_0)\\) and add constant phase offsets\n\\[\nI(t_0)-(\\lambda_-\\varsigma)(\\pi(t_0))t_0,\n\\quad\nI_{\\rm rot}(t_0)-(\\Omega_-\\varsigma)(\\pi(t_0))t_0.\n\\]\nThe maps agree in value. Stationarity and axisymmetry make the offsets irrelevant to the frozen profile. Their derivative discrepancies contain parameter jets but never elapsed time times a parameter derivative.</p><p>Solve the \\(s\\)-equation for radius. Along that graph the time derivative of \\(x\\) is \\(I'+O(|\\pi'|)\\), bounded away from zero. At fixed \\(x\\), angular differentiation of total rotation costs \\(O(|\\pi'|)\\), giving the graph and diffeomorphism assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cylinder data budget — original Proposition 7.3.** Start actual optical coordinates on \\(S\\) with \\(y^0=-x,y^1=x\\) and transport prepared angular labels along \\(L_1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Cylinder data budget — original Proposition 7.3.**</p><p>Start actual optical coordinates on \\(S\\) with \\(y^0=-x,y^1=x\\) and transport prepared angular labels along \\(L_1\\). Define a single angle-independent physical profile on the wedge by the two end prescriptions\n\\[\np(y^0)=p\\bigl(\\pi(I^{-1}(-y^0))\\bigr),\n\\]\njoined through a bounded interval. Hats mean exact profile evaluation after differentiation at fixed physical parameters.</p><p>For sufficiently small common base input, the sum of unit-\\(x\\)-cell supremum norms of actual-minus-frozen differences is at most any prescribed small \\(e_S\\) for\n\\[\nA,\\log A,\\gamma,b,\\chi_i,\\ell_i,\\xi_i\n\\quad\\text{in angular }H^6,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\gamma\\quad\\text{in angular }H^7,\n\\qquad\n\\partial_x(\\chi_i,\\ell_i,\\xi_i,b)|_S\n\\quad\\text{in angular }H^2.\n\\]\nAlso \\(\\sigma_0=|p'(y^0)|,\\sigma_1=0\\) have summed cell suprema \\(O(e_S)\\). Every fixed higher initial norm and parameter jet on \\(|x|\\leq CT\\) is \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On the fixed entry graphs, the sliced exterior estimate controls mixed metric residual derivatives through eight and one additional time derivative at that order in \\(L^2_{A_S}\\), \\(A_S&gt;1\\). Positive parameter derivatives through eight have enlarged-cell suprema from through-nine weighted \\(L^2\\) bounds.</p><p>For \\(|\\alpha|\\leq7\\), an iterated one-dimensional trace gives\n\\[\n\\int_{S^2}\\sup_{t,r}|D^\\alpha(g_{\\rm co}-G_{p,c})|^2\\,d\\omega\n\\leq C\\sum_{\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon'\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\|\\partial_t^\\varepsilon\\partial_r^{\\varepsilon'}\nD^\\alpha(g_{\\rm co}-G_{p,c})\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThe mixed term uses the order-eight estimate for the time derivative, not a ninth-order pointwise trace. For cell norms \\(a_n\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_na_n\n\\leq\n\\left(\\sum_n\\langle n\\rangle^{-A_S}\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\sum_n\\langle n\\rangle^{A_S}a_n^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]</p><p>Lemma 8.2 removes growing phase errors. Noncharacteristic eikonal and angular equations express optical initial jets in metric jets; the displayed geometric quantities require metric derivatives through seven and parameter jets through eight. Sphere products and the trace give the stated bounds. Bounded middle joins use finite-time through-nine spacetime estimates. Higher orders use Proposition 7.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform low characteristic estimates — original Proposition 7.4.** On every smooth finite past diamond \\( 0\\leq s\\leq s_*,\\qquad y^0\\leq C_0,\\quad y^1\\leq C_1, \\qquad C_0+C_1>0, \\) the entry assumptions imply uniform …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform low characteristic estimates — original Proposition 7.4.**</p><p>On every smooth finite past diamond\n\\[\n0\\leq s\\leq s_*,\\qquad y^0\\leq C_0,\\quad y^1\\leq C_1,\n\\qquad C_0+C_1&gt;0,\n\\]\nthe entry assumptions imply uniform small low budgets\n\\[\nE+R+X+D_b+P+S\\leq C(q_0)e_S.\n\\]\nHere \\(E\\) is the unsquared cell sum of localized angular-\\(H^5\\) paired \\(\\delta\\beta_i\\) and \\(\\sqrt q\\,\\delta(Q_i,\\mu_i)\\) null/slice/spacetime fluxes; \\(R\\) is the corresponding \\(\\delta\\chi_i\\) \\(H^6\\) flux sum; \\(X\\) the \\(\\sqrt q\\,\\delta\\xi_j\\) \\(H^6\\) sum; \\(D_b\\) the directional shift \\(H^6\\) sum; \\(P\\) the unweighted and exponentially localized \\(\\delta(\\gamma,\\log A)\\) \\(H^6\\), \\(\\delta\\xi\\) \\(H^5\\) point budget; and\n\\[\ng_i(y^i)=\\sup_{y^j}\n\\left(|\\delta(\\chi_i,\\ell_i)|_{H^4}\n+|\\delta B_i|_{H^5}\\right),\n\\qquad\nS=\\sum_{i,n}\\sup e^{-\\eta|y^i-n|}g_i.\n\\]\nThe metrics and inverses remain uniformly nondegenerate. Constants are independent of diamond size and location.</p>", "proof": "<p>Koszul and vacuum give\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i\\tau_i=\\ell_i\\tau_i-|\\chi_i|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_i\\xi_j=\\beta_i+\\tau_i\\xi_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j\\chi_i\n=\na\\operatorname{Sym}\\nabla\\xi_j-\\tfrac a2K\\gamma\n+a\\xi_j^{\\otimes2}\n-\\tfrac12(\\tau_i\\chi_j+\\tau_j\\chi_i)\n+\\chi_i\\chi_j+\\chi_j\\chi_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_j\\ell_i\n=\naK-\\chi_i\\cdot\\chi_j+\\tau_i\\tau_j\n+a(2\\xi_i\\cdot\\xi_j-|\\xi_i|^2).\n\\]\nThe connection identities are\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_i}L_i=\\ell_iL_i,\\quad\n\\nabla_EL_i=\\chi_i(E)^\\sharp+\\xi_i(E)L_i,\\quad\n\\nabla_{L_i}L_j=a\\xi_i^\\sharp.\n\\]\nThe angular Gauss and null Ricci contractions give the cross equations. Divergence, curl, and curvature variation yield\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_j\\beta_i\n=\\tfrac a2(dK+Jd\\,\\operatorname{curl}\\xi_j)\n+a(K*\\xi+\\xi*\\nabla\\xi+\\xi^3)\n+\\chi_i*\\nabla\\chi_j+\\chi_j*\\nabla\\chi_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i(Q_i,\\mu_i)\n=((\\operatorname{div}\\beta_i,\\operatorname{curl}\\beta_i),0)\n+\\chi_i*(K,\\nabla\\xi)+\\nabla\\chi_i*\\xi,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n[L_i,L_j]=a(\\xi_i-\\xi_j)^\\sharp,\\qquad\n\\partial_{y^1}b=a\\gamma^{-1}(\\xi_1-\\xi_0).\n\\]</p><p>With \\(w_{i,n}=e^{-\\eta|y^i-n|}\\), orientation change costs\n\\(w_{i,n}\\leq e^{2\\eta s}w_{j,-n}\\), absorbed by positive powers of \\(q\\).\nHodge and transport give\n\\[\nX\\leq C(E+\\sqrt{q_0}P),\\qquad\nD_b\\leq Ce_S+Cq_0^c(X+P),\n\\]\n\\[\nS\\leq Ce_S+Cq_0^c(P+S),\\qquad\nP\\leq C(e_S+E+R+D_b+S).\n\\]\nThe exact trace equation\n\\[\n(L_i+\\kappa)(\\tau_i/A)=-A^{-1}|\\chi_i|^2\n\\]\nand Hodge recovery yield\n\\[\nR\\leq C(E+e_S+S)+C(q_0^c+\\varepsilon_{\\rm boot})(P+D_b+R).\n\\]</p><p>Pair the angular systems through order five with equal \\(w_{i,n}^2\\) weights and \\(aw_{i,n}^2/2\\) on curvature variables. Angular principal terms cancel; the latter weight has strict lapse damping. Opposite-orientation products are controlled by cellwise two-dimensional Cauchy–Schwarz, giving\n\\[\nE\\leq Ce_S+C(q_0^c+S)(E+R+D_b+P+X).\n\\]\nFirst absorb the auxiliary budgets into \\(C(E+e_S)\\), then choose \\(q_0\\), bootstrap size, and finally \\(e_S\\) to close. All future boundary fluxes are favorable.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Pure jets and flank limits — original Proposition 7.5.** The summed unit-\\(y^i\\)-cell suprema of …", "statement": "<p>**Pure jets and flank limits — original Proposition 7.5.**</p><p>The summed unit-\\(y^i\\)-cell suprema of\n\\[\n\\sup_{y^j}\n\\left(\n|\\mathcal L_i\\chi_i-\\widehat{\\mathcal L_i\\chi_i}|_{H^2}\n+\n|L_i\\ell_i-\\widehat{L_i\\ell_i}|_{H^2}\n\\right)\n\\]\nare \\(O(e_S)\\). The same holds for the pure \\(y^0\\)-shift derivative against frozen differentiation.</p><p>On a full exhausted wedge,\n\\(A^{\\pm1},\\gamma^{\\pm1}\\) are uniformly \\(H^6\\), and\n\\(\\xi_i,a^{-1}\\partial_{y^1}b\\) uniformly \\(H^5\\). As\n\\(s,|y^0|,|y^1|\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\n|b|_{H^5}+\\sum_i|(\\chi_i,\\ell_i+\\kappa)|_{H^4}\\to0,\n\\]\nand the first ordinary derivatives of \\(b,\\chi_i,\\ell_i\\), together with\n\\(\\partial_{y^k}\\gamma\\) and \\(\\partial_{y^k}\\log a+\\kappa\\) at the stated orders, tend to zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(U_i=\\mathcal L_i\\chi_i,V_i=L_i\\ell_i\\), differentiate the cross equations and use\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_i\\xi_i=d\\ell_i-\\beta_i-\\tau_i\\xi_i,\n\\qquad L_iK=\\operatorname{div}\\beta_i-\\tau_iK.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_jU_i=-\\tfrac12\\tau_jU_i+U_i\\chi_j+\\chi_jU_i+F_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nL_jV_i=-U_i\\cdot\\chi_j+G_i.\n\\]\nNo remainder contains a new pure jet. Difference sources in \\(H^2\\) cost\n\\[\nC\\{q(P+g_i+g_j+\\sigma_j)+g_ig_j\\},\n\\]\nwith homogeneous coefficient \\(C(q+g_j)\\). Initial pure jets follow from tangential entry derivatives and cross equations. Localized Gronwall gives the summed estimate.</p><p>For \\(\\zeta_\\xi=\\xi_1-\\xi_0\\),\n\\[\n\\partial_{y^1}\\partial_{y^0}b\n=\na\\{\\ell_0\\gamma^{-1}\\zeta_\\xi\n-2\\gamma^{-1}\\chi_0\\gamma^{-1}\\zeta_\\xi\n+\\gamma^{-1}(2\\beta_0+2\\tau_0\\xi_0-d\\ell_0)\\}\n-[b,\\partial_{y^1}b].\n\\]\nEvery source retains \\(a\\), so integration gives the shift estimate.</p><p>Summable cell suprema vanish in their tails. Exact profile decay, cross equations, pure estimates, and\n\\[\n\\partial_{y^i}\\gamma=2\\chi_i-\\mathcal L_{B_i}\\gamma,\n\\quad\n\\partial_{y^i}\\log a+\\kappa\n=\\ell_i+\\kappa-B_i\\cdot d\\log a\n\\]\ngive the asserted limits.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Higher interior estimates — original Proposition 7.6.** On every fixed finite diamond, every fixed ordinary coordinate derivative of \\( \\gamma^{\\pm1},\\log A,b,a^{-1}\\partial_{y^1}b \\) has a finite bound depending on …", "statement": "<p>**Higher interior estimates — original Proposition 7.6.**</p><p>On every fixed finite diamond, every fixed ordinary coordinate derivative of\n\\[\n\\gamma^{\\pm1},\\log A,b,a^{-1}\\partial_{y^1}b\n\\]\nhas a finite bound depending on the corresponding smooth initial norms, low bounds, and diamond.</p><p>If the full wedge and its global summed low bounds exist, then, for fixed \\(U\\in\\mathbb R\\), \\(C&gt;0\\), and either labeling \\(\\{d,l\\}=\\{0,1\\}\\), all such fixed derivatives on\n\\[\ns\\geq0,\\qquad y^d\\leq U,\\qquad y^l\\leq CT\n\\]\nare \\(e^{o(T)}\\), also on restrictions and coordinate subsegments. Higher initial norms need not be small.</p>", "proof": "<p>At angular order \\(m\\geq5\\), set\n\\[\nY_i=|\\beta_i|_m,\\quad\nZ_i=\\sqrt q\\,|(Q_i,\\mu_i)|_m,\\quad\nR_i=|\\chi_i|_{m+1},\\quad\nz_i=|\\tau_i/A|_{m+1},\n\\]\nand include \\(D=|b|_{m+1}\\), \\(P_\\gamma=1+|\\gamma|_{m+1}\\), \\(P_i=|\\xi_j|_m\\).\nWith \\(h_i=C_0(q+g_i)\\), tame Hodge gives\n\\[\nR_i\\leq C_m(Y_i+z_i+h_iP_*),\\qquad\n|\\xi_j|_{m+1}\\leq C_m(Z_i/\\sqrt q+P_*).\n\\]\nPaired angular currents, trace, shift, metric, and torsion currents have only the interactions recorded in (7.40): \\(\\sqrt q\\)-suppressed pairs, \\(h_i\\)-weighted pairs, and the triangular metric/torsion arrows.</p><p>On a finite diamond the common weight \\(e^{-2K_{\\rm fin}s}\\), \\(K_{\\rm fin}&gt;C_m\\), absorbs every interaction.</p><p>For expanding regions, rescale \\(z_i^2\\) by a large \\(\\Lambda^2\\) and \\(P\\)-currents by small \\(\\varepsilon_P^2\\), retaining equal weights on each \\(Y_i,Z_i\\) pair. When \\(q,h_0,h_1\\) are small, the remaining arrows are triangular. In the asymmetric late regime, where \\(h_d\\) may remain bounded, strongly damp currents flowing in direction \\(d\\); after their removal the only unsuppressed arrows are \\(z_l\\) to \\(Y_d,P_l\\), and possibly \\(P_\\gamma\\), with no return to \\(z_l\\).</p><p>Choose the common weight\n\\[\nw=\\exp\\{-2\\delta_h(y^0+y^1)\n-2K_0(y^d+B)_+-2K_0\\min(s,S_0)\\}.\n\\]\nChoose rescalings first, then a small coefficient threshold, then \\(B,S_0\\) using vanishing tails, and finally \\(K_0\\). This absorbs all regions. Removing the weight costs\n\\[\ne^{2\\delta_h(U+CT)+2K_0(U+B)_++2K_0S_0},\n\\]\nand \\(\\delta_h\\) is arbitrary.</p><p>Do not divide the curvature norm by \\(\\sqrt q\\). Recover unweighted quantities through damped trace convolution and metric/torsion transport. The bounded potential\n\\[\n\\Phi=C_0\\sum_i\\int_{-\\infty}^{y^i}g_i(v)\\,dv\n+\\frac{C_0q_0}{\\kappa}(1-e^{-2\\kappa s})\n\\]\nhas \\(\\partial_{y^i}\\Phi=h_i\\); multiplication by \\(e^{-C\\Phi}\\) absorbs the resulting integral inequalities. Cross transports recover pointwise angular bounds.</p><p>Induct on longitudinal derivatives. Differentiated cross equations contain only the new pure shape jet with integrable homogeneous coefficient \\(C_mh_j\\); torsion and curvature derivatives reduce to already controlled angular equations. Pure lapse and shift recovery is triangular. Polynomial-length integrations preserve \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite restarts and exhaustion — continuation argument in original Proposition 7.7.** The entry solution continues to every compact target diamond, and exhaustion gives the full product wedge.", "statement": "<p>**Finite restarts and exhaustion — continuation argument in original Proposition 7.7.**</p><p>The entry solution continues to every compact target diamond, and exhaustion gives the full product wedge. The uniform low sums persist under exhaustion; \\(S\\), with a sufficiently small past collar, is Cauchy.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a compact target use a slightly larger tilted domain\n\\[\n0\\leq s\\leq s_*,\\qquad y^i\\leq C_i-\\alpha s\n\\]\nwith small \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\); its sides are strict exits. Lemmas 8.4 and 8.6 bound all fixed smooth norms up to a putative finite terminal time. Since \\(s\\) is bounded, \\(a=q_0e^{-2\\kappa s}A\\) has a positive lower bound.</p><p>Restart from an earlier slice, extending its induced data smoothly over endpoint margins; constraints are unnecessary outside the protected margin. Reduced wave existence and optical transport give a uniform short continuation. Causal monotonicity \\(dy^i\\geq0\\), gauge propagation, and geometric uniqueness identify it on the exiting region. No finite terminal time occurs.</p><p>Exhaustion preserves the low suprema and unsquared cell sums. Only then use their vanishing tails in the expanding estimates of Lemma 8.6.</p><p>The function \\(s\\) is temporal and \\(|dx/ds|\\leq1\\) on causal curves. Angular speeds are bounded on compact coordinate ranges. Past causal curves reach \\(S\\) or continue at an ordinary interior point; a small variable-thickness past collar makes future curves there cross \\(S\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.8", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exchange of labels — original Lemma 7.8.** On the wedge of Lemma 8.7, exchanging \\(y^0,y^1\\) and choosing the corresponding one-shift angular gauge preserves the same spacetime and initial cylinder, and reestablishes …", "statement": "<p>**Exchange of labels — original Lemma 7.8.**</p><p>On the wedge of Lemma 8.7, exchanging \\(y^0,y^1\\) and choosing the corresponding one-shift angular gauge preserves the same spacetime and initial cylinder, and reestablishes all entry, low, pure, and higher estimates.</p>", "proof": "<p>Negate prepared \\(x\\) on \\(S\\). Optical uniqueness identifies the exchanged functions. Transport new angular labels along the old \\(L_0\\); at every finite point this is finite-duration smooth flow on a compact sphere and hence a diffeomorphism.</p><p>Reverse \\(T_K,\\phi\\) simultaneously, use \\(-I,-I_{\\rm rot}\\) and the other exact angular transport map, and define the new profile along the new \\(y^0\\). The trace proof applies afresh. Apply the finite-diamond estimates and exhaust. No prior uniform derivative estimate for the long angular coordinate change is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Complete-bridge comparison development — original Proposition 8.2.** For one sufficiently small \\(p_{10}\\) neighborhood, every datum \\(d\\) has a smooth time-oriented vacuum development \\(P_d\\) containing:", "statement": "<p>**Complete-bridge comparison development — original Proposition 8.2.**</p><p>For one sufficiently small \\(p_{10}\\) neighborhood, every datum \\(d\\) has a smooth time-oriented vacuum development \\(P_d\\) containing:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. a finite bridge evolution and a past collar of the entire original bridge;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. both late exterior evolutions;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. the complete deep cylinder \\(S\\) and its full double-null wedge.</p><p>The complete original bridge \\(\\Sigma\\) is Cauchy for \\(P_d\\). There is a time-orientation-preserving open isometric embedding\n\\[\nP_d\\hookrightarrow M_d.\n\\]\nThe comparison region is not asserted to exhaust \\(M_d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Start the exterior evolutions from the induced delayed-slice data and choose a fixed matching time \\(t_a&gt;1\\) within Lemma 9.1’s finite range. Identify the exterior and finite vacuum solutions by a wave map with matching initial identification and normals. Pullback gives matching first jets; reduced wave uniqueness on enlarged outflow domains makes the map an isometry. Its fixed-time \\(H^{10}\\) control gives at least \\(H^9\\) spacetime closeness through the straightened join.</p><p>Join the prepared end portions of \\(S\\) to the middle in bounded overlaps using small smooth coordinate changes. Instantaneous profile comparison keeps radial entry graphs uniformly close without accumulated angular phase errors. Retain the finite piece below \\(S\\), attach late exterior pieces across common matching faces, discard the deeper computing boundaries, and attach Proposition 8’s wedge across the entire cylinder.</p><p>Use common collars and retain no duplicate open future continuation. Product neighborhoods at corners give a Hausdorff smooth manifold.</p><p>A past causal curve in the wedge reaches \\(S\\), since \\(s\\) decreases and \\(|dx/ds|\\leq1\\). In a late exterior it proceeds to the finite block, not through the future side of \\(S\\). Lemma 9.1 then carries it to \\(\\Sigma\\). The past collar gives the other direction, and crossing orientation gives uniqueness. The maximal-development theorem therefore supplies the open embedding.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite bridge evolution — original Lemma 8.1.** Fix the exact profile depth, a computing radius \\(r_b\\) strictly deeper than the entry cylinder and above \\(r_-\\), delayed slices \\( t_e=T_e+r_*-G(r)=0, \\) and bounded …", "statement": "<p>**Finite bridge evolution — original Lemma 8.1.**</p><p>Fix the exact profile depth, a computing radius \\(r_b\\) strictly deeper than the entry cylinder and above \\(r_-\\), delayed slices\n\\[\nt_e=T_e+r_*-G(r)=0,\n\\]\nand bounded forward matching bands on them. Every sufficiently \\(p_{10}\\)-small bridge datum has a finite vacuum Cauchy development containing those slices, bands, and a past collar of the entire bridge. Induced metric and second-form data have compact orders \\(H^{10},H^9\\), retain end symbols, and have finite higher norms. Artificial future cuts are strict exits.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an exact Kerr range cut at a radius below \\(r_b\\) but above \\(r_-\\), and at fixed large \\(t_e=L\\) on both ends. Impose both time inequalities in the trapped block. Every active cut is spacelike future outflow.</p><p>For past causal curves in the trapped block,\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{dT_K}{dr}\\right|\n\\leq\n\\frac{\\sqrt{(r^2+a^2)^2-\\Delta a^2\\sin^2\\vartheta}}{|\\Delta|}.\n\\]\nNear \\(r_+\\) this is \\(|r_*'|+O(1)\\). Thus the interior Kruskal variables\n\\[\nU_+=e^{\\kappa_+(r_*+T_K)},\\qquad\nV_+=e^{\\kappa_+(r_*-T_K)}\n\\]\nhave finite nonnegative past limits. The corotating angular speed is \\(O(|\\Delta|^{-1/2})\\), integrable in \\(r\\), giving a regular horizon endpoint. Past continuation follows a generator to bifurcation or enters an exterior, where decreasing \\(T_e\\), bounded speeds, and the bridge value prevent escape before reaching \\(\\Sigma\\).</p><p>Apply finite-time vacuum stability on an enlargement and include compact causal shadows. Spacelike energy flux after nine commutations gives the \\(H^{10}\\times H^9\\) data without a highest-order graph-trace loss. Uniformly local end charts and bounded propagation preserve symbols. Low closeness preserves strict cuts.</p><p>A compact temporal-function region, enlarged to cover possible bounded-time excursions, together with exterior time far out, prevents finite-range causal escape. A short past collar supplies the other Cauchy direction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**First runout and a branch seed — original Proposition 8.8.** Let \\(d\\) belong to the common base neighborhood and \\(O\\subset T\\Sigma\\) be open.", "statement": "<p>**First runout and a branch seed — original Proposition 8.8.**</p><p>Let \\(d\\) belong to the common base neighborhood and \\(O\\subset T\\Sigma\\) be open. Suppose \\(w\\in O\\) has finite future proper duration in \\(M_d\\) and its endpoint is reached on a regular geodesic arc in a \\(C^2\\) extension.</p><p>Then some \\(w'\\in O\\) first runs out of \\(P_d\\) at a noncorner branch:\n\\[\ny^0\\to y_*^0&lt;\\infty,\\ y^1\\to+\\infty,\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\ny^1\\to y_*^1&lt;\\infty,\\ y^0\\to+\\infty.\n\\]\nThe new seed need not have a regular exit in the extension. If \\(d\\in A(O,N)\\), its duration and parallel-curvature bounds still apply to this seed.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Lemma 10.4, only a corner needs alteration. At a late corner point replace the tangent by\n\\[\nU_\\sigma=(1+\\sigma)\\dot y^0L_0\n+(1+\\sigma)^{-1}\\dot y^1L_1+\\Xi,\n\\qquad \\sigma&gt;0.\n\\]\nIt remains future unit timelike, and multiplies the initial \\(Y\\)-speed ratio by \\((1+\\sigma)^2\\). The original and changed corner-necessary intervals are disjoint if\n\\[\n4\\delta_c&lt;2\\log(1+\\sigma).\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\), a sufficiently late change therefore produces a branch.</p><p>Choose \\(\\sigma\\) uniformly small enough to return to \\(O\\). Along the original tail \\(\\Xi\\) is bounded and\n\\[\n-g(U,U_\\sigma)\n=\n1+(1+|\\Xi|^2)\n\\left(\\frac{1+\\sigma+(1+\\sigma)^{-1}}2-1\\right),\n\\]\nso relative rapidity is \\(O(\\sigma)\\) uniformly late.</p><p>At first runout there is a regular ambient state: an ordinary point of \\(M_d\\) if runout precedes maximal time, or the assumed regular extension state otherwise. Choose one precompact temporal chart and a convex timelike slope ball. Backward geodesic integration from late change points to a fixed earlier interior slice changes the state by \\(O(\\sigma)\\) with one uniform constant.</p><p>The backward arc remains in \\(P_d\\). Connect its earlier endpoint to the late change point by a path equal to the original track except for a small early bump. Spatial interpolation gives a fixed-endpoint timelike homotopy. Lemma 1.4, applied to the globally hyperbolic \\(P_d\\), keeps the changed arc inside. Earlier compact geodesic flow then returns its seed to \\(O\\).</p><p>Fix the ambient chart and earlier-flow tolerance first, choose one small \\(\\sigma\\), and only then choose the change point late enough for the ratio separation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Compact exterior excursions — original Lemma 8.3.** On fixed compact exterior cylinders, \\( \\|g-G_{p,c}\\|_{C^1}+|p'|+|c'|\\leq\\rho_c(t), \\qquad \\rho_c\\in L^1. \\) For a future unit geodesic inextendible in \\(P_d\\) and …", "statement": "<p>**Compact exterior excursions — original Lemma 8.3.**</p><p>On fixed compact exterior cylinders,\n\\[\n\\|g-G_{p,c}\\|_{C^1}+|p'|+|c'|\\leq\\rho_c(t),\n\\qquad \\rho_c\\in L^1.\n\\]\nFor a future unit geodesic inextendible in \\(P_d\\) and never crossing \\(S\\), every portion \\([t_1,t_2]\\) inside a fixed large co-moving radius satisfies\n\\[\n\\dot t(t)\\leq C\\dot t(t_1),\n\\]\nindependently of duration and repeated horizon-strip visits. If endpoints lie at a uniformly timelike stationary-energy interface,\n\\[\nE(t_2)\\leq E(t_1)\n\\exp\\!\\left(C\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\rho_c(t)\\,dt\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(E=-g(\\partial_t,U)\\) and instantaneous axial momentum \\(P=g(\\Phi_{p,c},U)\\), deformation gives\n\\[\n|E'|+|P'|\\leq C\\rho_c\\dot t.\n\\]\nIn a horizon strip choose a stationary timelike \\(N_H\\) with\n\\[\n(\\mathcal L_{N_H}g)(V,V)&gt;c&gt;0,\\qquad V=U/\\dot t.\n\\]\nStart with a stationary timelike \\(N_0\\); on the generator,\n\\(b(\\nabla_HN_0,H)=-\\kappa_+b(N_0,H)&gt;0\\).\nMultiplication by a suitable radial exponential supplies strict sign off the generator. Thus \\(-g(N_H,U)\\asymp\\dot t\\) is nonincreasing on each strip visit.</p><p>Below a smaller interior level, radial time forces crossing \\(S\\) in bounded \\(t\\), so an avoiding geodesic cannot enter. Outside the strip a bounded timelike combination of stationary and axial Killing fields gives\n\\[\n\\dot t\\leq C(|E|+|P|),\n\\]\nincluding the ergoregion.</p><p>Use one monotone majorant\n\\[\nB(t)=|E(t_1)|+|P(t_1)|\n+C\\int_{t_1}^t\\rho_c(v)\\dot t(v)\\,dv,\n\\]\nincluding initial strip energy if needed. It bounds all visits without a per-return factor. Gronwall gives the first estimate; integrating the \\(E\\)-equation itself gives the stated exponential return estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Outer-track integrability — original Lemma 8.4.** Interpolate a uniformly future timelike field \\(Z_K\\) from \\(\\partial_t\\) near the compact interface to \\(D\\) far out.", "statement": "<p>**Outer-track integrability — original Lemma 8.4.**</p><p>Interpolate a uniformly future timelike field \\(Z_K\\) from \\(\\partial_t\\) near the compact interface to \\(D\\) far out. Along any future unit geodesic eventually proceeding in this end and never crossing \\(S\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt}\\log E_K,\\qquad E_K=-g(Z_K,U)\\asymp\\dot t,\n\\]\nhas integrable absolute value over all outer portions, including arbitrarily many returns.</p>", "proof": "<p>The deformation cost consists of integrable overlap terms, \\(\\psi(V,V)\\), and\n\\[\n|DB|+|\\partial s_s|\n\\leq\n\\frac C{|x_{\\rm av}|}\n\\int_{t-|x_{\\rm av}|/10}^t|p'(v)|\\,dv.\n\\]\nAlong late outer portions \\(|x_{\\rm av}|\\leq2t\\), uniformly over returns. The right side is bounded by\n\\[\nC\\langle t\\rangle^{-A/2}\n\\mathcal M(\\langle\\cdot\\rangle^{A/2}|p'|)(t).\n\\]\nThe maximal \\(L^2\\) inequality and \\(A&gt;1\\) give integrability.</p><p>For \\(t\\geq C_1r\\), strong bulk and radial trace give on \\(r\\asymp R\\)\n\\[\n\\int_{C_1R}^\\infty\nt^A\\sup_{r,\\omega}|\\psi|^2\\,dt\n\\leq CR^{-0.115}F^2.\n\\]\nThe shell sum and time Cauchy–Schwarz are finite.</p><p>For \\(r\\asymp t\\),\n\\[\n|\\psi|\\lesssim r^{-1+e/2}\\langle q/r^{\\delta_f}\\rangle^{-A_H/2},\n\\]\nwhile the improved gauge contractions give\n\\[\n|\\psi(L,L)|+|\\psi(L,e_{\\rm scr})|\n\\lesssim r^{-2+0.01}.\n\\]\nEmitter differentiation and causality imply\n\\[\nq'\\geq-Cr^{-1+0.01},\\qquad\n|v_{\\rm scr}|^2\\leq C(|q'|+r^{-1+0.01}),\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\n|\\psi(V,V)|\n\\leq Cr^{-2+0.01}\n+C|\\psi|(|q'|+r^{-1+0.01}).\n\\]\nWhere \\(|q|\\geq r^\\nu\\), the field is integrable by the positive exponent\n\\(\\tfrac12A_H(\\nu-\\delta_f)-e/2\\).</p><p>Near the cone, clip \\(q\\) on each time dyad to an interval of length \\(O(R^\\nu)\\), extending it by the upper constant during compact excursions. Its negative variation is \\(O(R^{0.01})\\), so\n\\[\n\\int_{\\substack{R\\leq t\\leq2R\\\\|q|\\leq r^\\nu}}\n|q'|\\,dt\\leq C(R^\\nu+R^{0.01}).\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(R^{-1+e/2}\\) is dyadically summable.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**No finite exterior runout — original Proposition 8.5.** A future unit timelike geodesic from \\(\\Sigma\\), inextendible in \\(P_d\\), which never crosses \\(S\\), has infinite future proper duration.", "statement": "<p>**No finite exterior runout — original Proposition 8.5.**</p><p>A future unit timelike geodesic from \\(\\Sigma\\), inextendible in \\(P_d\\), which never crosses \\(S\\), has infinite future proper duration.</p>", "proof": "<p>Such a geodesic eventually proceeds in one end with \\(t_e\\to\\infty\\). Finite pieces have compact regular closure up to bounded-speed far excursions, global hyperbolicity excludes total imprisonment, and bounded-time late portions cannot run out. An interior position limit has bounded causal slopes; the linear parallel-tangent equation in temporal coordinates bounds the tangent and permits continuation.</p><p>Extend \\(Z_K\\) arbitrarily to a smooth future timelike field inside the interface. On a finite track interval, split the absolutely continuous logarithmic energy into outer portions and countably many compact excursions. Lemma 10.1 bounds each excursion increment by the integral of \\(\\rho_c\\), with no fixed return loss. Lemma 10.2 bounds total outer variation. The decomposition is absolutely convergent on finite intervals.</p><p>Thus outer energy stays bounded, and the compact estimate bounds \\(\\dot t\\) between outer visits. Consequently \\(\\dot t\\) is bounded on the late track, and\n\\[\n\\int^\\infty dt/\\dot t=\\infty.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**First-runout classification — original Corollary 8.6.** A future unit geodesic from \\(\\Sigma\\) with finite maximal proper duration in \\(M_d\\), followed through its initial connected portion in \\(P_d\\), crosses \\(S\\) …", "statement": "<p>**First-runout classification — original Corollary 8.6.**</p><p>A future unit geodesic from \\(\\Sigma\\) with finite maximal proper duration in \\(M_d\\), followed through its initial connected portion in \\(P_d\\), crosses \\(S\\) and first runs out at a branch or at the corner\n\\[\ny^0\\to+\\infty,\\qquad y^1\\to+\\infty.\n\\]\nIts first-runout time may precede its maximal time in \\(M_d\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The first-runout time is no later than the finite maximal time. Lemma 10.3 rules out avoiding \\(S\\). Both optical coordinates increase strictly along timelike curves. If both had finite limits, \\(s,x\\) would remain bounded; bounded causal slopes, compactness of the sphere, and the tangent continuation argument would give ordinary continuation. Thus at least one limit is infinite.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Corner ratios — original Lemma 8.7.** On the double-null wedge suppose \\( |\\chi_i|+|\\ell_i+\\kappa|\\leq C(q+g_i(y^i)), \\qquad |b|\\leq C(q+g_0(y^0)), \\qquad g_i\\in L^1(\\mathbb R). \\) For a future unit timelike geodesic …", "statement": "<p>**Corner ratios — original Lemma 8.7.**</p><p>On the double-null wedge suppose\n\\[\n|\\chi_i|+|\\ell_i+\\kappa|\\leq C(q+g_i(y^i)),\n\\qquad\n|b|\\leq C(q+g_0(y^0)),\n\\qquad g_i\\in L^1(\\mathbb R).\n\\]\nFor a future unit timelike geodesic starting at a fixed point, put\n\\[\nY_i=e^{-\\kappa y^i},\\qquad\nU=\\dot y^0L_0+\\dot y^1L_1+\\Xi.\n\\]\nIts remaining maximal wedge track has finite duration, bounded screen velocity, an angular limit, and a branch or corner endpoint. Each \\(|\\dot Y_i|\\) is comparable to its positive initial value.</p><p>There is a tangent-independent \\(\\delta_c(y_{\\rm in}^0,y_{\\rm in}^1)\\to0\\) as both initial coordinates tend to infinity such that\n\\[\ne^{-2\\delta_c}\\frac{|\\dot Y_0^{\\rm in}|}{|\\dot Y_1^{\\rm in}|}\n\\leq\n\\frac{|\\dot Y_0|}{|\\dot Y_1|}\n\\leq\ne^{2\\delta_c}\\frac{|\\dot Y_0^{\\rm in}|}{|\\dot Y_1^{\\rm in}|}.\n\\]\nA corner endpoint necessarily satisfies the same bounds with the middle ratio replaced by \\(Y_0^{\\rm in}/Y_1^{\\rm in}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(p_i=-g(U,L_i)=a\\dot y^j&gt;0\\). Then\n\\[\n1+|\\Xi|_\\gamma^2=2a\\dot y^0\\dot y^1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\dot p_i/p_i\n=\\ell_i\\dot y^i+(\\xi_i-\\xi_j)(\\Xi)\n-\\chi_i(\\Xi,\\Xi)/p_i.\n\\]\nSince \\(|\\Xi|^2/p_i\\leq2\\dot y^i\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac d{d\\tau}\\log(e^{\\kappa y^i}p_i)\\right|\n\\leq C(q+g_i)\\dot y^i+C|\\Xi|.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\ne^{\\kappa y^i}p_i=(q_0/\\kappa)A|\\dot Y_j|,\n\\quad\n1+|\\Xi|^2=(2q_0A/\\kappa^2)|\\dot Y_0||\\dot Y_1|.\n\\]\nMonotonicity and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\int|\\Xi|\\,d\\tau\\leq C\\sqrt{Y_0^{\\rm in}Y_1^{\\rm in}},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int q\\dot y^i\\,d\\tau\n\\leq(q_0/\\kappa)Y_0^{\\rm in}Y_1^{\\rm in},\n\\quad\n\\int g_i\\dot y^i\\,d\\tau\n\\leq\\int_{y_{\\rm in}^i}^\\infty g_i.\n\\]\nTheir sum bounds the logarithmic variation of \\(A|\\dot Y_i|\\); taking ratios cancels \\(A\\). Individual bounds give finite duration and bounded \\(\\Xi\\). The shift integral is finite, so the angular track has finite length. Both \\(Y_i\\) cannot have positive limits, or ordinary continuation would be possible. Integrating the ratio in the corner case gives its necessary condition.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Protected exterior vacuum correction — original Proposition D.1.** For \\(m\\geq0\\), define \\( \\|f\\|_{H_j^m} = \\|f\\|_{H^m(B_2)} + \\sup_{L\\geq1}L^j \\|f(L\\cdot)\\|_{H^m(1<|y|<2)}. \\) For an integer \\(s\\geq3\\), set …", "statement": "<p>**Protected exterior vacuum correction — original Proposition D.1.**</p><p>For \\(m\\geq0\\), define\n\\[\n\\|f\\|_{H_j^m}\n=\n\\|f\\|_{H^m(B_2)}\n+\n\\sup_{L\\geq1}L^j\n\\|f(L\\cdot)\\|_{H^m(1&lt;|y|&lt;2)}.\n\\]\nFor an integer \\(s\\geq3\\), set\n\\[\nY^s=H_4^s(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R\\oplus T^*\\mathbb R^3),\n\\qquad\nX^s=H_1^{s+2}(\\operatorname{Sym}^2)\n\\oplus H_2^{s+1}(\\operatorname{Sym}^2).\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\n\\Phi(g,k)=\n\\bigl(R_g+(\\operatorname{tr}_gk)^2-|k|_g^2,\\\n\\nabla^jk_{ij}-\\nabla_i\\operatorname{tr}_gk\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Suppose \\(d_B=(\\delta+a_B,b_B)\\) is smooth and positive, with all derivatives of \\(a_B,b_B\\) of symbol orders \\(-1,-2\\). Let \\(\\phi\\) be smooth and compactly supported in \\(\\{|y|\\geq1\\}\\), with\n\\[\n\\Phi(d_B)=\\phi\\quad\\text{on }|y|\\geq1.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small\n\\[\n\\beta_s+\\eta_s,\\qquad\n\\beta_s=\\|a_B\\|_{H_1^{s+2}}+\\|b_B\\|_{H_2^{s+1}},\n\\quad\n\\eta_s=\\|\\phi\\|_{Y^s},\n\\]\nthere is a smooth correction \\(v_c\\), supported in \\(\\{|y|\\geq1\\}\\), such that\n\\[\n\\Phi(d_B+v_c)=0\\quad\\text{there},\n\\qquad\n\\|v_c\\|_{X^s}\\leq C_s\\eta_s.\n\\]\nThe corrected metric is positive. The correction is smooth across \\(|y|=1\\) and has the stated symbol weights at every order, without higher-order smallness. No moment conditions are imposed on \\(\\phi\\), no compact support of the correction is asserted, and no constraint equation is required in the artificial inner ball.</p>", "proof": "<p>Define\n\\[\nN(v)=\\Phi(d_B+v)-\\Phi(d_B)-Pv.\n\\]\nWeighted Sobolev products give on a small \\(X^s\\)-ball\n\\[\n\\|N(v)-N(w)\\|_{Y^s}\n\\leq C_s(\\beta_s+R)\\|v-w\\|_{X^s}.\n\\]\nThe scalar nonlinear weights are\n\\[\na\\,\\partial^2a:\\ 1+3=4,\\quad\n(\\partial a)^2:\\ 2+2=4,\\quad b^2:\\ 2+2=4,\n\\]\nand momentum weights are \\(a\\partial b\\) and \\((\\partial a)b\\), also weight four.</p><p>Solve\n\\[\nz_c=-\\phi-N(Kz_c)\n\\]\nby contraction on \\(\\|z_c\\|_{Y^s}\\leq2\\eta_s\\). Iteration preserves exterior support. Then \\(v_c=Kz_c\\) gives exact exterior constraints and the stated estimate.</p><p>For all-order smoothness use rotations and argument dilations at the same base norm. If \\(U_g,V_g\\) are the group actions, define\n\\[\nK_g=U_gKV_g^{-1},\\quad\n\\phi_g=V_g\\phi,\\quad\nN_g(w)=V_gN(U_g^{-1}w).\n\\]\nThe equation\n\\[\nw+K_g\\phi_g+K_gN_g(w)=0\n\\]\nhas an invertible \\(w\\)-derivative by the same smallness. Lemma 11.1 and the smooth background symbols give a smooth implicit-function branch.</p><p>Although dilation moves the support boundary, \\(U_gv_c\\) lies in the common contraction ball and solves this full-space equation. Uniqueness identifies it with the smooth branch without assuming continuity of the unknown orbit in advance.</p><p>Distributional group differentiation gives all iterated rotations and \\(E=y\\cdot\\nabla\\) in \\(X^s\\). Since\n\\[\nr^2\\partial_i=y_iE+\\sum_jy_j\\Omega_{ji},\n\\]\nthey control all scaled derivatives on exterior annuli and near the unit sphere. Thus the correction is smooth across its support boundary and has all symbol orders. Base smallness preserves positivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exterior inverse for flat constraints — original Lemma D.2.** For \\( P(q,p)= (\\partial_i\\partial_jq_{ij}-\\Delta q_{ii},\\ \\partial_jp_{ij}-\\partial_ip_{jj}), \\) there is one linear operator \\(K:Y^s\\to X^s\\), bounded …", "statement": "<p>**Exterior inverse for flat constraints — original Lemma D.2.**</p><p>For\n\\[\nP(q,p)=\n(\\partial_i\\partial_jq_{ij}-\\Delta q_{ii},\\\n\\partial_jp_{ij}-\\partial_ip_{jj}),\n\\]\nthere is one linear operator \\(K:Y^s\\to X^s\\), bounded for every integer \\(s\\geq3\\), such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}z\\subset\\{|y|\\geq1\\}\n\\Longrightarrow\nPKz=z,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{supp}Kz\\subset\\{|y|\\geq1\\}.\n\\]\nIt is defined on all inputs, not merely supported ones. Its conjugates by rotations and positive argument dilations are smooth in operator norm \\(Y^s\\to X^s\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(z=(f,F)\\), solve\n\\[\n-2\\Delta u=f,\\qquad \\Delta W_i=F_i\n\\]\nby Newton potentials, and put\n\\[\nq_{0,ij}=u\\delta_{ij},\n\\qquad\np_{0,ij}=\\partial_iW_j+\\partial_jW_i\n-\\tfrac12\\delta_{ij}\\partial_kW_k.\n\\]\nThen \\(P(q_0,p_0)=z\\). Splitting the Newton integral near the origin, near \\(y\\), and elsewhere gives \\(u,W=O(\\langle y\\rangle^{-1}\\|z\\|_{Y^s})\\). Rescaled elliptic estimates give\n\\[\n\\|(q_0,p_0)\\|_{X^s}\\leq C_s\\|z\\|_{Y^s}.\n\\]</p><p>Choose radial \\(\\chi=0\\) for \\(r\\leq1.2\\), \\(\\chi=1\\) for \\(r\\geq1.8\\), and define\n\\[\nG=P((\\chi-1)(q_0,p_0)).\n\\]\nThen \\(P(\\chi(q_0,p_0))=z+G\\). Integration by parts gives zero scalar constant/linear moments and zero vector translation/rotation moments. For exterior-supported \\(z\\), \\(G\\) is supported in the closed annulus \\(A=\\{1\\leq r\\leq2\\}\\).</p><p>Construct an annular divergence primitive. For a unit bump \\(\\zeta\\),\n\\[\n(D_\\zeta e)(x)\n=\n\\int e(v)(x-v)\n\\int_1^\\infty\\zeta(v+t(x-v))t^2\\,dt\\,dv.\n\\]\nIts support lies in segments joining the source to the bump, and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}D_\\zeta e=e-\\zeta\\int e.\n\\]\nCover \\(A\\) by narrow angular caps and choose cap bumps near radius \\(3/2\\) so all joining segments stay in \\(A\\). Transfer their masses to one fixed bump along paths in the open annulus. This gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}De=e-\\zeta_0\\int e,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{supp}De\\subset A,\n\\qquad\n\\|De\\|_{H^{m+1}}\\leq C_m\\|e\\|_{H^m}.\n\\]\nFor the derivative estimate, the kernel is a smooth coefficient times a degree-\\(-2\\) homogeneous kernel plus a smooth remainder. Its derivative is a bounded multiplication term plus a mean-zero degree-\\(-3\\) singular integral. Spherical-harmonic expansion and the classical \\(L^2\\) bound give one derivative. Higher orders follow from\n\\[\n\\partial_jD_\\zeta e\n=D_\\zeta(\\partial_je)+D_{\\partial_j\\zeta}e.\n\\]</p><p>For a vector \\(F'\\) with zero translation/rotation moments, let\n\\[\nT_{ij}=(DF_i')_j,\\qquad\nD_{ijk}=(D(T_{ij}-T_{ji}))_k,\n\\]\n\\[\nH_{ijk}=\\tfrac12(D_{ijk}-D_{jki}+D_{kij}),\n\\qquad\nS_{ij}=T_{ij}-\\partial_kH_{ijk}.\n\\]\nThen \\(H_{ijk}=-H_{ikj}\\), \\(H_{ijk}-H_{jik}=D_{ijk}\\), so \\(S\\) is symmetric and \\(\\partial_jS_{ij}=F_i'\\). Inverse trace reversal\n\\[\np_{ij}=S_{ij}-\\tfrac12\\delta_{ij}S_{kk}\n\\]\ngives the momentum primitive with one derivative gain.</p><p>For scalar \\(f'\\) with zero constant/linear moments, let \\(Y=Df'\\). Its translation moments vanish. Correct its rotation moments by adding\n\\[\n\\partial_j(C_{ij}\\zeta_0),\\qquad\nC_{ab}=-\\tfrac12\\int(y_aY_b-y_bY_a).\n\\]\nApply the symmetric divergence primitive to the corrected \\(Y\\), then inverse trace reversal, obtaining the scalar primitive with two derivative gain.</p><p>Denote the combined annular primitive by \\(\\mathcal B\\), extended to arbitrary inputs by fixed localization and final symmetrization, without claiming exactness off the moment-free supported subspace. Set\n\\[\nKz=\\chi(q_0,p_0)-\\mathcal BG.\n\\]\nThis has the asserted support and exactness.</p><p>For \\(T_\\lambda e(y)=e(\\lambda y)\\),\n\\[\nT_\\lambda D_\\zeta T_\\lambda^{-1}\n=\\lambda D_{\\zeta_\\lambda},\n\\qquad \\zeta_\\lambda(y)=\\lambda^3\\zeta(\\lambda y).\n\\]\nBumps, cutoffs, paths, moment functionals, and Newton factors vary smoothly under rotations and dilation. The kernel bounds use finitely many smooth seminorms, proving operator-norm smoothness with full gain.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite triangular comparison with one common rate — original Lemma C.1.** Fix \\(k\\geq0\\), \\(C_0>0\\), and \\(0<L_T\\leq C_0(1+T)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Finite triangular comparison with one common rate — original Lemma C.1.**</p><p>Fix \\(k\\geq0\\), \\(C_0&gt;0\\), and \\(0&lt;L_T\\leq C_0(1+T)\\). Let nonnegative absolutely continuous \\(E_0,\\dots,E_k\\) on \\([0,L_T]\\) satisfy\n\\[\nE_j'\\leq r_TE_j+b_T\\sum_{\\ell&lt;j}E_\\ell+S_j,\n\\]\nwhere \\(r_T,b_T,S_j\\) are nonnegative integrable functions.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nR_T(t)=\\int_0^tr_T,\\qquad\nB_T(t)=\\int_0^tb_T,\\qquad\nQ_k(B)=\\sum_{q=0}^k\\frac{((k+1)B)^q}{q!}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\max_{j\\leq k}E_j(t)\n\\leq e^{R_T(t)}Q_k(B_T(t))\n\\left[\n\\max_{j\\leq k}E_j(0)\n+\\int_0^te^{-R_T(s)}\\max_{j\\leq k}S_j(s)\\,ds\n\\right].\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(k\\), \\(B_T(L_T)\\leq e^{o(T)}\\) implies \\(Q_k(B_T(L_T))=e^{o(T)}\\). A uniform bound \\(b_T(t)\\leq e^{o(T)}\\) suffices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(\\widetilde E_j=e^{-R_T}E_j\\). Let \\(A\\) be the strictly lower-triangular matrix with entries \\(A_{j\\ell}=1\\) for \\(\\ell&lt;j\\). Positive-system comparison gives propagator\n\\[\n\\exp((B_T(t)-B_T(s))A).\n\\]\nSince \\(A^{k+1}=0\\) and \\(\\|A\\|_{\\ell^\\infty\\to\\ell^\\infty}\\leq k+1\\), its norm is bounded by \\(Q_k(B_T(t))\\). Variation of constants proves the formula. Only finitely many lower-order integrations occur, with one common exponential factor.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Deep packet, exact interior comparison, and tidal observation — original Proposition 9.4 and Lemma 9.5, with the construction of Section 9.2.** Let \\(g=g_d\\) be a fixed smooth background in the common base …", "statement": "<p>**Deep packet, exact interior comparison, and tidal observation — original Proposition 9.4 and Lemma 9.5, with the construction of Section 9.2.**</p><p>Let \\(g=g_d\\) be a fixed smooth background in the common base neighborhood. Suppose a future unit geodesic has bounded parallel curvature and first runout at a noncorner branch. Exchange labels if necessary so\n\\[\nu=y^0\\to u_*&lt;\\infty,\\qquad v=y^1\\to+\\infty.\n\\]\nLet \\(P_T\\) be its point \\(v=T\\). Fix \\(u_2&gt;u_*\\), \\(c_2&gt;0\\), and small \\(c_0&gt;0\\). Use\n\\[\n0\\leq s\\leq(T+u_*)/2+c_0,\\qquad\nu+c_0s/T\\leq u_2,\\qquad\nv+c_0s/T\\leq T+c_2.\n\\]\nAll constructions use enlarged domains and buffers.</p><p>For any prescribed tidal threshold \\(J\\), fixed mixed comparison order \\(k\\geq10\\), and sufficiently small rate tolerance \\(\\delta_2&gt;0\\), there is an oscillatory approximate perturbation \\(h^p\\) with amplitude \\(A_T\\) and frequency \\(\\lambda_p\\) as below. If exact entry jets satisfy the vacuum constraints and wave-map gauge, agree with \\(g\\) for \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu_0)T\\), and differ from the jets of \\(g+h^p\\) by at most\n\\[\ne^{-7\\kappa T/2}\\quad\\text{in }H^{k+1}\\times H^k,\n\\]\nthen, under the strict exponential margins specified below, they have an exact vacuum development on the domain with normalized error bounded by\n\\[\nCe^{(\\kappa+\\delta_2)T}\n\\left(e^{-7\\kappa T/2}\n+A_T\\lambda_p^{-M_1}\n+A_T^2\\lambda_p^{P_*}\\right).\n\\]\nIt controls normalized \\(C^2\\) error after fixed buffers.</p><p>At \\(P_T\\), the exact metric has a future unit tangent \\(U'\\) and unit spacelike \\(X'\\perp U'\\) with\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Riem}(U',X',U',X')|&gt;J.\n\\]\nThe corresponding backward geodesic reaches the entry cylinder with state converging to the background entry state.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 13.1 supplies the exponential optical frequency. Lemma 13.2 gives the normalized coefficient and curvature control used by the constrained hierarchy of Lemma 13.3 and the exact energy comparison of Lemma 13.4. Lemma 13.5 turns the normalized leading curvature term into the prescribed physical tidal size and establishes backward entry-state convergence. Formal accuracy and the small frequency exponent are chosen later, in the order recorded in Proposition 16.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Branch normalization — original Lemma 9.2.** Write \\( g=-2a\\,du\\,dv+\\gamma(d\\theta-b\\,du)^2, \\qquad a=q_0e^{-\\kappa(u+v)}A. \\) Along the branch let …", "statement": "<p>**Branch normalization — original Lemma 9.2.**</p><p>Write\n\\[\ng=-2a\\,du\\,dv+\\gamma(d\\theta-b\\,du)^2,\n\\qquad\na=q_0e^{-\\kappa(u+v)}A.\n\\]\nAlong the branch let\n\\[\n\\Xi=\\dot\\theta-b\\dot u,\\qquad\np=a\\dot v,\\qquad\nP=\\gamma\\Xi,\\qquad\n\\pi_u=-p-b\\cdot P.\n\\]\nThen \\(p\\asymp1\\), \\(P,\\Xi,\\dot u\\) are bounded, and\n\\[\nY_T:=\\dot v(P_T)\\asymp e^{\\kappa T}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Unit normalization gives \\(1+|\\Xi|^2=2p\\dot u\\), and the momentum equation gives\n\\[\n|(\\log p)^{\\boldsymbol\\cdot}|\n\\leq C(\\dot u+|\\Xi|).\n\\]\nThe integral of \\(\\dot u\\) is finite, and\n\\[\n\\int|\\Xi|\\,d\\tau\n\\leq C\\left(\\int du\\right)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int a\\,dv\\right)^{1/2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nHence \\(p\\asymp1\\).</p><p>With \\(v\\) as time, the unit-shell Hamiltonian is\n\\[\n-\\pi_v=\\frac{a(1+|P|_{\\gamma^{-1}}^2)}{2p},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{du}{dv}=\n\\frac{a(1+|P|_{\\gamma^{-1}}^2)}{2p^2}.\n\\]\nLow coefficient bounds give\n\\[\n|dP/dv|\\leq C(p+|P|)\\,du/dv.\n\\]\nFinite total \\(u\\)-variation and Gronwall bound \\(P\\), and then \\(\\Xi,\\dot u\\). Finally \\(a(P_T)\\asymp e^{-\\kappa T}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Normalized coefficient inputs — original Lemma 9.3.** Use \\( \\widehat e_i=a^{-1/2}L_i,\\quad \\widehat e_A=\\partial_A,\\quad Z_\\alpha=\\sqrt a\\,\\widehat e_\\alpha,\\quad W_t=L_0+L_1,\\quad W_x=L_1-L_0. \\) The connection …", "statement": "<p>**Normalized coefficient inputs — original Lemma 9.3.**</p><p>Use\n\\[\n\\widehat e_i=a^{-1/2}L_i,\\quad\n\\widehat e_A=\\partial_A,\\quad\nZ_\\alpha=\\sqrt a\\,\\widehat e_\\alpha,\\quad\nW_t=L_0+L_1,\\quad W_x=L_1-L_0.\n\\]\nThe connection matrices \\(V_\\alpha\\) of \\(\\nabla_{Z_\\alpha}\\) on this normalized basis are bounded and, as \\(\\min(v,-u,s)\\to\\infty\\),\n\\[\nV_0\\to D_0=\\operatorname{diag}(-\\kappa/2,\\kappa/2,0,0),\n\\quad\nV_1\\to-D_0,\\quad V_A\\to0.\n\\]\nContracted differentiated connection matrices in \\(a\\Box_g\\) and normalized entries of \\(a\\operatorname{Riem}(g)\\) are bounded and small in that limit. Undifferentiated connection products retain their radial diagonal constants.</p><p>Also\n\\[\nb,Db,W_t\\gamma,W_t\\log a+2\\kappa\\to0,\n\\]\nwhere here \\(Db\\) means all first ordinary coordinate derivatives. Every fixed ordinary derivative of the normalized arrays is \\(e^{o(T)}\\) on the required domains. Derivatives of \\(a,\\sqrt a,\\partial_vb\\) retain their respective factors.</p>", "proof": "<p>The complete connection formulas include\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_i}\\widehat e_i=\\tfrac12\\ell_i\\widehat e_i,\n\\quad\n\\nabla_{L_i}\\widehat e_j\n=-\\tfrac12\\ell_i\\widehat e_j+\\sqrt a\\,\\xi_i^A\\partial_A,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nabla_{L_i}\\partial_A\n=(\\chi_{i,A}{}^B-\\partial_AB_i^B)\\partial_B\n+\\sqrt a\\,\\xi_{i,A}\\widehat e_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nabla_{Z_A}\\widehat e_i\n=\\chi_{i,A}{}^B\\partial_B+\n\\tfrac12\\sqrt a(\\xi_i-\\xi_j)_A\\widehat e_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\nabla_{Z_A}\\partial_B\n=\\sqrt a\\,\\Gamma^C_{AB}\\partial_C\n+\\chi_{0,AB}\\widehat e_1+\\chi_{1,AB}\\widehat e_0.\n\\]\nUse the low and pure limits of Proposition 8. Curvature follows from the exact matrix identity\n\\[\naR(\\widehat e_\\alpha,\\widehat e_\\beta)\n=\nZ_\\alpha V_\\beta-Z_\\beta V_\\alpha\n+[V_\\alpha,V_\\beta]-V_{[Z_\\alpha,Z_\\beta]}.\n\\]\nPure longitudinal curvature uses the controlled \\(\\mathcal L_i\\chi_i\\); torsion derivatives reduce to the null equations. The remaining constant tensor-wave term is\n\\[\n-(L_0+C)(L_1-C)-(L_1-C)(L_0+C),\n\\]\nwhere a tensor entry with \\(n_i\\) radial \\(i\\)-slots has\n\\(c_n=\\kappa(n_0-n_1)/2\\). Higher estimates on the one-flank strip give all fixed derivative bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Constrained interior packet — Section 9.2.** Let \\(P\\) be the trace-reversed linearized wave-map reduced tensor wave operator and \\(C=\\operatorname{div}_g\\), so \\(CP=P_1C\\).", "statement": "<p>**Constrained interior packet — Section 9.2.**</p><p>Let \\(P\\) be the trace-reversed linearized wave-map reduced tensor wave operator and \\(C=\\operatorname{div}_g\\), so \\(CP=P_1C\\). Set\n\\[\nk_v=g^{-1}dv=-a^{-1}L_0,\\quad\nK_vh=h(k_v,\\cdot),\\quad\n\\mathcal T=2\\nabla_{k_v}+\\Box_gv.\n\\]\nFor any sufficiently high finite hierarchy order, amplitudes \\(p_j\\) solve\n\\[\ni\\mathcal Tp_j+Pp_{j-1}=0,\\qquad\niK_vp_j+Cp_{j-1}=0,\\qquad p_{-1}=0\n\\]\non \\(-v\\leq u\\leq u_f\\), with \\(u_f&gt;u_2\\), \\(v\\) near \\(T\\). Their normalized fixed jets are \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p><p>There is a screen contraction \\(\\sigma_T\\) with two-sided \\(e^{o(T)}\\) size. For\n\\[\n\\lambda_p=e^{\\zeta T},\\qquad\nA_T=\\frac{4J_{\\rm amp}}{Y_T^2\\lambda_p^2\\sigma_T},\n\\]\nchoose\n\\[\nh^p=\nA_T\\operatorname{tr.rev.}_g\n\\operatorname{Re}\n\\left(e^{i\\lambda_pv}\\sum_{j=0}^L\\lambda_p^{-j}p_j\\right),\n\\]\nwith phase fixed at \\(P_T\\). Here \\(J_{\\rm amp}\\) satisfies\n\\(2J_{\\rm amp}&gt;J+C_w+2\\), where \\(C_w\\) bounds background unit tidal contraction.</p><p>At fixed base comparison orders, the normalized reduced residual, multiplied by \\(a\\), is\n\\[\ne^{o(T)}\n\\left(A_T\\lambda_p^{-M_1}\n+A_T^2\\lambda_p^{P_*}\\right).\n\\]\nIncreasing finite formal accuracy makes \\(M_1\\) arbitrarily large without increasing the fixed positive exponent \\(P_*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiplication of transport by \\(-a/2\\) gives \\(L_0\\)-transport plus area expansion. Normalized entry blocks have diagonal \\(L_0+c_n\\) and couple only to higher weights.</p><p>At \\(u=-v\\), determine \\(00,0A,01\\) entries from gauge and give \\(AB\\) free data: a compact angular bump times tracefree polarization for \\(j=0\\), zero for \\(j&gt;0\\). Give \\(1A,11\\) zero terminal values at \\(u=u_f\\). Solve descending weights, forward for nonnegative and backward for negative weights. The constant diagonal signs are favorable.</p><p>For \\(q_j=iK_vp_j+Cp_{j-1}\\), conjugated Bianchi gives\n\\[\ni\\mathcal T_1q_j+P_1q_{j-1}=0.\n\\]\nIts zero initial data imply \\(q_j=0\\). The contraction uses only entries with a \\(0\\)-slot, so terminal choices do not overprescribe gauge.</p><p>The full strip lies in Proposition 8’s bounded-\\(u\\) expanding region. Small-average angular transport and higher coefficient induction give subexponential jets and inverse flow. The leading screen tensor is parallel transported times area amplitude and has two-sided subexponential size. Choose \\(X\\perp U\\), \\(dv(X)=0\\), \\(g(X,X)=1\\), to realize a diagonal screen contraction of that size.</p><p>Successive phase and transport cancellations give the linear inverse-frequency residual. Nonlinear relative-field products in scaled covariant derivatives are quadratic, with no inverse-lapse loss in normalized arrays.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exact deep comparison — original Proposition 9.4.** For sufficiently small fixed \\(\\delta_2>0\\), choose a mass parameter \\(\\varepsilon_0>0\\), then \\(\\nu_0>0\\), sufficiently small.", "statement": "<p>**Exact deep comparison — original Proposition 9.4.**</p><p>For sufficiently small fixed \\(\\delta_2&gt;0\\), choose a mass parameter \\(\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\), then \\(\\nu_0&gt;0\\), sufficiently small. Under the exact-entry assumptions of Proposition 13, the normalized error \\(F=g'-g-h^p\\) satisfies the stated bound in through-\\(k\\) mixed energies.</p><p>For a normalized entry \\(f\\) of weight \\(c_n\\), the reference slice density is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\left(\n|W_tf|^2+|(W_x-2c_n)f|^2\n+2a|d_\\theta f|_\\gamma^2+\\varepsilon_0^2|f|^2\n\\right)dx\\,d\\operatorname{vol}_\\gamma.\n\\]\nThe energy sums this density over ordinary derivatives through \\(k\\) and all entries. The bound must have a fixed exponential margin below \\(A_T\\), and the fixed base packet norms must be exponentially small.</p>", "proof": "<p>Bootstrap error energies by \\(e^{-(2\\kappa+c')T}\\). Relative cone change is exponentially small, making all side cuts strict outflow. Finite propagation keeps the exact change in\n\\(v\\geq(1-\\nu_0)T-o(1)\\).</p><p>For an ordinary commuted normalized entry retain the scalar drift\n\\[\ne^{2c_nx}a\\Box_{g'}(e^{-2c_nx}f).\n\\]\nFirst coefficient hits at top order have bounded coefficients small in the bulk; higher hits multiply lower energies with \\(e^{o(T)}\\) coefficients. In particular, a first hit on \\(b\\partial_A\\partial_v\\) is\n\\[\n(Db)\\partial_v(\\partial_AD^{j-1}F),\n\\]\nwith small \\(Db\\), while a first angular-principal hit retains \\(\\sqrt a\\). Perturbative highest-error placements may lose \\(a^{-1/2}\\leq Ce^{\\kappa T/2}\\); exponentially small packet/error factors absorb this after \\(\\zeta\\) is sufficiently small.</p><p>Apply massive scalar stress energy to \\(e^{-2c_nx}f\\), with mass squared \\(\\varepsilon_0^2/(2a)\\) and multiplier \\(e^{4c_nx}W_t\\). Limiting bulk terms are\n\\[\n-4c_nW_tf(W_x-2c_n)f\n-2\\kappa a|d_\\theta f|^2+\\varepsilon_0^2fW_tf.\n\\]\nSince \\(|c_n|\\leq\\kappa\\), the square-root energy rate is \\(2\\kappa\\) plus arbitrarily small in the bulk, bounded elsewhere.</p><p>The total interval is \\(T/2+O(1)\\). Outside \\(\\min(v,-u,s)\\geq B\\), support persists for only \\(O(1)+O(\\nu_0T)\\) time. Choose mass, threshold, and \\(\\nu_0\\) so the integrated rate is at most \\((\\kappa+\\delta_2)T\\), with surplus.</p><p>The energy system is triangular in commutation order. Proposition 12 makes the lower-order factor \\(e^{o(T)}\\) without multiplying the common exponential rate. Initial mixed jets follow from the entry equation. The strict improvement gives continuation on finite nondegenerate domains; homogeneous gauge propagation gives vacuum. Fixed Sobolev buffers yield normalized \\(C^2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tidal size and backward state — original Lemma 9.5.** Under the strict comparison margins, the observation and backward-state conclusions of Proposition 13 hold.", "statement": "<p>**Tidal size and backward state — original Lemma 9.5.**</p><p>Under the strict comparison margins, the observation and backward-state conclusions of Proposition 13 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(H=U/Y_T\\). In normalized primal slots \\(H=O(\\sqrt a)\\), \\(X=O(1)\\), while \\(dv(H)=1,dv(X)=0\\). The phase–phase curvature term is\n\\[\n\\tfrac12A_T\\lambda_p^2\\sigma_T.\n\\]\nOther packet terms cost\n\\[\ne^{o(T)}(A_T\\lambda_p+A_T^2\\lambda_p^{P_*}),\n\\]\nand comparison terms cost its error bound times \\(e^{o(T)}\\). Multiplication by \\(a\\) converts curvature derivatives to the scaled fields; the two \\(H\\)-slots cancel that scaling. A normalized near-identity frame isometry produces \\(U',X'\\); its errors are also \\(o(Y_T^{-2})\\). Multiply by \\(Y_T^2\\) and use the definition of \\(A_T,J_{\\rm amp}\\).</p><p>For backward flow use the unit-shell Hamiltonian of Lemma 13.1 on a compact state tube with \\(p\\) bounded below. The background Lipschitz cost is \\(ae^{o(v)}\\), integrable on the tail. Solve the perturbed shell with \\(\\pi_v=az'\\). Its root derivative stays nonzero. Terminal errors satisfy\n\\[\n\\delta(\\pi_u,P)=O(A_Te^{o(T)}/\\sqrt a),\n\\qquad\n\\delta(\\pi_v/a)=O(A_Te^{o(T)}/a).\n\\]\nNormalized coefficient changes are exponentially smaller than \\(a\\) on bounded \\(u\\), so the Hamiltonian vector-field error is exponentially small. Integrate over length \\(O(T)\\) and use Gronwall with the integrable background majorant. The track converges through the transverse entry hit and remains inside side margins; earlier fixed pieces use compact flow dependence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Return rays and constrained exterior beams — original Propositions 9.6 and 9.7.** Use the background, branch packet, and entry patch of Proposition 13.", "statement": "<p>**Return rays and constrained exterior beams — original Propositions 9.6 and 9.7.**</p><p>Use the background, branch packet, and entry patch of Proposition 13. On its selected exterior end orient the prepared parameter so\n\\[\nI'(t)=\\lambda_-\\varsigma&gt;0,\\qquad I(t_T)=T,\\qquad t_T\\asymp T.\n\\]\nLet \\(z_*=C_e(t_T)\\).</p><p>For every prescribed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), sufficiently large fixed radius and then large \\(T\\), null rays launched by \\(dv\\), followed to the past, reach the original initial end in\n\\[\n(1-\\alpha)t_T&lt;|z-z_*|&lt;(1+\\alpha)t_T.\n\\]\nTheir entry crossing is unique and transverse, with separation from entry outside a uniform collar. Fixed phase-space flow jets on all subsegments are \\(e^{o(T)}\\); tensor parallel transport and inverse are bounded.</p><p>For every fixed base buffer \\(N_b\\) and inverse accuracy \\(M_1\\), sufficiently high finite complex phase and amplitude orders produce a real exterior perturbation \\(h^B\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|h^B\\|_{C^{N_b}}\n\\leq A_T\\lambda_p^{N_b+3/2}e^{o(T)},\n\\]\nand reduced-wave and gauge residuals are\n\\[\ne^{o(T)}\n\\left(A_T\\lambda_p^{-M_1}\n+A_T^2\\lambda_p^{P_0}\\right),\n\\qquad P_0=2N_b+7.\n\\]\nIts entry jets match \\(h^p\\) with linear error\n\\(A_T\\lambda_p^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}\\). Its original-slice support lies in the displayed annulus up to thin tube margins. Increasing formal accuracy does not increase \\(P_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 14.1 supplies the rays, and Lemmas 14.2–14.4 give the constrained superposition.</p><p>For last amplitude index \\(L\\), desired spatial degree \\(D\\), and mixed-time buffer \\(k_0\\), choose finite degrees\n\\[\nN_j=D+2k_0+2(L-j)+2,\n\\]\nand enough additional phase degrees for all eikonal remainders. A wave source uses two preceding-amplitude jets and gauge one, so this backwards degree assignment suffices.</p><p>Localize in tubes of radius \\(e^{-b_pT}\\), \\(0&lt;b_p&lt;\\zeta/2\\). Gaussian width is smaller; cutoff errors are Gaussian-small. A Taylor remainder of degree \\(d\\) gains \\(\\lambda_p^{-d/2}e^{o(T)}\\). Stop beams after the strict inner exit. There are no other entry crossings.</p><p>Absolute center integration costs \\(\\lambda_p^{3/2}\\), giving the base norm. Quadratic nonlinear residuals cost the square of the base norm with two extra derivatives, yielding \\(P_0=2N_b+7\\). Increasing formal accuracy introduces only finite subexponential coefficient bounds and nonpositive frequency powers, not a larger fixed base loss.</p><p>Take real parts, undo trace reversal, and multiply by \\(A_T\\). Gauss–Codazzi and wave/gauge residuals give the compactly supported constraint defect of the original-slice beam seed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Backward escape and flow bounds — original Proposition 9.6.** The real-ray conclusions of Proposition 14 hold, including short launch margins and continuation through the deeper inner exit.", "statement": "<p>**Backward escape and flow bounds — original Proposition 9.6.**</p><p>The real-ray conclusions of Proposition 14 hold, including short launch margins and continuation through the deeper inner exit.</p>", "proof": "<p>On compact co-moving supports, \\(g\\to G_{p_\\infty}\\) in the required low differentiated orders. Prepared launch covectors approach\n\\[\n\\lambda_-d(r_*+F+T_K).\n\\]\nAccumulated phase offsets do not affect this Jacobian statement.</p><p>The frozen covector has zero axial momentum and energy-normalized radial potential\n\\[\n(r^2+a^2)^2-\\Delta\n\\bigl(|\\partial_\\vartheta F|^2+a^2\\sin^2\\vartheta\\bigr)_{\\rm init}.\n\\]\nThe depth choice in Proposition 8 makes the bracket \\(&lt;4a^2\\). For \\(r\\geq r_+\\),\n\\[\n\\Delta&lt;r^2,\\qquad (r^2+a^2)^2/r^2\\geq4a^2,\n\\]\nso the potential is strictly positive. It is also positive between horizons. The past-directed \\(g^{-1}dv\\) has increasing radius and crosses the event horizon transversely in regular ingoing coordinates.</p><p>Near entry \\(Q=(r-r_-)f\\), with \\(f&gt;0\\). Bounded \\(d\\vartheta/dr\\) and a sufficiently small profile neighborhood make \\(dQ/dr&gt;0\\); other used compact portions are separated from entry. Compact convergence gives unique transverse crossing and bounded regular-time compact transit.</p><p>At large fixed radius the ray is approximately outward radial in past laboratory time. Far bounds give\n\\[\n|g-\\eta|\\lesssim r^{-1+0.01},\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\partial g|\n\\lesssim r^{-2+0.01}\n+r^{-1+e/2}\\langle q/r^{\\delta_f}\\rangle^{-A_H/2},\n\\qquad\n|\\partial^2g|=o_{r\\to\\infty}(1).\n\\]\nAlong the outward cone, \\(q\\) decreases at a rate bounded below. On a radial dyad the second term integrates to\n\\[\nO(R_f^{(e-(1-\\delta_f)A_H)/2}),\n\\]\nwith negative exponent. Integrated connection cost is small after the fixed radius is large. Velocity and frequency remain nondegenerate until \\(t=0\\), and velocity integration gives the annulus.</p><p>For phase-space variations, position-to-momentum and diagonal blocks tend to zero far out; momentum-to-position is bounded. Rescale position variation by a small fixed factor, then enlarge radius, making the homogeneous rate arbitrarily small. Compact transit has fixed duration. Higher variation induction uses the same propagator and subexponential lower products, including launch-center derivatives. Integrated connection bounds control tensor transport and inverse.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Complex phase and amplitude transport — first stage of original Proposition 9.7.** Gaussian beams centered on the entry patch can be propagated along the return rays through real caustics with positive imaginary …", "statement": "<p>**Complex phase and amplitude transport — first stage of original Proposition 9.7.**</p><p>Gaussian beams centered on the entry patch can be propagated along the return rays through real caustics with positive imaginary Hessian. Phase, amplitude, inverse graph, and all fixed finite jets have \\(e^{o(T)}\\) all-subsegment bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>For entry coordinates \\(w_s=(x,\\theta)\\), launch at center \\(Y\\) the phase\n\\[\nv(0,w_s)+\\tfrac i2|w_s-Y|^2,\n\\]\nwith temporal root agreeing with \\(dv\\). The complex gradient graph has differential blocks \\(P_c,Q_c\\) satisfying\n\\[\nP_c^*Q_c-Q_c^*P_c=2i\\,\\operatorname{Im}M_c.\n\\]\nLemma 14.1’s subsegment bounds imply \\(P_c^{-1}=e^{o(T)}\\); the propagated imaginary Hessian is positive with subexponential inverse.</p><p>The central amplitude is tensor parallel transport times the inverse square root of the density ratio formed from\n\\[\n\\sqrt{|\\det g|}\\,k^{t_e}\\det P_c.\n\\]\nHigher transport jets have the same homogeneous propagator with lower-jet sources. All constructions use finite smooth Taylor jets at real positions, not spatial analyticity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Wave-gauge compatibility of beam jets — second stage of original Proposition 9.7.** The finite complex phase-amplitude jets can satisfy both wave transports and linearized wave-gauge equations, while retaining free …", "statement": "<p>**Wave-gauge compatibility of beam jets — second stage of original Proposition 9.7.**</p><p>The finite complex phase-amplitude jets can satisfy both wave transports and linearized wave-gauge equations, while retaining free contraction-kernel data for matching.</p>", "proof": "<p>For complex phase \\(\\phi\\), let\n\\[\nh_\\phi=g^{-1}(d\\phi,d\\phi),\\quad\nP_\\lambda=P+i\\lambda\\mathcal T-\\lambda^2h_\\phi,\n\\quad\nC_\\lambda=C+i\\lambda K.\n\\]\nExact conjugated Bianchi gives \\(C_\\lambda P_\\lambda=P_{1,\\lambda}C_\\lambda\\).</p><p>Supply enough eikonal degrees that all required differentiated \\(h_\\phi\\)-jets vanish in the finite quotient. For amplitudes \\(r_{j,Y}\\), put\n\\[\nW_j=i\\mathcal Tr_{j,Y}+Pr_{j-1,Y},\n\\qquad\nq_j=iKr_{j,Y}+Cr_{j-1,Y}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\niKW_j+CW_{j-1}=i\\mathcal T_1q_j+P_1q_{j-1}.\n\\]\nOnce lower defects vanish, wave transport makes \\(q_j\\) homogeneous. At launch contraction by \\(k\\) has right inverse\n\\[\nf\\longmapsto l\\otimes f+f\\otimes l-f(k)l\\otimes l,\n\\qquad l(k)=1.\n\\]\nUse it also in formal jets to prescribe forced gauge components and leave projected kernel jets free.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Triangular entry matching — third stage of original Proposition 9.7.** After Gaussian superposition with measure \\( (\\lambda_p/2\\pi)^{3/2}\\,dY, \\) free central contraction-kernel values can be chosen successively so …", "statement": "<p>**Triangular entry matching — third stage of original Proposition 9.7.**</p><p>After Gaussian superposition with measure\n\\[\n(\\lambda_p/2\\pi)^{3/2}\\,dY,\n\\]\nfree central contraction-kernel values can be chosen successively so that the integrated beam coefficients match the real-phase interior hierarchy, including the required normal Cauchy jets.</p>", "proof": "<p>In a short entry collar center projection is a diffeomorphism. Gaussian moments give an integer inverse-frequency expansion\n\\[\ne^{i\\lambda_pv}\\sum_j\\lambda_p^{-j}p_j.\n\\]\nOn the initial surface the order-\\(j\\) coefficient is its new central value \\(a_j(Y)=r_{j,Y}(0,Y)\\) plus terms already fixed by earlier jets. Derivatives of \\(a_j\\) enter one order later because odd moments vanish.</p><p>For fixed \\(T\\), compare coefficients after letting an auxiliary frequency tend to infinity in sufficiently accurate wave and gauge residuals. The integrated coefficients satisfy both real-phase hierarchies. Once lower coefficients match, the forced gauge terms at order \\(j\\) agree. The discrepancy is in the contraction kernel, so its free central value cancels it. Extend it in spatial jets using \\(I-R_kK\\), preserving support and subexponential bounds.</p><p>The real-phase transport is noncharacteristic at entry; agreement of sources and coefficient value fixes the same normal derivatives. Thus value and normal-jet matching are not independent prescriptions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Exact corrected data and ordinary exterior transfer — original Proposition 9.9.** For the beam seed of Proposition 14, there are smooth complete exact vacuum data \\(d_T\\in\\mathcal D\\), agreeing with \\(d\\) on a large …", "statement": "<p>**Exact corrected data and ordinary exterior transfer — original Proposition 9.9.**</p><p>For the beam seed of Proposition 14, there are smooth complete exact vacuum data \\(d_T\\in\\mathcal D\\), agreeing with \\(d\\) on a large protected inner ball and on the other end, with the permitted noncompact decaying correction tail.</p><p>Use ordinary wave-map reduction with fixed target \\(g=g_d\\):\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ric}(\\widetilde g)_{\\mu\\nu}\n+\\widetilde\\nabla_{(\\mu}\\Upsilon_{\\nu)}(\\widetilde g;g)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Upsilon_\\mu(\\widetilde g;g)\n=\n-\\widetilde g_{\\mu\\lambda}\\widetilde g^{\\alpha\\beta}\n\\bigl(\\Gamma(\\widetilde g)^\\lambda_{\\alpha\\beta}\n-\\Gamma(g)^\\lambda_{\\alpha\\beta}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThere are no nonlocal gauge additions or modulation feedback in this comparison.</p><p>For every small \\(\\delta_3&gt;0\\), localization and far thresholds can be chosen so the exact exterior solution exists through \\(t_e=t_T+C_2\\), inside\n\\[\n|y|&lt;R_T(t_e)\n=\nC_{\\rm out}(1+T)+\nV_{\\rm out}(t_T+C_2-t_e),\n\\]\nand its error from \\(g+h^B\\) is\n\\[\nCe^{\\delta_3T}\n\\left(A_T\\lambda_p^{-M_1}\n+A_T^2\\lambda_p^{P_0}\\right)\n\\]\nin the required fixed mixed norms.</p><p>It equals \\(g\\) on the earlier needed compact-in-radius region up to \\(t_T-\\alpha'T\\), where \\(\\alpha'&gt;0\\) can be chosen sufficiently small. With the final parameter choices its entry jets agree exactly with \\(g\\) for \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu_0)T\\) and otherwise satisfy Proposition 13’s entry error.</p>", "proof": "<p>Initialize exact wave-map Cauchy jets from the corrected second form and the approximate lapse/shift. The wave-map algebraic normal solve is invertible on spacelike slices; initial errors have Proposition 14’s residual size and are exactly zero in the protected region.</p><p>Lemma 15.3 gives early support protection. For the error estimate use actual-principal ordinary scalar component stress energies, laboratory mixed commutations, and a small fixed positive mass. Both artificial boundaries are strict outflow. Far out, first and second background derivatives tend uniformly to zero, so the top linear rate is arbitrarily small after a fixed large radius. On the complement it is bounded independently of \\(R,T\\), but support spends at most \\(\\alpha'T+O(1)\\) there.</p><p>Higher background derivatives multiply lower error energies with subexponential coefficients; Proposition 12 handles them with one common exponential rate. Remaining quasilinear placements contain an exponentially small packet or error factor. Choose the far rate and \\(\\alpha'\\) small to obtain \\(e^{\\delta_3T}\\). Strict improvement gives ordinary continuation and exact gauge propagation gives vacuum.</p><p>Transform entry jets to the fixed background wedge coordinates with fixed trace buffers, costing \\(e^{o(T)}\\), and add the linear beam-matching error. The final inequalities in Proposition 16 make the result smaller than \\(e^{-7\\kappa T/2}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Protected correction applied to the seed — original Proposition 9.8.** For any fixed correction order \\(s\\geq3\\), an approximately constrained scaled beam datum with sufficiently small …", "statement": "<p>**Protected correction applied to the seed — original Proposition 9.8.**</p><p>For any fixed correction order \\(s\\geq3\\), an approximately constrained scaled beam datum with sufficiently small\n\\[\n\\|a_B\\|_{H_1^{s+2}}\n+\\|b_B\\|_{H_2^{s+1}}\n+\\|\\phi\\|_{H_4^s}\n\\]\nand compact exterior defect \\(\\phi=\\Phi(d_B)\\) admits a correction supported outside the unit ball, with \\(X^s\\)-norm bounded by \\(C_s\\|\\phi\\|_{H_4^s}\\), smooth symbols at all orders, and no moment restriction.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is Proposition 11 applied to the artificially extended scaled datum. Its single-base-order contraction and group-action regularity give the estimate and all-order smoothness. The artificial interior defect is irrelevant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Scaling, exactness, and data-seminorm estimate — Section 9.6.** Let \\( \\rho_T=(1-2\\alpha)t_T,\\qquad y'=(z-z_*)/\\rho_T. \\) Keep Cartesian metric components and scale second-form components by …", "statement": "<p>**Scaling, exactness, and data-seminorm estimate — Section 9.6.**</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\rho_T=(1-2\\alpha)t_T,\\qquad\ny'=(z-z_*)/\\rho_T.\n\\]\nKeep Cartesian metric components and scale second-form components by\n\\[\n\\widetilde h(y')=h(z_*+\\rho_Ty'),\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde K(y')=\\rho_TK(z_*+\\rho_Ty').\n\\]\nFor \\(s\\geq m+3\\), enlarged for all comparison buffers, the corrected whole-bridge data satisfy\n\\[\np_m(d_T-d)\n\\leq\ne^{o(T)}\n\\left(\nA_T\\lambda_p^{m+5/2}\n+A_T\\lambda_p^{-M_1}\n+A_T^2\\lambda_p^{P_0}\n\\right).\n\\]\nThey are smooth vacuum complete data, equal to \\(d\\) on\n\\(|z-z_*|&lt;\\rho_T\\) and off the selected end.</p>", "proof": "<p>Small fixed drift gives \\(|z|\\asymp\\rho_T|y'|\\) for \\(|y'|\\geq1/2\\). Original symbols imply\n\\[\n|\\partial_{y'}^j(\\widetilde h-\\delta)|\n\\leq C_j\\rho_T^{-1}|y'|^{-1-j},\n\\quad\n|\\partial_{y'}^j\\widetilde K|\n\\leq C_j\\rho_T^{-1}|y'|^{-2-j}.\n\\]\nCut the background perturbations to flat data between radii \\(1/2\\) and \\(3/4\\). The beam lies strictly outside the unit ball, and its scaled size tends to zero once \\(\\zeta\\) is small. Both constraint components scale by \\(\\rho_T^2\\). The defect is smooth compactly supported and has Proposition 14’s residual bound with polynomial rescaling factors absorbed in \\(e^{o(T)}\\).</p><p>Apply Lemma 15.1, undo scaling, retain the original datum inside and on the other end. Smooth support makes the join exact; positivity and asymptotic comparability give completeness.</p><p>Scaled annular Sobolev embedding gives the displayed seminorm estimate. For correction components \\((q_c,p_c)\\), physical components are \\(q_c(y')\\), \\(\\rho_T^{-1}p_c(y')\\). Each physical weighted derivative costs at most \\(C\\rho_T\\) times its scaled bound, a harmless polynomial factor. The second form uses one additional beam derivative, explaining \\(m+5/2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Causal protection despite a noncompact tail — support argument in original Proposition 9.9.** Let \\(s_*=\\sup|C_e'|<1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Causal protection despite a noncompact tail — support argument in original Proposition 9.9.**</p><p>Let \\(s_*=\\sup|C_e'|&lt;1\\). Suppose a causal path from the changed initial support first reaches a fixed large co-radius \\(R\\) at time \\(\\tau\\leq t_T\\). Then\n\\[\n(1-2\\alpha)t_T\n\\leq\n(1+\\delta_R)\\tau+s_*(t_T-\\tau)+O(R),\n\\qquad \\delta_R\\to0,\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\nt_T-\\tau\n\\leq\n\\frac{2\\alpha+\\delta_R}{1+\\delta_R-s_*}\\,t_T+O_R(1).\n\\]\nThus fixed large \\(R\\) and small \\(\\alpha\\) protect every required early entry point, irrespective of the correction’s outer extent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Before first hit, laboratory causal speed is at most \\(1+\\delta_R\\), including the exponentially small comparison bootstrap. The initial point is outside the protected ball about \\(z_*\\), while the hit lies at \\(C_e(\\tau)+O(R)\\). The triangle inequality gives the formula.</p><p>Since \\(I(t_T)=T\\), \\(I'\\leq C_I\\), entry points with \\(v&lt;(1-\\nu_0)T\\) occur by\n\\[\nt_e\\leq t_T-(\\nu_0/C_I)T+O(1).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\alpha'&lt;\\nu_0/(2C_I)\\) and make the first-hit allowance smaller. Ordinary local wave-map uniqueness gives exact early equality. Fixed middle and unselected-end parts lie in unchanged finite-time domains; the deeper face is future outflow, so no unexamined route through the interior or other end contributes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Finite tidal observation in the full corrected development — original Proposition 9.1.** Let \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\), and suppose a seed \\(w\\in T\\Sigma\\) has a noncorner branch as first runout from \\(P_d\\), with …", "statement": "<p>**Finite tidal observation in the full corrected development — original Proposition 9.1.**</p><p>Let \\(d\\in\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\), and suppose a seed \\(w\\in T\\Sigma\\) has a noncorner branch as first runout from \\(P_d\\), with bounded curvature in its parallel positive metric up to runout.</p><p>For every integer \\(m\\geq10\\), \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), and \\(J&gt;0\\), there are smooth complete vacuum data \\(d_T\\in\\mathcal D\\) and seeds \\(w_T\\to w\\), for arbitrarily large finite \\(T\\), such that\n\\[\np_m(d_T-d)\\to0,\n\\]\nand the unit geodesic of \\(w_T\\) in the full maximal development of \\(d_T\\) has a finite point with future unit tangent \\(U_T\\) and unit spacelike \\(X_T\\perp U_T\\) for which\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{Riem}(g_{d_T})(U_T,X_T,U_T,X_T)|&gt;J.\n\\]\nFor large \\(T\\), \\(p_m(d_T-d)&lt;\\eta\\), and the data may be kept in any prescribed relatively open neighborhood of \\(d\\). Convergence is required at the prescribed finite order, not simultaneously in every seminorm with one frequency exponent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(m,\\eta,J\\). If a relatively open neighborhood \\(V\\) is prescribed, choose a finite relative ball\n\\[\np_{m_V}(d'-d)&lt;\\eta_V\n\\]\ninside it, and replace the construction order and tolerance by\n\\[\n\\widehat m=\\max(m,m_V,10),\\qquad\n\\widehat\\eta=\\min(\\eta,\\eta_V).\n\\]</p><p>Make Lemma 16.1’s choices at this enlarged finite order. Proposition 14 gives the constrained exterior approximation; Proposition 15 gives smooth exact whole-bridge vacuum data and exact early agreement with sufficiently accurate entry jets. Proposition 13 gives the deep exact solution, strict tidal observation, and backward entry-state convergence. Lemma 16.2 places the observation and its geodesic in the full maximal development of the complete corrected datum.</p><p>The seminorm estimate and Lemma 16.1 give \\(p_{\\widehat m}(d_T-d)\\to0\\), attaining both the tolerance and prescribed neighborhood. The frequency exponent may depend on this finite order.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Order and margins of parameter choices — Section 9.8.** Fix \\(m\\), correction order \\(s\\geq m+3\\), all deep and exterior comparison orders, and all Sobolev, normal-jet, and trace buffers.", "statement": "<p>**Order and margins of parameter choices — Section 9.8.**</p><p>Fix \\(m\\), correction order \\(s\\geq m+3\\), all deep and exterior comparison orders, and all Sobolev, normal-jet, and trace buffers. Fix \\(N_b\\), then the positive quadratic exponents \\(P_*,P_0\\), independently of later formal accuracy.</p><p>Choose small \\(\\delta_2,\\delta_3&gt;0\\), then deep mass, bulk threshold, and \\(\\nu_0\\), and exterior radius/localization parameters. Choose \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) small enough for all fixed base losses and\n\\[\n\\delta_3+(P_0-4)\\zeta&lt;\\kappa/2,\n\\qquad\n\\delta_2+2\\zeta&lt;\\kappa/2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta_2+(P_*-2)\\zeta&lt;\\kappa,\n\\qquad\n(m+\\tfrac12)\\zeta&lt;\\kappa.\n\\]\nChoose \\(0&lt;b_p&lt;\\zeta/2\\). Only then choose \\(M_1\\) so\n\\[\n(M_1+2)\\zeta&gt;\\tfrac32\\kappa+\\delta_3,\n\\qquad\nM_1\\zeta&gt;\\kappa+\\delta_2,\n\\]\nand choose finite formal orders realizing it. Finally choose \\(T\\) large.</p><p>These choices simultaneously give the exact entry bound, strict deep/exterior improvements, negligible observation errors, and \\(p_m(d_T-d)\\to0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Branch normalization and packet amplitude give the two-sided size\n\\[\nA_T=\\exp((-2\\kappa-2\\zeta)T+o(T)).\n\\]\nExterior linear and quadratic error exponents are\n\\[\n-2\\kappa-(M_1+2)\\zeta+\\delta_3,\n\\qquad\n-4\\kappa+(P_0-4)\\zeta+\\delta_3,\n\\]\nboth strictly below \\(-7\\kappa/2\\).</p><p>After division by \\(A_T\\), the three deep error exponents are\n\\[\n-\\kappa/2+\\delta_2+2\\zeta,\\qquad\n\\kappa+\\delta_2-M_1\\zeta,\\qquad\n-\\kappa+\\delta_2+(P_*-2)\\zeta,\n\\]\nall negative. Assign every subexponential factor a share of the surplus. This gives errors \\(o(Y_T^{-2})\\) at the tidal normalization.</p><p>The direct beam contribution to \\(p_m\\) has exponent\n\\[\n-2\\kappa+(m+\\tfrac12)\\zeta&lt;0;\n\\]\nboth correction contributions also decay. All formal orders are finite and chosen after the fixed positive exponents, so no higher-order smallness or all-seminorm convergence is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Whole corrected bridge attachment — original Proposition 9.10.** For sufficiently large finite \\(T\\), the exact datum \\(d_T\\) has a smooth globally hyperbolic vacuum development containing the entire initial bridge …", "statement": "<p>**Whole corrected bridge attachment — original Proposition 9.10.**</p><p>For sufficiently large finite \\(T\\), the exact datum \\(d_T\\) has a smooth globally hyperbolic vacuum development containing the entire initial bridge, including the full constraint-correction tail, and the finite tidal observation of Proposition 13. Its adaptive backward geodesic reaches \\(\\Sigma\\) with seed \\(w_T\\to w\\). This development embeds openly in the full maximal development of \\(d_T\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The data converge in \\(p_{10}\\), and their protected ball contains a fixed-origin ball of radius\n\\[\n(1-2\\alpha-s_*)t_T-O(1)\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nThus every fixed compact early causal shadow is unchanged.</p><p>Construct the full finite lower bridge development for \\(d_T\\) by Proposition 9’s finite-range argument, retaining every spatial radius and a past collar. The central joins, other end, and bent compact selected slice are unchanged; near infinity the selected horizontal slice carries the actual corrected datum and tail.</p><p>Attach the ordinary exterior comparison across an enlarged band near \\(t_a\\), using wave-map uniqueness and straightening with the same new data. The lower block is untruncated in radius; only the added future block is cut by\n\\[\nf(t_e,y)=|y|-R_T(t_e)&lt;0.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\ndf/dt_e\\geq V_{\\rm out}-V_{\\max}&gt;0,\n\\]\na past curve in the added block keeps \\(f&lt;0\\) until it reaches the unrestricted lower block. It cannot end at an omitted matching corner.</p><p>Attach the deep exact solution across the used spacelike part of \\(S\\) and its collar. Its active future cuts are\n\\[\ns&lt;s_{\\max},\\quad\nu+c_0s/T&lt;u_2,\\quad\nv+c_0s/T&lt;T+c_2.\n\\]\nFor exponentially small relative cone error \\(\\varepsilon_T\\),\n\\[\n\\frac d{ds}(u+c_0s/T),\\\n\\frac d{ds}(v+c_0s/T)\n\\geq c_0/T-C\\varepsilon_T&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe observation has strict terminal and side margins, so past causal curves reach \\(s=0\\) inside the attached interval.</p><p>Omit all unused future faces and edges. Retained neighborhoods are unions and intersections of past-preserved strict sublevels. Common collars give Hausdorff smooth gluing with no duplicate continuations. Every past-inextendible curve reaches the entry slice and then the complete corrected bridge; bounded speeds prevent bounded-time escape to infinity, and the past collar supplies the opposite Cauchy direction. The maximal-development theorem gives the open embedding.</p><p>The background’s earlier compact geodesic tube is unchanged for large \\(T\\). Proposition 13’s converging backward entry states continue through it to seeds \\(w_T\\to w\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 6, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 70, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 16, "cite"], [7, 19, "cite"], [8, 2, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 3, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 4, "cite"], [10, 7, "cite"], [11, 5, "cite"], [11, 7, "cite"], [12, 7, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 9, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [16, 29, "cite"], [16, 30, "cite"], [16, 35, "cite"], [16, 56, "cite"], [17, 16, "part"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 16, "cite"], [20, 16, "cite"], [21, 16, "part"], [22, 16, "part"], [23, 28, "cite"], [24, 16, "part"], [25, 16, "part"], [26, 16, "part"], [27, 16, "part"], [28, 16, "cite"], [29, 56, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 29, "cite"], [32, 29, "part"], [33, 29, "cite"], [34, 29, "cite"], [34, 56, "cite"], [35, 34, "cite"], [36, 34, "cite"], [37, 34, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [37, 40, "cite"], [38, 34, "cite"], [38, 39, "cite"], [39, 34, "cite"], [39, 43, "cite"], [39, 48, "cite"], [40, 34, "cite"], [40, 48, "cite"], [41, 34, "cite"], [42, 47, "cite"], [43, 47, "cite"], [44, 47, "cite"], [45, 47, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [46, 54, "cite"], [47, 34, "cite"], [47, 48, "cite"], [47, 50, "cite"], [47, 53, "cite"], [48, 54, "cite"], [49, 54, "cite"], [50, 54, "cite"], [50, 55, "cite"], [51, 54, "cite"], [52, 54, "cite"], [53, 54, "cite"], [54, 34, "cite"], [55, 34, "cite"], [56, 0, "cite"], [56, 62, "cite"], [57, 56, "cite"], [57, 58, "cite"], [57, 60, "cite"], [58, 56, "cite"], [58, 61, "cite"], [59, 0, "cite"], [59, 68, "cite"], [59, 81, "cite"], [59, 82, "cite"], [59, 86, "cite"], [60, 59, "cite"], [60, 69, "cite"], [61, 59, "cite"], [61, 62, "cite"], [62, 59, "cite"], [62, 71, "cite"], [63, 59, "cite"], [63, 66, "cite"], [63, 72, "cite"], [64, 59, "cite"], [65, 59, "cite"], [65, 66, "cite"], [66, 59, "cite"], [66, 67, "cite"], [66, 75, "cite"], [67, 59, "cite"], [68, 0, "cite"], [68, 96, "cite"], [69, 68, "cite"], [70, 0, "cite"], [71, 73, "cite"], [72, 73, "cite"], [73, 74, "cite"], [74, 70, "cite"], [75, 70, "part"], [76, 91, "cite"], [77, 76, "cite"], [78, 83, "cite"], [78, 90, "cite"], [79, 90, "cite"], [80, 79, "cite"], [80, 84, "cite"], [81, 79, "cite"], [82, 79, "cite"], [83, 79, "cite"], [84, 79, "cite"], [85, 90, "cite"], [85, 92, "cite"], [85, 94, "cite"], [86, 85, "cite"], [86, 87, "cite"], [87, 85, "cite"], [88, 85, "cite"], [89, 85, "cite"], [91, 92, "cite"], [92, 90, "part"], [93, 90, "cite"], [94, 0, "cite"], [94, 79, "cite"], [94, 90, "cite"], [95, 94, "cite"], [96, 94, "cite"]]});
